/* Índice del buscador en inglés (generado por _src-publicaciones/ingles/indice_en.py, no editar a mano). */
window.CATTO_INDICE = (window.CATTO_INDICE || []).concat([{"t":"The modes of a rectangular waveguide","d":"Waveguides and modes","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/guias-onda#modos","x":"02 The modes of a rectangular waveguide Each field configuration that propagates is called a mode. In a rectangular waveguide of sides \\( a \\times b \\) there are transverse electric (TE) modes, with no electric field in the direction of propagation, and transverse magnetic (TM) modes, identified by two indices that count how many half waves fit along each side. Each mode has its cutoff frequency: \\[ f_{c,mn} = \\frac{c}{2} \\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{m}{a}\\right)^2 + \\left(\\frac{n}{b}\\right)^2} \\] Below \\( f_c \\) the mode does not propagate: it is attenuated exponentially. The lowest-cutoff mode, the TE₁₀, has \\( f_c = c / 2a \\): the width of the guide has to be at least half a wavelength. The waveguide is used in the band where only the TE₁₀ propagates: above its cutoff and below the next mode. That way the energy travels in a single known configuration, without being split among modes that travel at different velocities. Standard waveguides have \\( a \\approx 2b \\) so that this band is as wide as possible: Waveguide Inside dimensions (mm) TE₁₀ cutoff Operating band WR-284 72.14 × 34.04 2.08 GHz 2.6 to 3.95 GHz (S band) WR-90 22.86 × 10.16 6.56 GHz 8.2 to 12.4 GHz (X band) WR-28 7.11 × 3.56 21.1 GHz 26.5 to 40 GHz (Ka band)"},{"t":"How the wave travels in the waveguide","d":"Waveguides and modes","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/guias-onda#propagacion","x":"03 How the wave travels in the waveguide Above cutoff, the mode advances with a wavelength longer than that of free space and with two different velocities: \\[ \\lambda_g = \\frac{\\lambda_0}{\\sqrt{1 - (f_c/f)^2}} \\] \\[ v_p = \\frac{c}{\\sqrt{1 - (f_c/f)^2}} \\] \\[ v_g = c\\,\\sqrt{1 - (f_c/f)^2} \\] The phase velocity exceeds the speed of light, but it does not carry information: the energy travels at the group velocity, always less than \\( c \\), and \\( v_p\\, v_g = c^2 \\) holds. Since the group velocity depends on frequency, the waveguide is dispersive: a pulse is distorted as it travels through it, the more so the closer to cutoff you work. That is why the operating band starts well above cutoff. The wave impedance of the TE₁₀ is \\( \\eta_0 / \\sqrt{1 - (f_c/f)^2} \\), greater than that of vacuum. A waveguide below cutoff also has uses: as an attenuator, or like the holes in the door of a microwave oven, which are waveguides too small to let 2.45 GHz out. Lab · which modes propagate The cutoff frequencies of the first modes of three standard waveguides, on a frequency axis. You choose the waveguide and the operating frequency: the modes whose cutoff lies to the left propagate. WR-284 (S band) WR-90 (X band) WR-28 (Ka band) Operating frequency ="},{"t":"Other guided structures","d":"Waveguides and modes","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/guias-onda#otras","x":"04 Other guided structures Structure Dominant mode Limit Circular waveguide TE₁₁, with \\( f_c = 1.841\\, c / 2\\pi a \\) The polarization can rotate; it is used in rotary joints Coaxial cable TEM, with no cutoff frequency Above a certain frequency the first TE mode appears and the cable stops being single-mode Microstrip on a circuit board Quasi-TEM Radiation and surface modes at high frequencies Optical fiber Modes guided by total internal reflection Covered in optical fibers"},{"t":"Where it appears in electronics","d":"Waveguides and modes","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/guias-onda#electronica","x":"05 Where it appears in electronics Case Why a waveguide Airport or ship radar Peak powers of kilowatts in S or X band with low losses Satellite dish feed The feed horn is the end of a waveguide that flares open Microwave oven A short waveguide carries the power from the magnetron to the cavity Point-to-point cellular links Waveguide transitions in the radio units at tens of GHz"},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Waveguides and modes","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/guias-onda#laboratorio","x":"06 In the lab Activity 1 · Measuring the guide wavelength With an X-band waveguide bench, a slotted line and a detector, locate two consecutive minima of the standing wave with the waveguide short-circuited and calculate \\( \\lambda_g \\). Compare it with the formula. Activity 2 · Below cutoff Sweep the generator frequency from 6 to 9 GHz with a section of WR-90 and measure the transmitted power. Locate the cutoff of the TE₁₀. Activity 3 · The oven door Measure the holes in the mesh of a microwave oven door and calculate the cutoff frequency of each hole as a circular waveguide."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Waveguides and modes","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/guias-onda#errores","x":"07 Common mistakes Working too close to cutoff. Dispersion and losses grow a lot. Using the waveguide above the second mode. The energy is split among modes and the signal degrades. Believing that a phase velocity greater than c violates relativity. Information travels at the group velocity. Confusing \\( \\lambda_g \\) with \\( \\lambda_0 \\). Quarter-wave sections in the waveguide are measured with \\( \\lambda_g \\)."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Waveguides and modes","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/guias-onda#autoevaluacion","x":"08 Self-assessment What is the TE₁₀ cutoff of a waveguide 30 mm wide? \\( c / 2a = 3 \\times 10^8 / 0.06 = 5 \\) GHz. What is \\( \\lambda_g \\) in a WR-90 at 10 GHz? \\( 30 / \\sqrt{1 - (6.557/10)^2} \\approx 39.7 \\) mm. Why are standard waveguides twice as wide as they are tall? So that the band where only the TE₁₀ propagates is as wide as possible: with \\( a = 2b \\), the TE₂₀ and the TE₀₁ cut off at the same frequency, twice that of the TE₁₀. What is the relationship between phase velocity and group velocity? Their product is \\( c^2 \\)."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Waveguides and modes","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/guias-onda#ampliar","x":"09 Further reading David M. Pozar. Microwave Engineering. 4th ed., Wiley, 2011. The reference text on microwaves: rectangular and circular waveguides with all their modes. Robert E. Collin. Foundations for Microwave Engineering. 2nd ed., Wiley-IEEE Press, 2001. The classic, rigorous treatment of guided structures. David K. Cheng. Field and Wave Electromagnetics. 2nd ed., Addison-Wesley, 1989. Waveguides derived from Maxwell's equations, with the continuity of this course."},{"t":"Two complementary disciplines","d":"Industrial hygiene and safety","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/higiene-seguridad-industrial#campo","x":"01 Two complementary disciplines Safety at work deals with accidents: sudden events that cause injury, such as a fall, being caught in machinery or an electric shock. Hygiene deals with occupational diseases: harm that sets in slowly through repeated exposure to an agent, such as deafness from noise or lead poisoning from soldering. Both share the goal of anticipating, recognizing, evaluating and controlling risks before they cause harm. The Italian physician Bernardino Ramazzini published in 1700 the first systematic treatise on the diseases of trades, and proposed asking every patient what work they did. In Argentina, the legal basis is Law 19,587 on Hygiene and Safety at Work and its regulatory decree 351/79, which are covered in the legal framework unit. The presentation for technicians is in health and safety, in the Electronics Technician program."},{"t":"Risk agents","d":"Industrial hygiene and safety","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/higiene-seguridad-industrial#agentes","x":"02 Risk agents Type Examples In electronics Physical Noise, vibration, lighting, heat load, radiation Radio frequency in antenna tests, lasers, inspection X-rays Chemical Gases, vapors, fumes, dusts, liquids Solder flux fumes, lead, cleaning solvents, etching acids Biological Bacteria, viruses, fungi Equipment maintenance in hospitals Ergonomic Postures, repetitive movements, loads Manual assembly, prolonged work under a microscope Psychosocial Mental load, shifts, deadline pressure On-call duty for critical systems For each agent, hygiene defines a measurable quantity and a reference value: the concentration in the air for a chemical contaminant, the sound level for noise, the illuminance for light. The evaluation compares the measured exposure with that value."},{"t":"Noise: level, dose and equivalent level","d":"Industrial hygiene and safety","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/higiene-seguridad-industrial#ruido","x":"03 Noise: level, dose and equivalent level Noise is the most widespread physical agent in industry, and the deafness it causes is irreversible. The sound level is measured with A-weighting, in dBA, which imitates the sensitivity of the ear. SRT (Superintendency of Occupational Risks) Resolution 295/03 adopted as its criterion 85 dBA for 8 hours, with a 3 dB exchange rate: for every 3 dB more, which is twice the energy, the permitted time is cut in half: \\[ T(L) = \\frac{8\\ \\mathrm{h}}{2^{(L - 85)/3}} \\] \\[ D = \\sum_i \\frac{C_i}{T(L_i)} \\] \\[ L_{eq,8h} = 85 + 10 \\log_{10} D \\] \\( C_i \\) is the time spent at level \\( L_i \\). If the dose \\( D \\) exceeds 1, or the 8-hour equivalent level exceeds 85 dBA, the exposure is excessive. The measurement procedure is in SRT Resolution 85/12; always check the current version of the rules in the Boletín Oficial (Argentina’s Official Gazette). Lab · the noise dose of a working day A working day with three tasks at different sound levels. Each bar is the fraction of the daily dose contributed by one task; the red line is 100%. If the hours add up to more than 8, the last task is adjusted. Task 1: level = Task 1: duration = Task 2: level = Task 2: duration = Task 3: level ="},{"t":"The hierarchy of controls","d":"Industrial hygiene and safety","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/higiene-seguridad-industrial#control","x":"04 The hierarchy of controls Once a risk has been evaluated, the measures are chosen in order of decreasing effectiveness. The first ones act on the hazard; the last ones depend on each person doing the right thing every time: Level Measure Example with noise 1 Elimination Replace a riveting process with a bonding one 2 Substitution A compressor with a lower sound level 3 Engineering controls Acoustic enclosure, anti-vibration mounts 4 Administrative controls Task rotation to lower the dose, signage 5 Personal protective equipment Hearing protectors Personal protective equipment is the last resort, not the first: it protects only if it is worn properly and all the time, and it does not reduce the risk for anyone else."},{"t":"In the laboratory","d":"Industrial hygiene and safety","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/higiene-seguridad-industrial#laboratorio","x":"05 In the laboratory Activity 1 · Noise map Using a sound level meter, measure the sound level at different stations in a workshop and draw up a plan with the values. Estimate the dose received by a person according to their route through the day. Activity 2 · Soldering fumes Compare the exposure to flux fumes at a soldering station with and without local extraction, observing the plume with a lamp placed at the side. Activity 3 · Survey of agents Walk through an electronics laboratory and list the risk agents at each station, with the control measure that applies according to the hierarchy."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Industrial hygiene and safety","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/higiene-seguridad-industrial#errores","x":"06 Common mistakes Adding decibels as plain numbers. Two 85 dBA sources give 88 dBA, not 170. Believing that a slightly higher level is only slightly riskier. With a 3 dB exchange rate, 91 dBA allows only 2 hours. Starting with hearing protectors. They are the last step of the hierarchy of controls. Confusing an accident with an occupational disease. The disease appears years later and is harder to attribute."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Industrial hygiene and safety","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/higiene-seguridad-industrial#autoevaluacion","x":"07 Self-assessment How much time per day is allowed at 94 dBA? 1 hour: 9 dB above 85 is three halvings. A person spends 4 hours at 88 dBA and 4 hours at 80 dBA. What dose do they receive? \\( 4/4 + 4/25.4 \\approx 1.16 \\): 116%, excessive. What 8-hour equivalent level corresponds to a dose of 200%? \\( 85 + 10\\log_{10} 2 = 88 \\) dBA. Why are soldering fumes a chemical agent and not a physical one? Because the harm is caused by the substances that are inhaled: the flux compounds and the metals."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Industrial hygiene and safety","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/higiene-seguridad-industrial#ampliar","x":"08 Further reading International Labour Organization. Encyclopaedia of Occupational Health and Safety. Spanish edition: “Enciclopedia de salud y seguridad en el trabajo”, Ministerio de Trabajo y Asuntos Sociales de España, 2001. The reference work, with chapters by risk agent and by industry. Barry S. Levy, David H. Wegman, Sherry L. Baron and Rosemary K. Sokas (eds.). Occupational and Environmental Health. 7th ed., Oxford University Press, 2017. Occupational hygiene from the public health perspective, with exposure assessment. Superintendency of Occupational Risks (SRT). Resolutions 295/2003 and 85/2012. Boletín Oficial de la República Argentina (in Spanish). The reference values for physical and chemical agents, and the noise measurement protocol."},{"t":"The precursors","d":"Origins and evolution of industrial organization","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/historia-organizacion#precursores","x":"01 The precursors Industrial organization was born with the factory. In The Wealth of Nations (1776), Adam Smith described how, in a pin factory, dividing the work into simple operations multiplied output: each worker gained dexterity, did not lose time switching tasks and could use specialized tools. In 1832, Charles Babbage added that the division made it possible to pay for each task according to the skill it demanded. The Industrial Revolution —the steam engine, textile mechanization, interchangeable parts— put those ideas into practice on a large scale."},{"t":"Scientific management and general management","d":"Origins and evolution of industrial organization","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/historia-organizacion#cientifica","x":"02 Scientific management and general management Author Contribution Frederick W. Taylor The Principles of Scientific Management (1911): study each task with a stopwatch, set the best method and a standard time, select and train the workers, pay for performance Frank and Lillian Gilbreth Motion study: break work down into elementary motions and eliminate the unnecessary ones Henry L. Gantt The bar chart for planning and controlling production Henri Fayol General and Industrial Management (1916): the functions of forecasting, organizing, commanding, coordinating and controlling, and principles such as unity of command Henry Ford The moving assembly line (1913): the work goes to the worker; mass production of a standardized product The studies at the Hawthorne plant of Western Electric, between 1924 and 1932, in which Elton Mayo took part, showed that productivity also depended on social factors and on the attention that work groups received: the human relations school was born."},{"t":"From statistical quality to the Toyota system","d":"Origins and evolution of industrial organization","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/historia-organizacion#calidad-y-flujo","x":"03 From statistical quality to the Toyota system Walter Shewhart, at Bell Laboratories, invented the control chart in the 1920s; W. Edwards Deming and Joseph Juran brought statistical control and quality management to postwar Japanese industry. At Toyota, Taiichi Ohno developed a production system aimed at eliminating waste: making just in time what the next process asks for, with kanban cards, and stopping the line when a defect appears. In the West it was spread from the late 1980s as lean production. Today Industry 4.0 adds sensors, connectivity, data and flexible automation to those ideas."},{"t":"The learning curve","d":"Origins and evolution of industrial organization","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/historia-organizacion#aprendizaje","x":"04 The learning curve In 1936, T. P. Wright published a study on the cost of building aircraft: every time the cumulative quantity doubled, the labor hours per unit were multiplied by a constant factor, the learning rate \\( r \\). The time of unit \\( n \\) is: \\[ T_n = T_1\\, n^{b}, \\quad b = \\log_2 r \\] With \\( r \\) = 80 %, \\( b \\approx -0.32 \\): unit 2 takes 80 % of the first, unit 4 takes 64 %, unit 8 takes 51 %. The curve is used to budget production runs, set prices and negotiate contracts. Typical values range from 70 % in complex manual tasks to 95 % in highly automated processes. Lab · Wright’s learning curve The time of each unit (blue) and the cumulative average time (yellow) for a run of N units, on a logarithmic scale, where the curve becomes a straight line. Time of the first unit T 1 = Learning rate r = Number of units N ="},{"t":"In the laboratory","d":"Origins and evolution of industrial organization","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/historia-organizacion#laboratorio","x":"05 In the laboratory Activity 1 · Your own learning Assemble the same simple circuit ten times on a breadboard, time each assembly and fit the learning rate. Activity 2 · Timeline Place the contributions on this page on a timeline and relate them to the technological changes of each era. Activity 3 · Waste Walk through the university laboratory or a workshop and identify examples of the Toyota system wastes: waiting, motion, transportation, inventory, defects."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Origins and evolution of industrial organization","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/historia-organizacion#errores","x":"06 Common mistakes Reducing Taylor to the stopwatch. His proposal included methods, selection, training and incentives. Believing that lean production is just keeping little inventory. It is a complete system of flow, quality and continuous improvement. Applying the learning curve without limit. At some point the time settles at the one set by the machine or the process. Confusing the learning rate with the reduction per unit. It is the reduction each time the quantity doubles."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Origins and evolution of industrial organization","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/historia-organizacion#autoevaluacion","x":"07 Self-assessment With T 1 = 100 h and r = 80 %, how long does unit 8 take? \\( 100 \\cdot 0.8^3 = 51.2 \\) h. And unit 100? About 22.7 h. What management functions did Fayol define? Forecasting, organizing, commanding, coordinating and controlling. What is a kanban? A card or signal with which the next process asks the previous one to produce or deliver parts: production is pulled by demand."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Origins and evolution of industrial organization","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/historia-organizacion#ampliar","x":"08 Further reading Idalberto Chiavenato. Introducción a la teoría general de la administración. McGraw-Hill. (In Spanish.) The schools of management, from Taylor and Fayol to the present ones. Taiichi Ohno. Toyota Production System: Beyond Large-Scale Production. Productivity Press, 1988. (Spanish edition: “El sistema de producción Toyota: más allá de la producción a gran escala”, Gestión 2000.) The system told by the person who created it. James P. Womack, Daniel T. Jones and Daniel Roos. The Machine That Changed the World. Rawson Associates, 1990. (Spanish edition: “La máquina que cambió el mundo”, McGraw-Hill.) The study that spread lean production in the West."},{"t":"No artifact is socially neutral","d":"The social dimension and impact of engineering","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/impacto-social#no-neutral","x":"01 No artifact is socially neutral A comfortable idea says that technology is a neutral tool and that everything depends on how it is used. It is partly true and, taken literally, false: design decisions distribute possibilities. A device that can only be repaired at the official service center, a platform that decides which content is seen first, or a machine that requires one operator fewer are already distributing power, work and money before anyone uses it “well” or “badly.” Langdon Winner raised it in 1980 with a question that stuck: Do artifacts have politics? His answer is yes, in two ways: some are deliberately designed to produce a social effect, and others demand, by their very nature, a certain form of organization —a nuclear power plant requires a control and security structure that a solar panel on a roof does not need—. Three positions to discuss Technological determinism: technology advances on its own and society adapts. It explains little and removes responsibility. Instrumentalism: it is just a neutral tool. It ignores that the design already contains decisions. Social construction of technology: the social groups involved —users, manufacturers, the State, workers— shape which technology is developed and which falls by the wayside. It is the most useful for thinking about the engineer’s role, because it gives back the capacity to decide."},{"t":"First-, second- and third-order effects","d":"The social dimension and impact of engineering","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/impacto-social#ordenes","x":"02 First-, second- and third-order effects Almost no important technology produces only the effect it was designed for. It is best analyzed in layers: First order What the technology does directly, and which was in the design goal. Second order What changes in practices and in organization once use becomes widespread. Third order Deep social transformations, which usually appear years later and which nobody was seeking. Lab · consequence tree Choose a technology and open the layers one at a time. Before opening the next one, it pays to venture aloud what might be there. The cell phone Industrial automation LED lighting GPS Electronic voting Open the next layer Reset"},{"t":"Assess the impact beforehand, not afterwards","d":"The social dimension and impact of engineering","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/impacto-social#evaluacion","x":"03 Assess the impact beforehand, not afterwards There is a discipline for this, technology assessment, and a handful of questions that any project should be able to answer: Question Why it matters Who wins and who loses? The benefits and the costs rarely fall on the same people Who decides? If those affected do not take part, the rejection comes later and costs more What happens if it fails? It is not the same for a toy to fail as for a ventilator or a traffic light Is it reversible? The less reversible, the more caution it demands: this is the precautionary principle What alternatives were discarded? Every technical choice closes off paths; it pays to know which ones Who is left out? Accessibility, cost, connectivity, language: a solution that excludes 20% is not finished Risk is not only probability In engineering, risk is calculated as probability times consequence, but socially what also weighs is who bears it, whether it is voluntary and whether it is reversible. A small risk imposed on others is tolerated far less than a larger one taken on by choice. Ignoring that difference is the cause of many conflicts between technically correct projects and the communities where they are installed."},{"t":"Cases where the technical and the social cannot be separated","d":"The social dimension and impact of engineering","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/impacto-social#casos","x":"04 Cases where the technical and the social cannot be separated Electronic voting. An apparently technical problem —counting votes faster— that involves secrecy, verifiability, auditing and public trust. The Argentine debate of recent years showed that the central objections were not about speed but about whether an ordinary citizen can verify the result without trusting an expert. It is a textbook case: the technical answer depends on which values are chosen as priorities. Free-to-air digital television. Choosing a standard —European, Japanese-Brazilian or American— was not just a modulation decision: it defined the local industry, patents, the cost of receivers and content policy. Automation and employment. Every job that a system automates reorganizes work. The engineer’s question is not only whether it can be automated, but which tasks are redistributed and what training is needed. Accessibility. A screen reader, a digital hearing aid or a communication device for people without speech expand rights. And conversely: a touch-only interface leaves out anyone who cannot see. Universal design proposes thinking from the start about the diversity of users, instead of adapting afterwards. Unequal connectivity. A public service that goes digital without considering where there is no coverage or devices turns an advance into a barrier."},{"t":"The responsibility of the designer","d":"The social dimension and impact of engineering","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/impacto-social#responsabilidad","x":"05 The responsibility of the designer Something practical follows from all of the above: the person who designs is not a neutral executor. They choose materials, margins, safety levels, what gets documented and what does not, what can be repaired, what data is collected and who can access it. Each of those decisions has consequences for third parties who will never read the schematic. That does not mean the engineer decides alone or carries the whole weight: it means their technical opinion is part of the social decision and that staying silent is also a decision. The professional ethics developed in the profession in Argentina and professional ethics puts that in writing."},{"t":"Activities","d":"The social dimension and impact of engineering","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/impacto-social#laboratorio","x":"06 Activities Activity 1 · Impact analysis of your own project Take a project from your degree program and answer the six questions of section 3 in writing. Identify at least one second-order effect that was not in the initial goal. Activity 2 · A technology that did not thrive Research a case of a technically viable technology that did not take hold —Betamax, the magneto-optical disc, 3D TV— and reconstruct which social and economic groups tipped the balance. It helps show that “the best one” does not win on its own. Activity 3 · Accessible design Redesign the interface of a laboratory device so that it can be used by a blind person or by someone with reduced mobility in their hands. List which hardware and firmware changes would be needed and how much they would cost."},{"t":"Ideas worth revisiting","d":"The social dimension and impact of engineering","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/impacto-social#errores","x":"07 Ideas worth revisiting “Technology is neutral, it depends on the use.” The design already contains decisions that distribute costs and benefits. “Technical progress is inevitable.” Many viable technologies were abandoned and others prevailed for economic or political reasons, not because they were better. Believing that social effects are “a topic for another course.” They show up in concrete design decisions. Confusing acceptance with participation: announcing a decision already made is not consulting. Thinking of accessibility as an add-on at the end, when it is much cheaper if it is considered from the start."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"The social dimension and impact of engineering","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/impacto-social#autoevaluacion","x":"08 Self-assessment What does Winner maintain when he says that artifacts have politics? That some are designed to produce specific social effects and that others, because of their scale or their risk, require certain forms of organization and control. Give an example of a second-order effect of the cell phone. For example, the disappearance of the habit of agreeing in advance on a place and a time to meet: coordination came to happen on the fly. Why is an imposed risk resisted more than a voluntary one? Because the social perception of risk includes who decides and who bears it, not only the probability and the magnitude of the harm. What is the precautionary principle? That in the face of potentially serious or irreversible harm, the lack of full scientific certainty must not be used as an excuse for not taking action. What does universal design mean? Designing from the outset so that the product can be used by the widest possible diversity of people, without the need for later adaptations."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"The social dimension and impact of engineering","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/impacto-social#ampliar","x":"09 Further reading Langdon Winner. The Whale and the Reactor: A Search for Limits in an Age of High Technology. University of Chicago Press, 1986 (Spanish edition: “La ballena y el reactor,” Gedisa). It includes the essay “Do Artifacts Have Politics?”, the text that gave order to this discussion. Hernán Thomas and Alfonso Buch (eds.). Actos, actores y artefactos. Sociología de la tecnología. Universidad Nacional de Quilmes, 2008 (in Spanish). An Argentine textbook of social studies of technology, with local cases. Wiebe Bijker and Trevor Pinch. The Social Construction of Facts and Artifacts. Social Studies of Science, 1984 (Spanish translation in Actos, actores y artefactos, UNQ). The founding text of the SCOT approach (social construction of technology), with the case of the bicycle. Ruth Schwartz Cowan. More Work for Mother. Basic Books, 1983. How household appliances, which promised to free up time, ended up redefining domestic work. A classic on unintended consequences."},{"t":"Three pieces and nothing else","d":"System elements and implementation","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/implementacion-sistemas#tres","x":"01 Three pieces and nothing else Any LTI system, however complicated, is built from three elements. There is no fourth: Element What it does In continuous time In discrete time Adder \\( y = x_1 + x_2 \\) A node, or a summing amplifier An addition Gain \\( y = a\\,x \\) A resistive divider, a stage A multiplication Memory Stores the past An integrator: capacitor or inductor A delay \\( z^{-1} \\): a register The memory row is the important one. Without it, a system can only respond to the present value —it would be static— and there would be neither filters nor dynamics. In an analog circuit that memory is the energy stored in a capacitor; in a processor, a memory location that holds the previous sample. \\[ y[n] = \\sum_{k=0}^{M} b_k\\,x[n-k] \\;-\\; \\sum_{k=1}^{N} a_k\\,y[n-k] \\] Each \\( x[n-k] \\) and each \\( y[n-k] \\) is a delay; each \\( b_k \\) and each \\( a_k \\), a gain; and the summation signs, adders. The equation is the diagram."},{"t":"From the diagram to the filter","d":"System elements and implementation","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/implementacion-sistemas#lab","x":"02 From the diagram to the filter The same transfer function can be built in several ways, and they are not equivalent in the number of components or in numerical behavior. The lab draws the structure, counts the pieces and shows what filter results. Lab · the block diagram, piece by piece Choose the structure and look at the diagram: the squares are delays, the triangles gains and the circles adders. Below, the resulting impulse response and magnitude. direct form II · the most widely used direct form I FIR · zeros only low-pass high-pass band-pass high-Q resonator Frequency = 0.15 · fs"},{"t":"The structures, and why there is more than one","d":"System elements and implementation","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/implementacion-sistemas#estructuras","x":"03 The structures, and why there is more than one Structure Delays Advantage Problem Direct form I \\( M + N \\) Comes straight from the equation Uses twice the memory Direct form II \\( \\max(M,N) \\) The minimum number of delays Sensitive to quantization if the order is high Cascade Same as II Robust: each section has its own poles You have to factor and order the sections Parallel Same Errors do not accumulate between branches Requires partial fractions Direct form II is also called canonical because it uses the smallest possible number of memory elements, and it is the one implemented by almost all libraries. Its problem appears at high orders. Why cascade is used in practice The coefficients of a real filter are stored with a finite number of bits. In direct form, the denominator coefficients are those of the complete polynomial, and rounding them moves all the roots at once: in an eighth-order filter, a tiny error in one coefficient can push a pole outside the unit circle and make it unstable. In cascade, on the other hand, the filter is built from second-order sections, each with its two poles. Rounding the coefficients of one section moves only those two, and only slightly. That is why no high-order digital filter is implemented in direct form: it is factored into second-order sections, which in the jargon are called biquads."},{"t":"FIR and IIR","d":"System elements and implementation","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/implementacion-sistemas#firiir","x":"04 FIR and IIR FIR IIR Feedback None: \\( a_k = 0 \\) Yes Impulse response Finite: it dies out on its own Infinite Stability Always stable The poles must be checked Linear phase Obtained with symmetric coefficients No, unless an all-pass is added Order for the same selectivity High: tens or hundreds Low: just a few Analog equivalent None Resembles an RLC The trade-off is straightforward: an FIR is safer and can have linear phase, but it needs many more coefficients; an IIR achieves the same with a low order, in exchange for keeping an eye on stability and for not being able to have linear phase. For real-time audio, where latency matters, the IIR usually wins; for block processing where phase matters, the FIR. Memory costs too A 200-coefficient FIR needs to store 200 past samples and perform 200 multiplications for each output sample. A fourth-order IIR that does something similar needs 4. On a small microcontroller that difference decides whether the filter fits or not, and it is the reason why the filters in embedded instrumentation are almost always cascaded biquads."},{"t":"In the lab","d":"System elements and implementation","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/implementacion-sistemas#laboratorio","x":"05 In the lab Activity 1 · Draw and count For \\( H(z) = \\dfrac{1 + 2z^{-1} + z^{-2}}{1 - 0.5z^{-1} + 0.3z^{-2}} \\), draw the direct forms I and II, and count the delays, multipliers and adders of each. Activity 2 · Programming a biquad Program a second-order section in direct form II on a microcontroller, measure how many cycles it takes per sample and calculate the maximum sampling frequency it supports. Activity 3 · Breaking a filter with quantization Design an 8th-order IIR low-pass, round its coefficients to 8 bits in direct form and calculate where the poles end up. Repeat by factoring into four biquads and compare how much they moved in each case."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"System elements and implementation","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/implementacion-sistemas#errores","x":"06 Common mistakes Implementing a high order in direct form. Coefficient quantization can make it unstable. Forgetting the sign of the feedback. The \\( a_k \\) enter subtracted in the difference equation. Confusing order with number of coefficients. An FIR of order \\( M \\) has \\( M+1 \\) coefficients. Expecting linear phase from an IIR. It does not have it, unless an all-pass is added to correct it. Not initializing the delay registers. At startup, a filter with garbage inside delivers a transient that can last a long time."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"System elements and implementation","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/implementacion-sistemas#autoevaluacion","x":"07 Self-assessment What are the three elements of an LTI system? Adder, gain and memory: an integrator in continuous time, a delay in discrete time. Why is direct form II called canonical? Because it uses the smallest possible number of memory elements: \\( \\max(M,N) \\). Why are high-order filters implemented in cascade? Because rounding the coefficients in direct form moves all the roots at once; in cascade each section has its own poles and the error stays bounded. What is a biquad? A second-order section. High-order filters are built by chaining several of them. Why is an FIR always stable? Because it has no feedback: its impulse response has a finite number of terms. When is an IIR the right choice? When selectivity is needed with few resources and phase is not critical. It achieves with order 4 what an FIR would need tens of coefficients for."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"System elements and implementation","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/implementacion-sistemas#ampliar","x":"08 Further reading John G. Proakis and Dimitris G. Manolakis. Digital Signal Processing: Principles, Algorithms, and Applications. 4th ed. (Spanish edition, “Tratamiento digital de señales”), Prentice Hall, 2006. All the structures with their diagrams, and the analysis of finite-precision arithmetic effects. Alan V. Oppenheim and Ronald W. Schafer. Discrete-Time Signal Processing. 3rd ed. (Spanish edition, “Tratamiento de señales en tiempo discreto”), Prentice Hall, 2010. The chapter on structures and the one on coefficient quantization effects. Richard G. Lyons. Understanding Digital Signal Processing. 3rd ed., Prentice Hall, 2010. The clearest one for real implementation: biquads, scaling and rounding errors."},{"t":"The step, which holds no mystery","d":"Unit impulse and unit step","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/impulso-escalon#escalon","x":"01 The step, which holds no mystery Let us start with the easy one. The unit step is zero before and one after: \\[ u(t) = \\begin{cases} 1 & t \\gt 0 \\\\ 0 & t \\lt 0 \\end{cases} \\qquad\\qquad u[n] = \\begin{cases} 1 & n \\ge 0 \\\\ 0 & n \\lt 0 \\end{cases} \\] In continuous time the value at \\( t = 0 \\) is left undefined, or taken as \\( 1/2 \\) for convenience. In discrete time there is no ambiguity: \\( u[0] = 1 \\). Its main use is to cut off signals. Multiplying by \\( u(t) \\) turns everything earlier into zero, and subtracting two shifted steps builds a pulse: \\( \\operatorname{rect} = u(t) - u(t - T) \\). It is the standard way to write piecewise-defined signals on a single line."},{"t":"The impulse, which does","d":"Unit impulse and unit step","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/impulso-escalon#impulso","x":"02 The impulse, which does In discrete time it is simple too: \\( \\delta[n] \\) equals 1 at \\( n = 0 \\) and zero elsewhere. A signal like any other. In continuous time, on the other hand, \\( \\delta(t) \\) is not a function. There is no function that is zero everywhere except at one point and has integral one: changing the value at a single point does not change any integral. What does exist is a limit, and more precisely a distribution: an object that is defined by how it acts inside an integral. \\[ \\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} x(t)\\,\\delta(t - t_0)\\,dt = x(t_0) \\] The sifting, or sampling, property. It is the operational definition of the impulse: the only thing you need to know about it. Any family of pulses that narrow while keeping area one gets to the same place: a rectangle, a triangle, a Gaussian, a sinc. The shape does not matter. What matters is that the area stays at one and the width tends to zero. Lab · the impulse as a limit A pulse of area one that keeps narrowing. Above, the pulse next to a smooth signal; below, the result of \\( \\int x(t)\\,p_\\Delta(t - t_0)\\,dt \\), which has to approach \\( x(t_0) \\) regardless of the shape of the pulse. Rectangular pulse Triangular pulse Gaussian Sinc Two-sided exponential Width Δ = 1.000 Position t₀ = 1.5"},{"t":"The properties that get used","d":"Unit impulse and unit step","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/impulso-escalon#propiedades","x":"03 The properties that get used Property Expression What it is for Sifting \\( \\int x(t)\\delta(t-t_0)dt = x(t_0) \\) Extracting a point value Product \\( x(t)\\,\\delta(t-t_0) = x(t_0)\\,\\delta(t-t_0) \\) Simplifying before integrating Area \\( \\int \\delta(t)\\,dt = 1 \\) It is what defines it Scaling \\( \\delta(at) = \\dfrac{1}{|a|}\\,\\delta(t) \\) Changes of variable Parity \\( \\delta(-t) = \\delta(t) \\) It is even Convolution \\( x(t) * \\delta(t - t_0) = x(t - t_0) \\) Convolving with an impulse shifts The scaling one is surprising: narrowing the impulse makes it taller, because the area has to keep equaling one. And the last row is the one that explains why \\( \\delta(t) \\) is the identity element of convolution: convolving with it changes nothing, and with a shifted version, it only shifts. The units of the impulse Since \\( \\int \\delta(t)dt = 1 \\) and \\( dt \\) is measured in seconds, \\( \\delta(t) \\) has units of 1/second. It is not dimensionless. That is why the impulse response of a circuit is measured in “volts per volt-second”, and why in practice it is not excited with an impulse but with a step: a pulse of area one and 1 μs width would be a million volts tall."},{"t":"One is the derivative of the other","d":"Unit impulse and unit step","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/impulso-escalon#relacion","x":"04 One is the derivative of the other The relationship between the two is the one that organizes everything: \\[ \\delta(t) = \\frac{du(t)}{dt}, \\qquad\\qquad u(t) = \\int_{-\\infty}^{t} \\delta(\\tau)\\,d\\tau \\] The derivative of a step is zero everywhere except at the jump, where it is infinite: that is exactly an impulse. And in discrete time the relationship is the same with differences and sums: \\[ \\delta[n] = u[n] - u[n-1], \\qquad\\qquad u[n] = \\sum_{k=-\\infty}^{n} \\delta[k] \\] From this follows something very practical. Since an LTI system commutes with the derivative, the impulse response is the derivative of the step response. And that solves a real lab problem: an impulse cannot be generated, but a step is generated with a switch. The step response is measured and then differentiated. How long a pulse can last and still count as an impulse It depends on the system, not on the pulse. What is required is that the pulse be much narrower than the fastest time constant of the system: as a rule, one tenth. For a circuit with \\( \\tau = 1 \\) ms, a 100 μs pulse is enough, and the system cannot tell that input from a true impulse. That is why in practice the impulse is approximated without drama, as long as it is known beforehand how fast the system is."},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Unit impulse and unit step","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/impulso-escalon#laboratorio","x":"05 In the lab Exercise 1 · Sifting by hand Compute \\( \\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} (t^2 + 3)\\,\\delta(t - 2)\\,dt \\), \\( \\int_0^{5} e^{-t}\\delta(t-3)dt \\) and \\( \\int_0^{5} e^{-t}\\delta(t-7)dt \\). Explain why the last one gives zero. Exercise 2 · h(t) without generating an impulse Measure the step response of an RC with the oscilloscope, export the data and differentiate it numerically. Compare the result with the theoretical expression \\( h(t) = \\frac{1}{RC}e^{-t/RC}u(t) \\). Exercise 3 · When a pulse is an impulse Excite the same RC with pulses of equal area and widths of \\( \\tau \\), \\( \\tau/10 \\) and \\( \\tau/100 \\). Check that from \\( \\tau/10 \\) on the response no longer changes shape: the system cannot tell those pulses from an impulse."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Unit impulse and unit step","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/impulso-escalon#errores","x":"06 Common mistakes Treating \\( \\delta(t) \\) as a function. It only makes sense inside an integral. Its “value” at zero is not a number. Forgetting the \\( 1/|a| \\) in \\( \\delta(at) \\). Narrowing raises the height. Sifting with an impulse whose argument falls outside the interval. If \\( t_0 \\) is not in the range, the result is zero. Believing that \\( \\delta[n] \\) is also a distribution. In discrete time it is an ordinary, everyday signal. Thinking the impulse is dimensionless. It has units of 1/second."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Unit impulse and unit step","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/impulso-escalon#autoevaluacion","x":"07 Self-assessment What is \\( \\int (t^2+3)\\,\\delta(t-2)\\,dt \\)? 7: it is the value of \\( t^2 + 3 \\) evaluated at \\( t = 2 \\). Why is \\( \\delta(t) \\) not a function? Because no function that is zero except at one point can have integral one. It is a distribution, defined by how it acts inside an integral. What is \\( \\delta(3t) \\)? \\( \\frac{1}{3}\\delta(t) \\). The factor \\( 1/|a| \\) preserves the area. What is the relationship between \\( h(t) \\) and the step response? \\( h(t) \\) is its derivative. That is why in the lab the step is measured and then differentiated. What does \\( x(t) * \\delta(t - 3) \\) give? \\( x(t - 3) \\): convolving with a shifted impulse shifts the signal. How wide can a pulse be and still pass for an impulse? Much less than the fastest time constant of the system; as a rule, one tenth."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Unit impulse and unit step","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/impulso-escalon#ampliar","x":"08 Further reading Alan V. Oppenheim and Alan S. Willsky. Signals and Systems. 2nd ed. (Spanish edition, “Señales y sistemas”), Prentice Hall, 1998. The impulse defined as a limit of pulses and its properties, with the care the subject deserves. Ronald N. Bracewell. The Fourier Transform and Its Applications. 3rd ed., McGraw-Hill, 1999. The best treatment of the impulse and of distributions for engineering ever written. Simon Haykin and Barry Van Veen. Signals and Systems. (Spanish edition, “Señales y sistemas”), Limusa Wiley, 2001. The relationship between impulse response and step response, with measurement examples."},{"t":"Faraday’s law","d":"Magnetic induction","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/induccion-magnetica#faraday","x":"01 Faraday’s law A magnetic field that changes in time creates an electric field. That is the discovery that joins electricity and magnetism into a single subject instead of two separate ones, and it is what makes it possible to generate electric power at scale. \\[ \\varepsilon = -\\frac{d\\Phi_B}{dt} \\qquad\\qquad \\Phi_B = \\int \\vec{B}\\cdot d\\vec{A} \\] The third Maxwell equation. What induces an EMF is not the field but its change: a stationary loop in a constant field, however strong, generates nothing. The flux can change in three ways, and all three are used: by varying the field —the transformer—, by varying the area —a circuit that deforms— or by varying the angle between the two, which is the generator of every power plant. The minus sign is Lenz’s law The induced current flows in the direction that opposes the change that produced it. It is not a matter of sign convention: it is the conservation of energy. If it were the other way around, the change would reinforce itself and we would have free energy. That is why it takes effort to move a magnet inside a short-circuited coil, and that mechanical work is exactly the electrical energy that appears."},{"t":"Self-inductance","d":"Magnetic induction","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/induccion-magnetica#autoinduccion","x":"02 Self-inductance A coil produces flux with its own current, so when that current changes, an EMF is induced in the coil itself. The constant of proportionality is the inductance: \\[ v = L\\,\\frac{di}{dt} \\qquad\\qquad L = \\frac{\\mu_0\\mu_r N^2 A}{\\ell} \\qquad\\qquad U = \\tfrac{1}{2}LI^2 \\] For a solenoid. Note the \\( N^2 \\): doubling the turns quadruples the inductance, because each turn produces twice the flux and there are also twice as many turns to capture it. The first expression has a brutal consequence. If the current is cut off suddenly, \\( di/dt \\) becomes enormous and so does the induced voltage. A coil does not allow its current to change instantaneously: if you insist, the coil generates whatever voltage it takes —thousands of volts— until something breaks or an arc jumps. Lab · the turn-off spike of a coil An RL circuit whose current is cut off. You vary the inductance and the turn-off time, and the voltage spike that the coil generates appears. Then try the flyback diode and see what changes. Inductance = 10 mH Current before opening = 1 A Turn-off time = 1 µs With a flyback diode in parallel The flyback diode is not optional Any inductive load switched by a transistor —a relay, a motor, a valve, a solenoid— needs a path for the current to keep flowing when the transistor opens. That path is the flyback diode in antiparallel with the coil: the current closes through it and decays smoothly, and the voltage across the transistor is limited to the supply voltage plus"},{"t":"Mutual inductance and the transformer","d":"Magnetic induction","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/induccion-magnetica#transformador","x":"03 Mutual inductance and the transformer When the flux of one coil passes through another, the current in the first induces an EMF in the second. If the two share an iron core, practically all the flux is shared and the voltages end up in the ratio of the turns: \\[ \\frac{V_2}{V_1} = \\frac{N_2}{N_1} \\qquad\\qquad \\frac{I_2}{I_1} = \\frac{N_1}{N_2} \\qquad\\qquad Z_{refl} = \\left(\\frac{N_1}{N_2}\\right)^2 Z_2 \\] Power is conserved —what is gained in voltage is lost in current— and the impedance is reflected with the square of the ratio. That third expression is the basis of impedance matching in audio and in radio frequency. And since the transformer needs the flux to vary, it does not work on direct current. Connecting it to DC is putting a short circuit across the source through the resistance of the winding, which is tiny. That is why power distribution is AC: only that way can the voltage be stepped up for transmission and stepped down for consumption."},{"t":"Eddy currents","d":"Magnetic induction","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/induccion-magnetica#foucault","x":"04 Eddy currents In a solid conductor subjected to a varying flux, the induced currents circulate in swirls within the material itself. They dissipate energy and, by Lenz’s law, brake whatever produced them. Where Effect What is done Transformer core Losses and heating Laminate it: sheets insulated from one another that cut the swirls Core at high frequency The losses grow with \\( f^2 \\) Ferrite: a magnetic ceramic that barely conducts Bus brake Brakes without contact or wear It is put to use Induction cooktop Heats the pot and not the burner It is put to use The first two rows explain why 50 Hz cores are made of stacked sheets and those of a 100 kHz switching power supply are ferrite: at that frequency, a solid iron core would become a heater. It is the same phenomenon solved in two ways depending on the frequency."},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Magnetic induction","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/induccion-magnetica#laboratorio","x":"05 In the lab Activity 1 · The slowly falling magnet Drop a neodymium magnet down a copper tube and down a plastic one of the same length, and time it. Explain the difference with Lenz’s law and estimate the energy dissipated in the tube. Activity 2 · Measuring the turn-off spike Switch a relay coil with a transistor and use the oscilloscope to observe the collector voltage when it opens, first without a flyback diode and then with it. Measure the spike in both cases and compare it with the maximum voltage of the transistor. Activity 3 · Turns ratio With a transformer that has two secondaries, measure all the no-load voltages and verify the turns ratio. Then load one secondary and measure the drop, obtaining the equivalent impedance of the winding."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Magnetic induction","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/induccion-magnetica#errores","x":"06 Common mistakes Believing that the field induces an EMF. What induces it is its change: a stationary loop in a constant field generates nothing. Taking the minus sign as a convention. It is Lenz’s law, and it is the conservation of energy. Switching an inductive load without a flyback diode. The spike destroys the transistor on the first cycle. Connecting a transformer to direct current. Without a change in flux, it is just the resistance of the winding. Using a solid iron core at high frequency. Eddy currents heat it up; use ferrite. Forgetting the \\( N^2 \\) in the inductance. Doubling the turns quadruples it."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Magnetic induction","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/induccion-magnetica#autoevaluacion","x":"07 Self-assessment What induces an electromotive force? The change of the magnetic flux in time, not the flux itself. What does the minus sign in Faraday’s law mean? That the induced current opposes the change that produced it. It is the conservation of energy. Why does a coil generate a spike when its current is cut off? Because \\( v = L\\,di/dt \\): if \\( di/dt \\) becomes enormous, so does the voltage. What is the flyback diode for? To give the coil current a path along which to keep flowing, and thus limit the voltage across the transistor. How is an impedance reflected through a transformer? With the square of the turns ratio. Why are 50 Hz cores laminated and 100 kHz cores ferrite? Because of eddy currents, whose losses grow with the square of the frequency. Lamination is enough at 50 Hz; at 100 kHz a material that does not conduct is needed."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Magnetic induction","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/induccion-magnetica#ampliar","x":"08 Further reading Raymond A. Serway and John W. Jewett. Physics for Scientists and Engineers with Modern Physics. 10th ed. (Spanish edition, “Física para ciencias e ingeniería”), Cengage, 2019. Faraday’s law, Lenz’s law, inductance, magnetic field energy and RL circuits. Edward M. Purcell and David J. Morin. Electricity and Magnetism. 3rd ed. (Spanish edition, “Electricidad y magnetismo”), Cambridge University Press, 2016. Induction treated with care, including the cases where the flux rule is misleading. Marty Brown. Power Supply Cookbook. 2nd ed., Newnes, 2001. Inductors, high-frequency transformers and the handling of the energy stored in a switching power supply."},{"t":"From the sample to the population","d":"Inference and estimation","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/inferencia#muestra","x":"01 From the sample to the population You never measure everything. You measure a sample and want to say something about the entire population: the whole batch, all the parts the process will ever produce, the true value of the resistance. That leap is inference, and it has rules. Population Sample What it is The entire set of interest The data you actually have Mean \\( \\mu \\), unknown \\( \\bar{x} \\), computable Standard deviation \\( \\sigma \\), unknown \\( s \\), computable It is called Parameter Estimator An estimator is a random variable: if you take another sample, you get another number. Being unbiased means that on average it gives the true parameter, and being consistent means that it approaches it as the sample grows. The sample mean has both properties. \\[ E[\\bar{X}] = \\mu \\qquad\\qquad \\sigma_{\\bar{X}} = \\frac{\\sigma}{\\sqrt{n}} \\] The second expression is the standard error: how much the sample mean varies from one sample to another. And by the central limit theorem, the distribution of \\( \\bar{X} \\) is approximately normal even if the population is not, provided that \\( n \\) is large enough."},{"t":"The confidence interval","d":"Inference and estimation","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/inferencia#intervalo","x":"02 The confidence interval Giving a single number as an estimate hides the uncertainty. A confidence interval reports a range built with a procedure that, repeated many times, captures the true parameter a known proportion of the time. \\[ \\bar{x} \\pm z_{\\alpha/2}\\frac{\\sigma}{\\sqrt{n}} \\qquad\\text{with }\\sigma\\text{ known}\\qquad\\qquad \\bar{x} \\pm t_{\\alpha/2,\\,n-1}\\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}} \\qquad\\text{with }\\sigma\\text{ estimated} \\] In practice \\( \\sigma \\) is almost never known, so you use Student’s t, which is a little wider to compensate for the extra uncertainty of having estimated the standard deviation as well. With large \\( n \\) the two coincide. What “95 % confidence” does NOT mean It does not mean that there is a 95 % probability that \\( \\mu \\) lies in that interval. Once calculated, the interval either contains \\( \\mu \\) or it does not: there is no chance involved, what is missing is information. What the 95 % describes is the procedure: if the sampling were repeated many times, 95 % of the intervals built this way would contain the true value. The lab below is made so that you can see this, because reading about it is not enough. Lab · what the confidence level means Forty samples taken from the same population, each with its interval. The vertical line is the true value, which in real life nobody knows. Count how many intervals miss it. Size of each sample = 10 Confidence level = 95 % Sampling seed = 1"},{"t":"How many measurements are needed","d":"Inference and estimation","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/inferencia#tamano","x":"03 How many measurements are needed Given a target precision, the sample size comes from solving for the half-width of the interval: \\[ n = \\left(\\frac{z_{\\alpha/2}\\,\\sigma}{E}\\right)^2 \\] With \\( E \\) the maximum allowable error. The square is what hurts: to cut the error in half you have to quadruple the sample, and to cut it by ten, multiply it by a hundred. If you want You need Half the error Four times as many measurements One tenth of the error A hundred times as many measurements Going from 95 % to 99 % confidence 73 % more measurements, for the same error The third row shows the other trade-off. For the same amount of data, more confidence means a wider interval: you can be more certain, or you can be more precise, but not both without measuring more. A 100 % confidence interval would be “from minus infinity to plus infinity,” perfectly true and perfectly useless. Averaging does not fix a systematic error The \\( \\sqrt{n} \\) reduces the random component of the uncertainty, not the systematic one. If the instrument is out of calibration and always reads 2 mV too high, measuring a million times gives an average with a tiny statistical uncertainty, centered 2 mV away from the true value. That is the difference between precision and accuracy, and the reason calibration exists."},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Inference and estimation","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/inferencia#laboratorio","x":"04 In the lab Activity 1 · The interval from a repeated measurement Measure the same voltage twenty times. Calculate the mean, the standard deviation and the 95 % confidence interval using Student’s t. Repeat with the first five measurements and compare the widths of the two intervals. Activity 2 · Checking the coverage Divide two hundred measurements into twenty groups of ten and build the 90 % interval of each group. Count how many contain the mean of the two hundred and compare with the two that theory predicts will fail. Activity 3 · Planning the experiment With the standard deviation estimated in activity 1, calculate how many measurements would be needed to know the voltage with an error of 0.1 mV at 95 %. Evaluate whether the number is practical and what would be advisable if it is not."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Inference and estimation","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/inferencia#errores","x":"05 Common mistakes Saying there is a 95 % probability that \\( \\mu \\) lies in the interval. The 95 % belongs to the procedure, not to that interval. Using \\( z \\) with small samples. With an estimated \\( \\sigma \\) and few data points, use t. Confusing \\( s \\) with the standard error. The latter is \\( s/\\sqrt{n} \\) and is much smaller. Believing that averaging corrects a systematic error. It only reduces the random one. Asking for more confidence expecting more precision. They go in opposite directions. Forgetting the square in the sample size. Improving tenfold costs a hundred times more."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Inference and estimation","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/inferencia#autoevaluacion","x":"06 Self-assessment What does a 95 % interval mean? That the procedure, repeated many times, produces intervals that contain the parameter 95 % of the time. What is the standard error of the mean? \\( \\sigma/\\sqrt{n} \\), or \\( s/\\sqrt{n} \\) if the standard deviation was estimated from the sample. When do you use t instead of z? When \\( \\sigma \\) is unknown and is estimated with \\( s \\). With large \\( n \\) the two coincide. How many measurements are needed to cut the error in half? Four times as many: the size goes with the square. What happens to the width when the confidence goes up? It grows. More confidence with the same data is paid for with less precision. Does averaging correct a calibration error? No. It reduces the random error and leaves the systematic one intact. That is what calibration is for."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Inference and estimation","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/inferencia#ampliar","x":"07 Further reading Douglas C. Montgomery and George C. Runger. Applied Statistics and Probability for Engineers. 7th ed. (Spanish edition, “Probabilidad y estadística aplicadas a la ingeniería”), Wiley, 2018. Chapters 7 and 8: sampling distributions, point estimation and confidence intervals. Sheldon Ross. Introduction to Probability and Statistics for Engineers and Scientists. 5th ed. (Spanish edition, “Introducción a la probabilidad y estadística para ingenieros”), Elsevier, 2018. The sampling distribution treated with care, including where Student’s t comes from. JCGM. Evaluation of measurement data — Guide to the expression of uncertainty in measurement. JCGM 100:2008 (Spanish edition published by the BIPM). How Type A and Type B uncertainties are combined, and how the expanded uncertainty is reported with its coverage factor."},{"t":"Two risks, two protections","d":"Protection and sizing of installations","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/instalaciones-dimensionamiento#seguridad","x":"01 Two risks, two protections An electrical installation has to protect against two different things. Overcurrent —overload or short circuit— heats the conductors until the insulation is damaged and a fire starts. Electric contact makes current flow through a person: direct, by touching a live part, or indirect, by touching an enclosure that became live because of a fault. Each risk has its own device: Device Protects against How it acts Thermal-magnetic circuit breaker Overload and short circuit A bimetal for slow overloads and an electromagnet for short circuits Fuse Overload and short circuit It melts; high breaking capacity Residual-current device (RCD) Direct and indirect contact Compares the current going out and the current coming back; it trips if more than 30 mA is missing Grounding Indirect contact Gives the fault current a low-resistance path and makes the protection act A current of about 30 mA through the body, for a fraction of a second, can cause ventricular fibrillation: that is why the 30 mA RCD is mandatory in dwellings. The practical presentation of installations and protections is in the technician program, in electrical installations and electrical protection."},{"t":"Switching and protection","d":"Protection and sizing of installations","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/instalaciones-dimensionamiento#maniobra","x":"02 Switching and protection Switching devices open and close circuits under normal conditions: switches, disconnectors (which only open without load, to isolate safely), contactors (which switch remotely and thousands of times). Protection devices open on their own on a fault. A thermal-magnetic circuit breaker does both things. Its trip curve (B, C or D) indicates at how many times the rated current the magnetic trip acts: 3 to 5 times for B, 5 to 10 for C, 10 to 20 for D, for loads with high inrush currents such as motors and transformers."},{"t":"Conductor sizing","d":"Protection and sizing of installations","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/instalaciones-dimensionamiento#conductores","x":"03 Conductor sizing A circuit is sized by chaining three conditions. The design current \\( I_B \\) comes from the load; the breaker has to have a rated current \\( I_n \\) greater than it; and the conductor, a current-carrying capacity \\( I_z \\) greater than the breaker’s, so that the breaker protects it: \\[ I_B \\le I_n \\le I_z \\] \\[ I_B = \\frac{P}{V \\cos\\varphi} \\] \\[ I_B = \\frac{P}{\\sqrt 3\\, V_L \\cos\\varphi} \\] The design current for a single-phase circuit and for a balanced three-phase one. The conductor’s current-carrying capacity depends on the cross-section, the insulation, how it is installed and the ambient temperature, and is given by the tables of the regulations. The second condition is the voltage drop: even if the conductor withstands the current, if the circuit is long the voltage at the load falls too far. The regulations limit it, typically between 3 and 5% depending on the type of circuit: \\[ \\Delta U \\approx k\\, \\frac{\\rho\\, L\\, I_B \\cos\\varphi}{S} \\] With \\( k = 2 \\) for single-phase and \\( \\sqrt 3 \\) for three-phase, \\( \\rho \\approx 0.0225\\ \\Omega\\,\\text{mm}^2/\\text{m} \\) for copper in service, \\( L \\) the length and \\( S \\) the cross-section. The reactance is neglected, valid for small cross-sections. In long circuits, it is the voltage drop and not the current that decides the cross-section. Lab · sizing a circuit You choose the load, its power factor and the circuit length. The design current is calculated, along with the standard breaker, the minimum cros"},{"t":"Power factor correction","d":"Protection and sizing of installations","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/instalaciones-dimensionamiento#factor","x":"04 Power factor correction An inductive load, such as a motor, takes in addition to the active power a reactive power that goes back and forth without doing work, but that flows through the conductors and heats them. Utilities penalize a low power factor. A capacitor in parallel supplies that reactive power locally: \\[ Q_C = P\\,(\\tan\\varphi_1 - \\tan\\varphi_2) \\] The reactive power of the capacitor to bring the power factor from \\( \\cos\\varphi_1 \\) to \\( \\cos\\varphi_2 \\). Correcting 15 kW from 0.85 to 0.95 requires 4.4 kvar and lowers the current from 26.8 A to 24.0 A. The concepts of AC power are in power in AC circuits, in the technician program. With electronic loads the situation changes: the low power factor is due to harmonics and not to phase shift, and a capacitor does not correct it, as seen in power quality."},{"t":"In the laboratory","d":"Protection and sizing of installations","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/instalaciones-dimensionamiento#laboratorio","x":"05 In the laboratory Activity 1 · Design of a circuit Size the outlet circuit of a workshop with five known loads, choosing the breaker, the cross-section and checking the voltage drop against the regulations in force. Activity 2 · Testing an RCD With an RCD tester, measure the trip current and trip time of a 30 mA breaker. Activity 3 · Correcting a motor Measure the active and reactive power of a motor at no load and under load, and calculate and connect the capacitor that brings its power factor to 0.95."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Protection and sizing of installations","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/instalaciones-dimensionamiento#errores","x":"06 Common mistakes Choosing the breaker by the cross-section and not by the load. It has to meet both conditions. Forgetting the voltage drop in long circuits. The conductor withstands the current, but the load receives too little voltage. Installing an RCD without grounding. It still protects against direct contact, but a fault to the frame stays live until someone touches it. Overcompensating the power factor. With the load disconnected, the capacitors leave the installation capacitive."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Protection and sizing of installations","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/instalaciones-dimensionamiento#autoevaluacion","x":"07 Self-assessment What current does a 10 kW three-phase load with cos φ = 0.8 draw at 380 V? \\( 10\\,000/(\\sqrt3 \\cdot 380 \\cdot 0.8) \\approx 19 \\) A. What drop does a single-phase circuit of 40 m, 10 A, cos φ = 1 and 2.5 mm² have? \\( 2 \\cdot 0.0225 \\cdot 40 \\cdot 10 / 2.5 = 7.2 \\) V: 3.3% of 220 V. What capacitor corrects 10 kW from cos φ 0.7 to 0.95? \\( 10 \\cdot (1.020 - 0.329) \\approx 6.9 \\) kvar. Why does the RCD not protect against overloads? Because it only measures the difference between the current going out and the current coming back; an overload flows in full through both conductors."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Protection and sizing of installations","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/instalaciones-dimensionamiento#ampliar","x":"08 Further reading Argentine Electrotechnical Association (AEA). Reglamentación para la Ejecución de Instalaciones Eléctricas en Inmuebles (AEA 90364) (in Spanish). AEA, current editions. The standard that governs installations in Argentina: protections, cross-sections, voltage drops and grounding. Marcelo A. Sobrevila. Instalaciones eléctricas (in Spanish). Alsina, various editions. The classic Argentine text on installation design. Schneider Electric. Electrical Installation Guide (Spanish edition: “Guía de diseño de instalaciones eléctricas”). Schneider Electric, according to IEC. The complete sizing of an installation with the IEC criteria, with examples."},{"t":"An area built from rectangles","d":"The definite integral","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/integral-definida#area","x":"01 An area built from rectangles How much charge did a varying current deliver? How much energy did a resistor dissipate? The two questions are the same: the area under a curve. And the area under an arbitrary curve has no formula, so it is constructed: the interval is split into n small pieces, a rectangle is raised over each one and they are added up. \\[ \\int_a^b f (x) d x = \\lim_{n \\to \\infty} \\sum_{i = 1}^n f (x_i^*) \\Delta x \\quad \\operatorname{with} \\; \\Delta x = \\frac{b -a}{n} \\] This is the Riemann sum. The more rectangles, the thinner they are, and the error tends to zero. The symbol ∫ is a stretched S: sum. The sign matters The definite integral is a signed area: whatever lies below the x-axis is subtracted. That is why the integral over one full cycle of a sinusoid is zero, even though the drawn area is not. That is exactly the zero average value of alternating current. Lab · Riemann sums Raise the number of rectangles and watch the sum approach the exact value. f(x) = x² on [0; 3] f(x) = sin x on [0; π] One cycle of a sinusoid on [0; 2π] Discharge: 5·e −x on [0; 3] Left endpoints Right endpoints Midpoints Trapezoids n 6"},{"t":"Properties used without a second thought","d":"The definite integral","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/integral-definida#propiedades","x":"02 Properties used without a second thought Property What it says Linearity \\( \\int (\\alpha f + \\beta g) = \\alpha \\int f + \\beta \\int g \\). It is superposition applied to integrals Additivity over the interval \\( \\int_a^b = \\int_a^c + \\int_c^b \\): it can be cut into pieces, useful for piecewise functions Orientation \\( \\int_a^b = -\\int_b^a \\), and \\( \\int_a^a = 0 \\) Monotonicity If \\( f \\le g \\) on \\( [a,\\, b] \\), then \\( \\int f \\le \\int g \\) Boundedness \\( m(b-a) \\le \\int f \\le M(b-a) \\), with m and M the minimum and the maximum Mean value theorem for integrals If f is continuous on [a, b], there is a point c where f(c) is exactly the average value: \\[ f_{\\operatorname{avg}} = \\frac{1}{b -a} \\int_a^b f (x) d x = f (c) \\] Geometrically: the rectangle of height f(c) has the same area as the region under the curve. This is what an average-responding instrument measures, and what an analog multimeter indicates on DC."},{"t":"Average value and RMS value of a signal","d":"The definite integral","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/integral-definida#valores","x":"03 Average value and RMS value of a signal Here the definite integral stops being an exercise and becomes the definition of two quantities that are measured every day in the lab: \\[ V_{\\operatorname{avg}} = \\frac{1}{T} \\int_0^T v (t) d t \\quad V_{\\operatorname{rms}} = \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{T} \\int_0^T v^2 (t) d t} \\] The RMS value, or root mean square, is the square root of the mean of the square. Its definition comes from requiring it to dissipate the same power as a DC value of the same size. Where 0.707 comes from For v(t) = V p ·sin(ωt), the square is V p ²·sin²(ωt), and using sin²θ = (1 − cos 2θ)/2, its mean value over one period is V p ²/2. So V rms = V p /√2 ≈ 0.707·V p. The 220 V of the mains is RMS: the peak is 311 V. All of that is a definite integral solved once. Waveform Average value (full cycle) RMS value Sine wave 0 V p /√2 = 0.707 V p Full-wave rectified sine wave 2V p /π = 0.637 V p 0.707 V p Half-wave rectified sine wave V p /π = 0.318 V p V p /2 = 0.5 V p Symmetric square wave 0 V p Symmetric triangle wave 0 V p /√3 = 0.577 V p That table explains why an ordinary multimeter, which measures the average value of the rectified signal and multiplies by 1.11, gets it wrong with non-sinusoidal waves: for a square wave it reads 11% too high. A “true RMS” instrument really does compute the integral of the square. See digital measuring instruments."},{"t":"When there is no antiderivative: numerical integration","d":"The definite integral","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/integral-definida#numerica","x":"04 When there is no antiderivative: numerical integration Many functions have no elementary antiderivative —e −x² is the classic case— and often you have only measured points, with no formula. In those cases you integrate numerically, which means settling for a well-chosen finite Riemann sum: Method Idea Error Rectangles Approximates f by a constant on each piece Proportional to Δx Midpoint The constant is taken at the center of the piece Proportional to Δx² Trapezoids Joins the endpoints with a segment Proportional to Δx² Simpson Fits a parabola over every two pieces Proportional to Δx⁴ This is exactly what a digital oscilloscope does when it displays the RMS value of a captured signal: it adds up the squares of the samples, divides by the count and takes the root. The integral became a computation over an array of numbers."},{"t":"In the lab","d":"The definite integral","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/integral-definida#laboratorio","x":"05 In the lab Exercise 1 · Riemann by hand Compute ∫₀³ x² dx with 3, 6 and 12 rectangles using the left, right and midpoint rules. Compare with the exact value (9) and see which converges fastest. Contrast with the lab. Exercise 2 · RMS value, measured and computed With the generator, apply a sine, a square and a triangle wave of equal peak amplitude to a resistor. Measure with an ordinary multimeter and with a true RMS one, and compare with the table. Explain the differences. Exercise 3 · Energy of a pulse Capture a current pulse through a known load, export the samples and compute the energy as the sum of v·i·Δt. Compare with the measured temperature rise, if the setup allows it."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"The definite integral","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/integral-definida#errores","x":"06 Common mistakes Confusing area with integral: if the function changes sign, to get the geometric area you have to integrate the absolute value or split the interval. Computing the RMS value as the root of the mean, or averaging without squaring. Using 0.707 for any waveform: that factor applies only to the sine wave. Forgetting the 1/T in the average or in the RMS value. Integrating over less than one period when computing values of a periodic signal. Measuring a non-sinusoidal signal with an average-responding multimeter and believing the number."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"The definite integral","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/integral-definida#autoevaluacion","x":"07 Self-assessment What is ∫₀³ x² dx? x³/3 evaluated between 0 and 3: 9. Why is the integral of a sinusoid over one period zero? Because the positive and negative half-cycles have equal areas and opposite signs. Its average value is zero. A ±10 V square signal. Average value and RMS value? Average value 0 and RMS value 10 V: the square is always 100. What is the peak value of a 220 V RMS mains supply? 220·√2 ≈ 311 V. Insulation is rated for the peak, not for the RMS value. Which numerical method converges faster, trapezoids or midpoint? Both have error proportional to Δx², but that of the midpoint rule is usually half as large. Simpson’s rule, which combines the two, is much better. An average-responding instrument measures a square wave of 10 V peak. What does it read? It rectifies (average value 10 V) and multiplies by 1.11: it reads 11.1 V, when the true RMS value is 10 V. An error of 11%."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"The definite integral","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/integral-definida#ampliar","x":"08 Further reading James Stewart. Single Variable Calculus: Early Transcendentals. 7th ed. (Spanish edition, “Cálculo de una variable. Trascendentes tempranas”), Cengage Learning, 2012. Chapter 5 builds the definite integral from Riemann sums, with the full notation. Manuel Sadosky and Rebeca Ch. de Guber. Elementos de cálculo diferencial e integral (in Spanish). Alsina. A classic, rigorous treatment of the definite integral and its properties. Robert L. Boylestad. Introductory Circuit Analysis. 12th ed. (Spanish edition, “Introducción al análisis de circuitos”), Pearson, 2011. Where average and RMS values are used, with the rectified waveforms worked out."},{"t":"Integrating along a path","d":"Line integrals in the complex plane","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/integrales-complejas#que","x":"01 Integrating along a path For a real variable, \\( \\int_a^b \\) has only one possible route: from the point \\( a \\) to the point \\( b \\) along the line. In the plane there are infinitely many paths between two points, and the integral is defined along one of them. The notation says so: \\[ \\int_C f(z)\\,dz = \\lim_{n \\to \\infty} \\sum_{k=1}^{n} f(z_k)\\,\\Delta z_k \\] The same Riemann sum as always, except that now each term is a product of complex numbers: it has a modulus and a phase. That detail changes everything. Each term \\( f(z_k)\\,\\Delta z_k \\) is a vector in the plane, and the integral is the sum of those vectors placed one after the other. In the lab it looks like this: a chain of little arrows whose final end point is the value of the integral. Why it matters to an electronics engineer The inverse Laplace transform is a line integral in the complex plane —the Bromwich integral, along a vertical line to the right of all the poles—. Nobody computes it that way in practice, but the method that is used, residues, is derived from here. Everything that follows in the course goes through this topic."},{"t":"How to compute it: parametrize","d":"Line integrals in the complex plane","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/integrales-complejas#calcular","x":"02 How to compute it: parametrize There is nothing new to invent. You describe the path with a real parameter \\( t \\), substitute, and end up with an ordinary integral: \\[ z = z(t), \\quad t \\in [a,b] \\;\\Longrightarrow\\; \\int_C f(z)\\,dz = \\int_a^b f\\big(z(t)\\big)\\,z'(t)\\,dt \\] The example you have to know by heart is the circle traversed once counterclockwise. With \\( z = r\\,e^{jt} \\) we get \\( dz = j\\,r\\,e^{jt}\\,dt \\), and then: \\[ \\oint_{|z| = r} \\frac{dz}{z} = \\int_0^{2\\pi} \\frac{j\\,r\\,e^{jt}}{r\\,e^{jt}}\\,dt = j\\int_0^{2\\pi} dt = 2\\pi j \\] It does not depend on the radius. That \\( 2\\pi j \\) will appear in every theorem from here on. And the rest of the powers, over the same circle, give zero: \\[ \\oint_{|z| = r} z^{n}\\,dz = 0 \\qquad \\text{for every integer } n \\ne -1 \\] The reason is that all those powers have an antiderivative —\\( z^{n+1}/(n+1) \\)— and an antiderivative makes the integral over a closed path vanish. The exception is \\( n = -1 \\), because its antiderivative would be the logarithm, which is multivalued and does not return to the same value after going around."},{"t":"The integral, path by path","d":"Line integrals in the complex plane","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/integrales-complejas#lab","x":"03 The integral, path by path Lab · the sum that gives the integral On the left, the path and the points where the function is evaluated. On the right, the terms \\( f(z_k)\\,\\Delta z_k \\) chained together: the end of the chain is the value of the integral. Click or tap the left plane to move the end point. f(z) = z f(z) = z² f(z) = 1/z f(z) = e^z f(z) = z̄ · not analytic f(z) = Re(z) · not analytic Path: the straight segment Path: the arc Path: in two legs Path: the circle |z| = 1, closed Subdivisions = 24"},{"t":"When the path does not matter","d":"Line integrals in the complex plane","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/integrales-complejas#camino","x":"04 When the path does not matter Let us try \\( f(z) = \\bar{z} \\), from \\( 0 \\) to \\( 1 + j \\). Along the straight segment, with \\( z = t(1+j) \\): \\[ \\int_0^1 t(1-j)\\,(1+j)\\,dt = \\int_0^1 2t\\,dt = 1 \\] And along the two-leg path, first from \\( 0 \\) to \\( 1 \\) and then from \\( 1 \\) to \\( 1+j \\): \\[ \\underbrace{\\int_0^1 t\\,dt}_{1/2} \\;+\\; \\underbrace{\\int_0^1 (1 - jt)\\,j\\,dt}_{\\tfrac12 + j} \\;=\\; 1 + j \\] Two paths, two different results. By contrast, with \\( f(z) = z \\) both give \\( j \\), and the same happens with \\( z^2 \\) and with \\( e^z \\). The difference is the usual one: \\( \\bar{z} \\) is not analytic, and the others are. If \\( f \\) is… The integral between two points… Over a closed path… Analytic on the whole region Does not depend on the path Is zero Analytic except at an enclosed pole Depends on which side you pass Equals \\( 2\\pi j \\) times the residue Not analytic Depends on the path Can be anything The first row is Cauchy’s integral theorem, which is the next topic. It is worth checking it beforehand in the lab: with \\( z^2 \\) the three paths end at the same point, and with \\( \\bar{z} \\) they do not."},{"t":"The ML bound","d":"Line integrals in the complex plane","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/integrales-complejas#cota","x":"05 The ML bound Often there is no need to compute the integral, only to bound it, especially to show that a piece of contour contributes zero when the radius goes to infinity. The tool is elementary: \\[ \\left| \\int_C f(z)\\,dz \\right| \\le M\\,L, \\qquad M = \\max_{z \\in C} |f(z)|, \\qquad L = \\text{length of } C \\] With it one proves, for example, that the integral of \\( 1/z^2 \\) over a semicircle of radius \\( R \\) is bounded by \\( (1/R^2)(\\pi R) = \\pi/R \\), which tends to zero. That argument is what allows contours to be closed at infinity, keeping only the part of interest, which is exactly what is done when computing an inverse transform."},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Line integrals in the complex plane","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/integrales-complejas#laboratorio","x":"06 In the lab Activity 1 · Verifying the 2πj Compute by hand \\( \\oint dz/z \\) over the circle of radius 2 parametrizing with \\( z = 2e^{jt} \\). Repeat with radius 0.5 and check that it gives the same result. Compare it with the lab by choosing the closed path. Activity 2 · The two paths of the conjugate Redo with pencil the two calculations of section 04 and verify that they give 1 and \\( 1+j \\). Then look in the lab for an end point where the difference between paths is largest. Activity 3 · The bound, measured For \\( f(z) = 1/(z^2+1) \\) over the upper semicircle of radius \\( R = 10 \\), estimate \\( M \\) and \\( L \\), compute the bound \\( ML \\) and compare it with the numerical value of the integral. Check how conservative it is."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Line integrals in the complex plane","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/integrales-complejas#errores","x":"07 Common mistakes Forgetting the \\( z'(t) \\). When parametrizing you have to multiply by the derivative of the path, not just change the variable. Taking for granted that the integral does not depend on the path. It holds only if \\( f \\) is analytic on the whole region between the two paths. Getting the direction of traversal wrong. Reversing it changes the sign; the positive direction is counterclockwise. Believing that \\( \\oint z^n dz = 0 \\) always. It fails precisely at \\( n = -1 \\), which is the interesting case. Using the ML bound as if it were the value. It is an upper bound, and it usually lies far above."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Line integrals in the complex plane","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/integrales-complejas#autoevaluacion","x":"08 Self-assessment What is \\( \\oint dz/z \\) over \\( |z| = 3 \\)? \\( 2\\pi j \\). It does not depend on the radius: the \\( r \\) cancels when parametrizing. And \\( \\oint z^5\\,dz \\) over the same circle? Zero: it has the antiderivative \\( z^6/6 \\), and over a closed path the antiderivative returns to the same value. Why is \\( n = -1 \\) the exception? Because its antiderivative is the logarithm, which is multivalued: after one trip around, the argument has changed by \\( 2\\pi \\). What does \\( \\int \\bar{z}\\,dz \\) give from 0 to \\( 1+j \\) along the straight segment? 1. Along the two-leg path it gives \\( 1 + j \\): the conjugate is not analytic and the path matters. What is the ML bound for? To prove that an arc contributes zero when the radius grows, so that contours can be closed at infinity. What line integral is behind the inverse Laplace transform? The Bromwich integral, along a vertical line to the right of all the poles. It is solved by closing the contour and applying residues."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Line integrals in the complex plane","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/integrales-complejas#ampliar","x":"09 Further reading Ruel V. Churchill and James W. Brown. Complex Variables and Applications. 7th ed. (Spanish edition, “Variable compleja y aplicaciones”), McGraw-Hill, 2004. The chapter on integrals, with the ML bound and path independence carefully proved. Murray R. Spiegel. Complex Variables. Schaum’s Outline Series (Spanish edition, “Variable compleja”), McGraw-Hill, 2011. Dozens of solved contour integrals, which is the only way to get the hang of them. A. David Wunsch. Complex Variables with Applications. 2nd ed. (Spanish edition, “Variable compleja con aplicaciones”), Addison-Wesley Iberoamericana, 1997. Connects the contour integral with the inverse Laplace transform earlier than any other."},{"t":"Integrating along a curve","d":"Line and surface integrals","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/integrales-linea-superficie#escalar","x":"01 Integrating along a curve Until now we integrated over intervals and over regions. Now the domain is a curve. If what is being integrated is a scalar field —a density, for example—, the line integral gives the total quantity along the wire: \\[ \\int_C f \\, d s = \\int_a^b f (\\vec{r} (t)) \\left| {\\vec{r}}' (t) \\right| \\, d t \\] The factor |r′(t)| converts the step in the parameter into actual length: it is the same one as in arc length. With f = 1 you get the length of the curve; with f = linear density, the mass of a wire; with f = charge density, the total charge of a thin-wire conductor."},{"t":"Circulation: the work done by a field","d":"Line and surface integrals","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/integrales-linea-superficie#vectorial","x":"02 Circulation: the work done by a field If what is being integrated is a vector field, only the component along the path counts. A dot product appears, and the result is the work —or the circulation —: \\[ W = \\int_C \\vec{F} \\cdot d \\vec{r} = \\int_a^b \\vec{F} (\\vec{r} (t)) \\cdot {\\vec{r}}' (t) \\, d t \\] The component of F perpendicular to the path does no work. That is why a centripetal force does not change the speed. Reversing the direction of travel changes the sign. And when the curve is closed, it is written with a circle on the integral sign: that is the circulation, which in electromagnetism appears in Ampère’s law and in Faraday’s law. Lab · work along different paths The three paths join the same endpoints. With a conservative field they give the same result; with a nonconservative one, they do not. F = (y, x) · conservative F = (0, −1) · weight, conservative F = (−y, x) · nonconservative F = (−x·y, x) · nonconservative Show all three paths"},{"t":"Path independence","d":"Line and surface integrals","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/integrales-linea-superficie#independencia","x":"03 Path independence Fundamental theorem for line integrals If F = ∇f, then for any path C going from A to B: \\[ \\int_C \\nabla f \\cdot d \\vec{r} = f (B) -f (A) \\] It is the vector-calculus counterpart of the Fundamental Theorem of Calculus: only the endpoints matter. And if the path is closed, A = B and the integral equals zero. That is why, in a DC circuit, Kirchhoff’s voltage law says that the sum of voltages around a closed loop is zero: the electrostatic field is conservative. In contrast, when there is a time-varying magnetic flux, the circulation of E around a loop is not zero, and its value is the induced EMF: that is precisely Faraday’s law."},{"t":"Surface integrals and flux","d":"Line and surface integrals","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/integrales-linea-superficie#superficie","x":"04 Surface integrals and flux The next step is to integrate over a surface. With a scalar field you get, for example, the mass of a thin sheet; with a vector field, the flux: how much passes through the surface. \\[ \\Phi = \\iint_S \\vec{F} \\cdot \\hat{n} \\, d S \\] n̂ is the unit normal to the surface. Only the perpendicular component counts: what moves “sideways” does not cross anything. Flux of Called Where it appears Electric field E Electric flux Gauss’s law: \\( \\Phi = \\dfrac{Q_{enc}}{\\varepsilon_0} \\) Magnetic field B Magnetic flux \\( \\Phi \\), in webers Faraday’s law: \\( \\text{EMF} = -\\dfrac{d\\Phi}{dt} \\). It is the principle of the transformer Current density J Current \\( I = \\iint \\vec{J} \\cdot \\hat{n} \\, dS \\) through the cross section of a conductor Poynting vector \\( \\vec{S} = \\vec{E} \\times \\vec{H} \\) Power The power carried by a wave through a surface Why orientation matters A closed surface has its normal pointing outward by convention; an open one requires declaring which is its positive face, and that choice fixes the positive direction of travel around the edge by the right-hand rule. The minus sign in Faraday’s law —Lenz’s law— depends on that convention."},{"t":"Where this is used in electronics","d":"Line and surface integrals","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/integrales-linea-superficie#aplicaciones","x":"05 Where this is used in electronics Induced EMF. A transformer works because a time-varying magnetic flux through the secondary induces a circulation of electric field: the output voltage. The whole calculation is a surface integral differentiated in time. Inductance. It is defined as flux linkage over current, L = Φ/I: another surface integral. Current through a conductor. If the current density is not uniform —skin effect at high frequency— the total current is the integral of J over the cross section. Power radiated by an antenna. It is the flux of the Poynting vector through a sphere surrounding it. From there comes the radiation pattern of antennas. Thermal dissipation. The heat leaving a heat sink is the flux of the heat-flux vector through its surface."},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Line and surface integrals","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/integrales-linea-superficie#laboratorio","x":"06 In the lab Activity 1 · Two paths, one result Compute by hand ∫F·dr for F = (y, x) from (0,0) to (2,1), first along the straight segment and then along the L-shaped path. Verify that they agree and find the potential f = xy that explains it. Activity 2 · Measuring an induced EMF With a coil and a magnet, measure the induced voltage when moving it at different speeds. Relate the reading to −dΦ/dt and verify that the sign changes when the motion is reversed. Activity 3 · Flux through a loop Compute the flux of a uniform field through a flat loop for three different angles, and check experimentally that the induced EMF when rotating it is maximum when the plane is parallel to the field."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Line and surface integrals","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/integrales-linea-superficie#errores","x":"07 Common mistakes Forgetting the factor |r′(t)| in the line integral of a scalar field. Confusing ∫f ds with ∫F·dr: the first does not depend on the direction, the second does. Assuming that work does not depend on the path without checking that the field is conservative. Forgetting the orientation of the surface and getting the sign of the flux wrong. Integrating the magnitude of the field instead of its normal component when computing flux."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Line and surface integrals","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/integrales-linea-superficie#autoevaluacion","x":"08 Self-assessment What does ∫ C ds represent with f = 1? The length of the curve C. Compute ∫F·dr for F = (y, x) from (0,0) to (2,1) along the straight segment. Since F = ∇(xy), the integral equals f(2,1) − f(0,0) = 2 − 0 = 2, regardless of the path. What is the circulation of a conservative field around a closed path? Zero. Why is Kirchhoff’s voltage law a case of this? Because the sum of voltage drops around a loop is the circulation of the electrostatic field, which is conservative and therefore gives zero. A 5 cm² loop in a perpendicular field of 0.2 T. What is the flux? Φ = B·A = 0.2 · 5·10 −4 = 10 −4 Wb. If the field dropped to zero in 1 ms, the average EMF would be 0.1 V. What does the flux of the Poynting vector measure? The electromagnetic power crossing the surface under consideration."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Line and surface integrals","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/integrales-linea-superficie#ampliar","x":"09 Further reading James Stewart. Multivariable Calculus. 7th ed. (Spanish edition, “Cálculo de varias variables. Trascendentes tempranas”), Cengage Learning, 2012. Chapter 16 goes through line integrals, path independence and surfaces. Jerrold E. Marsden and Anthony J. Tromba. Vector Calculus. 5th ed. (Spanish edition, “Cálculo vectorial”), W. H. Freeman, 2003. With formal care about the orientation and parametrization of surfaces. Matthew N. O. Sadiku. Elements of Electromagnetics. Spanish edition, “Elementos de electromagnetismo,” 5th ed., Alfaomega, 2018. The same integrals, but always posed from a field problem."},{"t":"Summing over a region","d":"Double and triple integrals","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/integrales-multiples#idea","x":"01 Summing over a region The single integral summed over an interval. The double integral sums over a region of the plane: the region is cut into small rectangles, the function is evaluated in each one, multiplied by its area, and all of them are added up. \\[ \\iint_D f (x, y) d A = \\lim_{n \\to \\infty} \\sum_{i = 1}^n f (x_i^*, y_i^*) \\Delta A_i \\] If f ≥ 0, the result is the volume under the surface above that region. With f = 1, it is the area of the region. As before, nobody computes the limit: it is converted into two chained single integrals, the iterated integrals. You integrate first in one variable, treating the other as a constant, and then in the one that is left."},{"t":"The limits, the only hard part","d":"Double and triple integrals","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/integrales-multiples#iteradas","x":"02 The limits, the only hard part For a region where, for each x between a and b, y runs between two curves: \\[ \\iint_D f \\, d A = \\int_a^b \\left[ \\int_{g_1 (x)}^{g_2 (x)} f (x, y) \\, d y \\right] d x \\] The inner limits may depend on the outer variable; the outer ones are always numbers. If that does not hold, the limits are wrong. Changing the order of integration The same region can be swept the other way around: for each y, x between two curves. The result is the same —guaranteed by Fubini’s theorem— but one of the two forms is usually much easier, and sometimes the other is simply impossible to solve by hand. Drawing the region is mandatory: it is the only way to read the limits without making mistakes. Lab · double integral over a region The grid shows the small rectangles of the sum; each one contributes f·ΔA. Increase the resolution and watch how it converges. Region: rectangle 0 ≤ x ≤ 2, 0 ≤ y ≤ 1 Region: triangle under y = x, 0 ≤ x ≤ 2 Region: circle of radius 1.5 Region: between y = x² and y = 2 f = 1 · gives the area f = x·y f = x² + y² f = 1 + x · variable density divisions 12"},{"t":"Polar coordinates: when the region is round","d":"Double and triple integrals","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/integrales-multiples#polares","x":"03 Polar coordinates: when the region is round If the region is a circle or a sector, the limits in Cartesian coordinates come out with awkward roots. In polar coordinates they become constants: \\[ x = \\rho \\cos \\theta, \\; y = \\rho \\operatorname{sin} \\theta \\quad d A = \\rho \\, d \\rho \\, d \\theta \\] The factor ρ is not optional: it is the Jacobian of the change of variables, and it accounts for the fact that polar small rectangles are larger away from the center. Worked example The area of a circle of radius R: ∬dA = ∫₀ 2π ∫₀ R ρ dρ dθ = ∫₀ 2π (R²/2) dθ = πR². Forgetting the ρ would have given 2πR, which does not even have units of area. In three dimensions the same approach gives cylindrical coordinates (dV = ρ dρ dθ dz), convenient for tubes and coils, and spherical coordinates (dV = r² sin φ dr dφ dθ), convenient for spheres and for radial fields."},{"t":"What they are used for","d":"Double and triple integrals","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/integrales-multiples#aplicaciones","x":"04 What they are used for Quantity Integral In electronics Area \\( \\iint \\, dA \\) Surface of a heat sink or of a trace Volume \\( \\iiint \\, dV \\text{ or } \\iint f \\, dA \\) Volume of a package, of a magnetic core Mass \\( \\iint \\delta (x,y) dA \\) With variable density; the same for charge with surface density \\( \\sigma \\) Center of mass \\( \\dfrac{1}{m}\\iint x\\cdot \\delta \\, dA \\) Mechanical balance of an assembly, center of gravity of a plate Moment of inertia \\( \\iint r^{2}\\cdot \\delta \\, dA \\) Rotors, disks, shafts: it determines the angular acceleration Average value \\( \\dfrac{1}{A}\\iint f \\, dA \\) Average temperature of a plate, average illuminance of a room And one that appears as soon as fields are studied: the total charge of a surface distribution is the double integral of the density, and from there the field follows by applying Gauss’s theorem, which is seen in the theorems of vector calculus."},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Double and triple integrals","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/integrales-multiples#laboratorio","x":"05 In the lab Exercise 1 · The same integral in both orders Compute ∬ xy dA over the triangle with vertices (0,0), (2,0) and (2,2), first integrating in y and then in x, and then the other way around. Verify that the result is the same and compare the work involved in each path. Exercise 2 · Cartesian versus polar Compute ∬ (x² + y²) dA over the circle of radius 2 in both systems. The goal is to get a feel for why in polar coordinates it takes three lines. Exercise 3 · Center of mass of a plate Cut an irregularly shaped plate out of cardboard, compute its center of mass by dividing it into simple shapes, and check it by hanging it from two different points: the plumb lines cross at the center of gravity."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Double and triple integrals","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/integrales-multiples#errores","x":"06 Common mistakes Putting variable limits on the outer integral. The outer ones are numbers; if a loose variable is left, the setup is wrong. Forgetting the Jacobian ρ when switching to polar coordinates, or the r² sin φ in spherical coordinates. Not drawing the region and deducing the limits by heart. Changing the order without redoing the limits: it is not enough to swap dx and dy. Integrating a density and calling the result “area”: it pays to check the units of the result."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Double and triple integrals","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/integrales-multiples#autoevaluacion","x":"07 Self-assessment Compute ∫₀¹∫₀² xy dy dx. The inner one: ∫₀² xy dy = x·[y²/2]₀² = 2x. Then ∫₀¹ 2x dx = 1. What does ∬ dA represent over a region? The area of the region. Why does the factor ρ appear in polar coordinates? Because the area element in polar coordinates is not dρ dθ but ρ dρ dθ: the arc grows with the radius. It is the Jacobian of the change of variables. Area of the circle of radius 3 using a double integral in polar coordinates. ∫₀ 2π ∫₀³ ρ dρ dθ = ∫₀ 2π (9/2) dθ = 9π ≈ 28.3. How is the charge of a plate with density σ(x,y) computed? By integrating the density over the surface: Q = ∬ σ(x,y) dA."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Double and triple integrals","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/integrales-multiples#ampliar","x":"08 Further reading James Stewart. Multivariable Calculus. 7th ed. (Spanish edition, “Cálculo de varias variables. Trascendentes tempranas”), Cengage Learning, 2012. Chapter 15 covers double and triple integrals, changing the order, polar coordinates and physical applications. Jerrold E. Marsden and Anthony J. Tromba. Vector Calculus. 5th ed. (Spanish edition, “Cálculo vectorial”), W. H. Freeman, 2003. Carefully develops the change of variables and the Jacobian in general. Claudio Pita Ruiz. Cálculo vectorial. Prentice Hall (in Spanish). Many worked examples of setting up the limits, which is where almost everyone gets stuck."},{"t":"Three ways to move heat","d":"Heat transfer mechanisms","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/intercambio-calor#tres","x":"01 Three ways to move heat Heat always goes from the hot body to the cold one, and it arrives by three paths that act at the same time. In electronics all three are present in any piece of equipment, but they do not weigh the same. Mechanism How it travels Law Where it dominates Conduction By contact, without the matter moving \\( \\dot{Q} = kA\\,\\Delta T/L \\) From the chip to the package and from there to the heat sink Convection The fluid moves and carries the heat away \\( \\dot{Q} = hA\\,\\Delta T \\) From the heat sink to the air Radiation Electromagnetic waves, no medium needed \\( \\dot{Q} = \\varepsilon\\sigma A(T^4 - T_a^4) \\) Contributes little below 100 °C; in a vacuum it is the only thing left The third row has the fourth power, so radiation grows very fast with temperature. At 60 °C over a 25 °C ambient, polished aluminum (emissivity 0.05) radiates barely about 12 W per square meter; black anodized —emissivity close to 0.9— radiates almost twenty times more, and the heat sink gains ten or fifteen percent overall, because the fins shade one another. That is why heat sinks are black, and not for aesthetics. \\[ \\sigma = 5.67\\times10^{-8}\\ \\mathrm{W/m^2K^4} \\] Stefan–Boltzmann constant. In a satellite there is no air, so there is no convection: all the electronics is cooled exclusively by radiation, and that is why spacecraft thermal design is a different problem."},{"t":"The analogy that solves everything","d":"Heat transfer mechanisms","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/intercambio-calor#analogia","x":"02 The analogy that solves everything Fourier's law for a flat wall has exactly the form of Ohm's law, and that coincidence is no accident: both describe a flow pushed by a potential difference against a resistance. \\[ \\Delta T = \\dot{Q}\\,R_{th} \\qquad\\longleftrightarrow\\qquad V = I\\,R \\] The dissipated power plays the role of current, the temperature difference that of voltage, and the thermal resistance —in degrees per watt— that of resistance. And thermal resistances in series add up, just like electrical ones. Thermal quantity Unit Equivalent to Dissipated power \\( \\dot{Q} \\) W Current Temperature difference K Voltage Thermal resistance \\( R_{th} \\) K/W Resistance Thermal capacitance \\( C_{th} \\) J/K Capacitance The last row explains the transient regime: a heat sink with a lot of mass takes time to heat up, just as a large capacitor takes time to charge, and the time constant is \\( R_{th}C_{th} \\). That is why a transistor withstands power peaks far above what it could withstand in steady state."},{"t":"The junction-to-ambient chain","d":"Heat transfer mechanisms","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/intercambio-calor#cadena","x":"03 The junction-to-ambient chain Heat is born at the semiconductor junction and has to reach the air. On the way it crosses three resistances in series, and the temperature drops across each one: \\[ T_j = T_a + P\\,(R_{jc} + R_{cs} + R_{sa}) \\] \\( R_{jc} \\) junction-to-case, which the manufacturer sets; \\( R_{cs} \\) case-to-heat-sink, which depends on the mounting; and \\( R_{sa} \\) heat-sink-to-ambient, which is the only one you choose by buying. \\( T_j \\) cannot exceed the \\( T_{j\\max} \\) in the datasheet, almost always 150 °C. Lab · calculating a heat sink The complete thermal chain, with values from real datasheets. You move the power and the ambient temperature, choose the package, interface and heat sink, and the junction temperature appears along with how much power the assembly can handle. TO-220 · Rjc 1.0 K/W TO-247 · Rjc 0.45 K/W D2PAK on copper · Rjc 1.0 K/W TO-92 · no heat sink possible dry contact · Rcs 1.2 K/W thermal paste · Rcs 0.5 K/W silicone pad · Rcs 1.3 K/W mica with paste, insulated · Rcs 1.4 K/W no heat sink clip-on heat sink · Rsa 20 K/W 50 mm finned · Rsa 5 K/W 100 mm finned · Rsa 2.5 K/W finned with fan · Rsa 0.8 K/W Dissipated power = 10 W Ambient temperature = 25 °C Thermal paste does not conduct: it fills Two apparently flat metal surfaces really touch over less than 1% of their area; the rest is air, which is an excellent insulator. The paste is not there because it conducts well —it conducts much worse than metal— but to displace that air. Hence m"},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Heat transfer mechanisms","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/intercambio-calor#laboratorio","x":"04 In the lab Activity 1 · Measuring a thermal resistance Mount a power resistor on a heat sink with a thermocouple attached to the fin. Dissipate a known power, wait for steady state and calculate \\( R_{sa} \\) from the difference with respect to ambient. Compare with the heat sink datasheet. Activity 2 · How much the paste contributes Repeat the previous measurement with dry contact, with paste and with a silicone pad, always with the same tightening torque. Calculate \\( R_{cs} \\) in the three cases and rank them. Activity 3 · The transient Record the heat sink temperature against time from the moment the power is applied until steady state. Fit an exponential, obtain the time constant and from it the thermal capacitance of the assembly."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Heat transfer mechanisms","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/intercambio-calor#errores","x":"05 Common mistakes Using too much thermal paste. The useful layer is the minimum; the excess adds resistance. Using \\( R_{ja} \\) when there is a heat sink. That value assumes the package in open air and it is not valid for the real assembly. Calculating with the room's ambient. Inside the enclosure it is quite a bit hotter. Forgetting that mica insulates but gets in the way. It adds thermal resistance in exchange for electrical insulation. Designing for \\( T_{j\\max} \\). That is the destruction limit, not the operating point: leave a margin. Always neglecting radiation. Below 100 °C it contributes little, but in a vacuum it is the only thing."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Heat transfer mechanisms","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/intercambio-calor#autoevaluacion","x":"06 Self-assessment What role does power play in the thermal analogy? That of current. The temperature difference plays voltage and the thermal resistance plays resistance. Why are heat sinks black? Because of emissivity: black anodizing radiates much better than polished aluminum, and that adds ten or fifteen percent. What is thermal paste really for? To displace the air trapped between two surfaces that only touch over a tiny fraction of their area. How is the electronics of a satellite cooled? Only by radiation: with no air there is no convection. What sets the thermal time constant? The product \\( R_{th}C_{th} \\), just as in an RC circuit. That is why a heat sink with mass withstands power peaks. Which of the three resistances in the chain is chosen by buying? \\( R_{sa} \\), the heat sink's. \\( R_{jc} \\) is set by the manufacturer of the semiconductor and \\( R_{cs} \\) depends on the mounting."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Heat transfer mechanisms","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/intercambio-calor#ampliar","x":"07 Further reading Raymond A. Serway and John W. Jewett. Physics for Scientists and Engineers with Modern Physics. 10th ed., Cengage, 2019 (Spanish edition, “Física para ciencias e ingeniería”). Conduction, convection and radiation with Fourier's law and Stefan–Boltzmann. Frank P. Incropera and David P. DeWitt. Fundamentals of Heat and Mass Transfer. 4th ed., Wiley, 1996 (Spanish edition, “Fundamentos de transferencia de calor”, Pearson, 1999). The reference of the discipline; the chapter on thermal resistances in series is exactly this unit. Gordon N. Ellison. Thermal Computations for Electronics. 2nd ed., CRC Press, 2020. Thermal design applied to electronic equipment, with real packages, heat sinks and enclosures."},{"t":"Two resources to share out","d":"System intercomparison","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/intercomparacion-sistemas#recursos","x":"01 Two resources to share out Every communication system spends two scarce resources: power and bandwidth. Comparing systems means seeing how much of each they need to deliver a given quality, which in analog is the output signal-to-noise ratio and in digital the error rate. Depending on which one is lacking, systems divide into two groups: System Scarce resource What is chosen Examples Power-limited The energy of the received signal Robust modulations and powerful codes, even if they take up bandwidth Space probes, GPS, battery-powered sensors Bandwidth-limited The allocated spectrum Constellations with many points and more power Microwave radio links, cable modem, Wi-Fi Cellular and Wi-Fi systems move from one group to the other depending on distance: they choose the modulation and the code at each instant from the signal-to-noise ratio that the receiver measures."},{"t":"Analog systems, compared","d":"System intercomparison","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/intercomparacion-sistemas#analogicos","x":"02 Analog systems, compared With the results of noise in analog modulations, for a tone and the same received power: System Bandwidth Output SNR Threshold SSB \\( W \\) \\( \\gamma \\) No DSB-SC \\( 2W \\) \\( \\gamma \\) No AM, m = 1 \\( 2W \\) \\( \\gamma/3 \\) With an envelope detector FM, β = 5 \\( 12W \\) \\( 37.5\\,\\gamma \\) Yes, near γ = 20 dB SSB is the most efficient in spectrum and wideband FM the one that makes the best use of power. PCM adds another possibility: above its own error threshold, quality does not depend on the channel but on the quantization, and the signal can be regenerated at every repeater without accumulating noise. That is the reason networks became digital."},{"t":"The Shannon plane","d":"System intercomparison","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/intercomparacion-sistemas#plano","x":"03 The Shannon plane In digital, the two measures are the spectral efficiency \\( \\eta = R_b/B \\) and the required \\( E_b/N_0 \\). The Shannon–Hartley formula, with \\( S = E_b R_b \\) and \\( N = N_0 B \\), transmitting at capacity, gives the minimum possible for each efficiency: \\[ \\eta = \\log_2\\!\\left(1 + \\eta\\, \\frac{E_b}{N_0}\\right) \\] \\[ \\frac{E_b}{N_0} \\ge \\frac{2^{\\eta} - 1}{\\eta} \\] With η = 1 bit/s/Hz the minimum is 0 dB; with η = 6, 10.2 dB. Even if infinite bandwidth is used, \\( E_b/N_0 \\) cannot fall below \\( \\ln 2 \\), −1.59 dB: this is the Shannon limit. Each modulation occupies a point in the plane, and the horizontal distance to the curve is the gain that a code could recover. M-QAM moves upward, gaining efficiency at the expense of power; orthogonal FSK moves to the left, spending bandwidth to save power. Lab · modulations on the Shannon plane The curve is the Shannon limit; below it no system is possible. The points are the uncoded modulations, at the \\( E_b/N_0 \\) that gives the chosen error rate, with ideal Nyquist pulses (α = 0). For coherent orthogonal FSK a tone spacing of \\( 1/2T \\) is assumed. Bit error rate 10⁻³ Bit error rate 10⁻⁵ Bit error rate 10⁻⁷ BPSK QPSK 8-PSK 16-QAM 64-QAM 256-QAM 4-FSK 8-FSK 16-FSK"},{"t":"Choosing a system","d":"System intercomparison","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/intercomparacion-sistemas#eleccion","x":"04 Choosing a system At an error rate of 10⁻⁵, uncoded modulations lie between 7 and 10 dB to the right of the limit. Error-correcting codes recover a good part of that distance: the convolutional codes of the 1970s gained about 5 dB, and present-day turbo and LDPC codes come within about 1 dB of the curve. The design of a link combines the pieces of the whole subject: The link budget, with the receiver noise figure, gives the available \\( E_b/N_0 \\). The allocated bandwidth sets the minimum spectral efficiency. The Shannon plane shows whether the pair is possible and what coding gain is needed. The modulation is chosen for its efficiency and for its envelope, depending on the available amplifiers."},{"t":"In the lab","d":"System intercomparison","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/intercomparacion-sistemas#laboratorio","x":"05 In the lab Activity 1 · Budget of a link Calculate the budget of a real microwave radio link from its datasheets and locate its operating point on the Shannon plane. Activity 2 · Adaptive modulation With a Wi-Fi analyzer, record the modulation and rate that a device uses at different distances from the access point, together with the reported signal-to-noise ratio. Activity 3 · Coding gain Simulate on the computer a BPSK with and without a Hamming (7,4) code, and measure the coding gain at an error rate of 10⁻⁵, taking into account the energy of the redundancy bits."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"System intercomparison","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/intercomparacion-sistemas#errores","x":"06 Common mistakes Comparing modulations by their error rate at the same S/N. The correct comparison is at the same \\( E_b/N_0 \\) and taking bandwidth into account. Believing that transmission is possible at any \\( E_b/N_0 \\) if a lot of bandwidth is used. Below −1.59 dB it is impossible. Forgetting the energy of the redundancy when measuring the gain of a code. Parity bits also cost power. Always choosing the most efficient modulation. In a power-limited system the opposite is better."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"System intercomparison","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/intercomparacion-sistemas#autoevaluacion","x":"07 Self-assessment What is the minimum E b /N 0 for transmitting at 2 bit/s/Hz? \\( (2^2 - 1)/2 = 1.5 \\): 1.76 dB. An uncoded QPSK needs 9.6 dB for 10⁻⁵. How much could an ideal code gain at the same efficiency? The limit at 2 bit/s/Hz is 1.76 dB: about 7.8 dB. What resource does a space probe have little of, and what modulation suits it? Power: a binary or few-point modulation, with a powerful code, even if it takes up more bandwidth. What bandwidth does an FM with β = 5 occupy and how much does it improve the SNR over DSB? 12W, six times DSB, and it improves it by \\( 1.5 \\cdot 25 = 37.5 \\) times: 15.7 dB."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"System intercomparison","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/intercomparacion-sistemas#ampliar","x":"08 Further reading Bernard Sklar. Digital Communications: Fundamentals and Applications. 2nd ed., Prentice Hall, 2001. The spectral-efficiency plane and the design trade-offs of a link. John G. Proakis and Masoud Salehi. Communication Systems Engineering. 2nd ed., Prentice Hall, 2002. The comparison of analog and digital systems in the same framework. A. Bruce Carlson and Paul B. Crilly. Communication Systems. 5th ed., McGraw-Hill, 2010. The trade-off between bandwidth and power in analog and digital systems."},{"t":"The program stops being in charge","d":"Graphical user interfaces","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/interfaces-graficas#inversion","x":"01 The program stops being in charge A console program is in charge: it asks for a value, calculates, prints, ends. A graphical interface reverses that relationship. The program starts, draws the window and waits; the one who decides what happens is the user. Sequential program Event-driven program The code controls the order The user controls the order scanf blocks until there is data Nothing blocks: you are notified when something happens Starts and ends Lives in a loop until the window is closed A single line of execution Many handlers, each one short The event loop Underneath every graphical interface there is the same loop, whatever it is called: while the window is open: event = get_from_queue() # waits if there is none handler = find_handler_for(event) handler(event) redraw_what_changed() The operating system deposits every click, every key press and every mouse movement into that queue. The program takes them out one at a time and calls the corresponding handler. Lab · the event loop, in slow motion Use the controls below: each action queues an event. Process them one at a time and see which handler runs. Press the button Checkbox Option A Option B Option C Slider 50 Process the next event Automatic loop"},{"t":"Widgets and containers","d":"Graphical user interfaces","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/interfaces-graficas#widgets","x":"02 Widgets and containers An interface is built from widgets —the controls— placed inside containers that decide how they are arranged. The container tree is what lets the window adapt when it is resized, instead of having everything in fixed coordinates. Widget What it is for Event it generates Button Triggering an action click Text field Typing a value change, key, focus Checkbox and radio button Choosing between alternatives state change Slider A continuous value change, many times per second List and table Showing and choosing records selection Canvas Free drawing redraw, mouse There are two approaches to arranging them: absolute positions —quick to do, but they break as soon as the size or the font changes— and layout managers by rows, columns or grid, which is always the better choice."},{"t":"Separating the interface from the logic","d":"Graphical user interfaces","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/interfaces-graficas#separar","x":"03 Separating the interface from the logic The rule that prevents the most problems: a button’s handler does not do the work, it only calls whatever does. If the calculation, the measurement or the access to the device is written inside the handler, it cannot be tested without opening the window, nor reused from a script. def on_press_measure(): # handler: three lines v = instrument.measure_voltage() # the logic, separate label.config(text=f\"{v:.3f} V\") chart.add(v) That is the core of the patterns studied under their own names —model-view-controller and its variants—: the model holds the data and the rules, the view displays them and the controller translates events into calls to the model. Never block the loop If a handler takes two seconds, the window is frozen for two seconds: it does not redraw, does not respond and the system marks it as “not responding”. Anything that takes time —reading a port, downloading, heavy calculation— goes in another thread, and when it finishes it notifies the interface. The rule is strict: only the interface thread may touch the widgets; the others send it the result through a queue or through the mechanism the library provides."},{"t":"An instrumentation interface","d":"Graphical user interfaces","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/interfaces-graficas#instrumentacion","x":"04 An instrumentation interface In electronics, the interface almost always accompanies a piece of equipment: a measurement bench, a process control, a recorder. What is expected of it: Visible status. Connected or not, on which port, since when. Continuous reading without blocking. One thread reads the instrument; the interface refreshes every 100 or 200 ms, not at 60 frames per second. Units and significant figures. Show 3.7 V, not 3.700000001. Logging and export. Saving the series as CSV is the first thing anyone using it will ask for. Errors in plain sight. If the equipment was disconnected, say so; do not leave the last value frozen. Confirmation of anything dangerous. A power output is not turned on with a careless click. Toolkit Language Where it fits Tkinter Python Comes with Python; ideal for getting started and for internal tools Qt (PyQt / Qt in C++) Python or C++ Large, cross-platform applications with graphics Dear ImGui C++ Debugging panels and lab tools Local web interface Any + browser The device serves a page; it is used from any machine"},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Graphical user interfaces","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/interfaces-graficas#laboratorio","x":"05 In the lab Exercise 1 · Logging the events Build a window with a button, a field and a slider, and make each handler print to the console which event arrived. Move the slider and count how many events a single drag generates. Exercise 2 · Freezing the window on purpose Put a sleep(3) inside a button’s handler and check that the interface goes dead. Then move that work to a thread and verify that the window stays alive. Exercise 3 · A panel for the microcontroller Using the protocol from the previous page, build an interface that turns an output on and off, sets a PWM with a slider and shows the sensor reading on a scrolling chart."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Graphical user interfaces","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/interfaces-graficas#errores","x":"06 Common mistakes Doing heavy work in the handler and freezing the window. Touching widgets from another thread. It is the cause of the hardest crashes to reproduce. Mixing the logic with the interface and being left with no way to test anything. Positioning everything in absolute coordinates and having it break when the size changes. Redrawing the entire chart at 60 frames per second when the data changes ten times per second. Not reporting the error and leaving the last value on screen as if it were current."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Graphical user interfaces","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/interfaces-graficas#autoevaluacion","x":"07 Self-assessment What is the event loop? The cycle that takes events from the queue, calls the corresponding handler and redraws. Every graphical interface has one. Why does a window freeze? Because a handler is taking a long time and the loop cannot keep processing events or redrawing. Can a worker thread update a label directly? No: only the interface thread may touch the widgets. The worker thread sends it the result through a queue or through the mechanism the library provides. How many events does dragging a slider generate? Many, one for each change of value. That is why a slider’s handler has to be cheap, or the real work has to be spaced out. What advantage does a layout manager have over fixed coordinates? The window adapts when the size, resolution or font changes, without the controls overlapping. What does the model-view-controller pattern separate? The data and rules (model), what is displayed (view) and the translation of events into actions (controller). It allows testing the logic without opening the window."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Graphical user interfaces","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/interfaces-graficas#ampliar","x":"08 Further reading Mark Summerfield. Rapid GUI Programming with Python and Qt. Prentice Hall, 2007. Events, threads and interface design with a real-application mindset. Official Python documentation. tkinter — Interface to Tcl/Tk. Python Software Foundation. The library that comes already installed: enough for most lab tools. Jakob Nielsen. Usability Engineering. Morgan Kaufmann, 1993. The classic heuristics: visibility of status, error prevention, user control."},{"t":"Two slits","d":"Interference and diffraction","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/interferencia-difraccion#interferencia","x":"01 Two slits Young’s experiment is one of the cleanest pieces of evidence that light is a wave. Two slits illuminated by the same source behave like two emitters in phase, and on the screen not two spots appear but a succession of fringes. \\[ d\\sin\\theta = m\\lambda \\quad\\text{maxima} \\qquad\\qquad \\Delta y = \\frac{\\lambda L}{d} \\quad\\text{fringe spacing} \\] The second expression holds for small angles, which is the usual case. Note that the spacing grows if the slits get closer: the closer together the sources are, the wider the pattern spreads. The decisive point is that coherence is required: the two slits have to emit with a stable phase relationship. That is why the experiment is done by illuminating both with the same source, and why two different lamps never produce fringes: their phases change randomly millions of times per second and the pattern averages out until it disappears."},{"t":"A single slit does something too","d":"Interference and diffraction","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/interferencia-difraccion#difraccion","x":"02 A single slit does something too A slit of finite width diffracts: the light spreads out as it passes, and a pattern appears with a wide central maximum and minima on either side. It seems the opposite of what one would expect —the narrower the slit, the wider the beam— and it comes from the same superposition. \\[ a\\sin\\theta = m\\lambda \\quad\\text{minima, with } m \\ne 0 \\qquad\\qquad I = I_0\\left(\\frac{\\sin\\beta}{\\beta}\\right)^2,\\ \\ \\beta = \\frac{\\pi a\\sin\\theta}{\\lambda} \\] The central maximum is twice as wide as the others and concentrates most of the energy. When the slit width is much greater than \\( \\lambda \\), the pattern narrows and the light once again looks like a ray: there geometric optics applies. In a real setup both things happen at once: the interference between slits draws the fine fringes and the diffraction of each slit modulates them, giving them an envelope. That is why in a double-slit pattern some fringes are missing: they fall exactly where the envelope is zero. Lab · interference and diffraction The complete pattern, with the diffraction envelope drawn separately. Increase the number of slits and watch how the maxima sharpen without moving: that is what turns a grating into a spectrometer. Number of slits = 2 Slit separation = 20 µm Width of each slit = 5 µm Wavelength = 633 nm"},{"t":"The diffraction grating","d":"Interference and diffraction","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/interferencia-difraccion#red","x":"03 The diffraction grating With many slits instead of two, the maxima are still where \\( d\\sin\\theta = m\\lambda \\) —the position does not change— but they become much narrower. And since their position depends on \\( \\lambda \\), each color emerges in a different direction: that is a spectrometer. \\[ R = \\frac{\\lambda}{\\Delta\\lambda} = mN \\] The resolving power grows with the number of illuminated slits and with the order. A grating of 600 lines per millimeter with one centimeter illuminated has 6000 slits, and in first order it distinguishes wavelengths separated by a tenth of a nanometer. Why a CD makes colors The tracks of a disc are 1.6 µm apart on a CD and 0.74 µm on a DVD, which is the same order as the wavelength of light. It works as a reflection diffraction grating: each color is deflected differently and that is why the rainbow appears. The DVD, with tracks closer together, spreads out more, and that difference can be seen by eye by putting the two side by side."},{"t":"The limit that diffraction imposes","d":"Interference and diffraction","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/interferencia-difraccion#resolucion","x":"04 The limit that diffraction imposes Every aperture diffracts, so the image of a point is never a point: it is a disk with rings. Two objects can be told apart as long as their disks do not overlap too much, and the Rayleigh criterion sets the threshold: \\[ \\theta_{\\min} = 1.22\\,\\frac{\\lambda}{D} \\] With \\( D \\) the diameter of the aperture. It is not a problem of lens quality: it is a physical limit. However perfect an instrument may be, it cannot separate two points closer together than that. Instrument \\( D \\) Limit Consequence Human pupil 3 mm 0.013° Telling apart two headlights at about 6 km 200 mm telescope 200 mm 0.7 arcseconds Separating double stars Deep-UV photolithography — Tens of nm The minimum size of a transistor The last row is the reason the chip industry has always run toward shorter wavelengths: from 436 nm to 365, to 248, to 193 nm, and from there to the extreme ultraviolet of 13.5 nm. Each jump was enormously expensive and each was made for the same reason: diffraction does not allow drawing details much smaller than the wavelength used to draw them."},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Interference and diffraction","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/interferencia-difraccion#laboratorio","x":"05 In the lab Activity 1 · Measuring λ with a double slit Project onto a distant wall the pattern of a laser through a double slit of known separation. Measure the fringe spacing and solve for \\( \\lambda \\). Compare with the stated wavelength of the laser. Activity 2 · Measuring the track pitch of a CD Use a CD as a reflection grating with a laser of known wavelength. Measure the angle of the first order and solve for the track spacing. Repeat with a DVD and compare. Activity 3 · Diffraction by a hair Illuminate a hair with a laser and measure the diffraction pattern it produces. Apply the slit formula —a thin obstacle gives the same pattern, by Babinet’s principle— and estimate its diameter."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Interference and diffraction","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/interferencia-difraccion#errores","x":"06 Common mistakes Believing that two lamps can be made to interfere. Coherence is required: the same source, a stable phase relationship. Confusing \\( d \\) with \\( a \\). One is the separation between slits; the other, the width of each one. Expecting the beam to narrow when the slit is narrowed. It spreads: it is the other way around. Thinking that more slits shift the maxima. They do not move them: they sharpen them. Attributing the resolution limit to the lenses. It is diffraction, and no lens overcomes it. Forgetting the diffraction envelope. It is what explains the fringes that are missing."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Interference and diffraction","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/interferencia-difraccion#autoevaluacion","x":"07 Self-assessment What condition is needed to see interference fringes? Coherence: the two sources must keep a stable phase relationship. That is why a single source is used for the two slits. What happens to the fringes if the slits are brought closer together? They move apart. The spacing is \\( \\lambda L/d \\), inversely proportional to \\( d \\). What changes when the number of slits is increased? The maxima become much sharper but do not move. That is what makes a diffraction grating useful. Why are some fringes missing in a double-slit pattern? Because they fall where the diffraction envelope of each slit is zero. What does the Rayleigh criterion say? That the minimum resolvable angle is \\( 1.22\\lambda/D \\). It is a physical limit, not one of optical quality. Why did photolithography move to shorter wavelengths? Because diffraction does not allow drawing details much smaller than the wavelength used."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Interference and diffraction","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/interferencia-difraccion#ampliar","x":"08 Further reading Eugene Hecht. Optics. 5th ed. (Spanish edition, “Óptica”), Pearson, 2017. Chapters 9 and 10: interference, coherence, Fraunhofer diffraction and gratings. Raymond A. Serway and John W. Jewett. Physics for Scientists and Engineers with Modern Physics. 10th ed. (Spanish edition, “Física para ciencias e ingeniería”), Cengage, 2019. Young, thin films, the single slit, gratings and the Rayleigh criterion with solved problems. Chris Mack. Fundamental Principles of Optical Lithography. Wiley, 2007. How the diffraction limit governs the minimum size of a transistor, and what is done to stretch it."},{"t":"Two ways of getting into a measurement","d":"Normal-mode and common-mode interference","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/interferencias-medicion#modos","x":"01 Two ways of getting into a measurement Interference is any voltage that adds to the one you want to measure. Depending on how it appears at the instrument’s terminals, two modes are distinguished: Normal mode (or differential): the interference is in series with the signal, between the two input terminals. The instrument has no way of separating it from the signal by its connection; only by its frequency or its shape. Common mode: the interference appears equally on both terminals with respect to ground. An ideal differential instrument ignores it, because it measures only the difference; a real one lets a fraction through. In a measurement of millivolts near the 220 V mains, 50 Hz interference can be thousands of times larger than the signal. Measuring well is, in large part, knowing where it comes from and how it is rejected."},{"t":"How interference couples in","d":"Normal-mode and common-mode interference","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/interferencias-medicion#acoplamientos","x":"02 How interference couples in Coupling Mechanism Remedy Capacitive The electric field of a conductor at a given voltage injects current into a high-impedance circuit through the capacitance between the two Shield grounded at a single point; lower the circuit impedance Inductive The magnetic field of a conductor carrying current induces a voltage in any loop it crosses: \\( V = \\omega B A \\) Reduce the loop area with a twisted pair; move away; magnetic shielding Conductive Two devices grounded at different points: the potential difference makes current flow through the grounds Single-point ground, galvanic isolation Radiated Radio-frequency fields that a cable receives like an antenna and a semiconductor rectifies Complete shielding, filters at the inputs The twisted pair works because each twist reverses the direction of the loop: the voltage induced in one twist cancels with that of the next. A cable shield, on the other hand, protects against the electric field, but very little against the low-frequency magnetic field."},{"t":"Ground loops","d":"Normal-mode and common-mode interference","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/interferencias-medicion#lazos","x":"03 Ground loops If a sensor is grounded at one end of a plant and the instrument at the other, there can be a volt or more between the two grounds, because of the currents flowing through the installation. That difference appears in common mode at the input, and if the cable shield joins the two grounds, a current flows through it that converts it into normal mode. The solutions are to break the loop: ground at a single point, measure with differential inputs, use floating instruments or galvanic isolators (isolation amplifiers, optocouplers, transformers)."},{"t":"Common-mode rejection","d":"Normal-mode and common-mode interference","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/interferencias-medicion#rechazo-comun","x":"04 Common-mode rejection The common-mode rejection ratio (CMRR) of a differential amplifier says how many times less it amplifies the common voltage than the differential one. But there is a second mechanism, often worse: if the impedances of the two lines are not equal, the common voltage produces different drops in each and is converted into a differential one before reaching the amplifier: \\[ V_{err,1} = \\frac{V_{cm}}{CMRR} \\] \\[ V_{err,2} \\approx V_{cm}\\, \\frac{\\Delta R}{|Z_{cm}|} \\] \\( \\Delta R \\) is the resistance difference between the two lines (sensor and cables) and \\( Z_{cm} \\), the impedance of each input to ground, dominated at 50 Hz by its capacitance. A 120 dB amplifier is useless if the imbalance of the lines converts the common voltage into a differential one before the input. The guard technique attacks the second mechanism: a shield around the lines is connected to the common-mode voltage, so that there is no potential difference across the leakage capacitances and no current flows through them."},{"t":"Normal-mode rejection","d":"Normal-mode and common-mode interference","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/interferencias-medicion#rechazo-normal","x":"05 Normal-mode rejection Normal-mode interference can only be separated by its frequency. If the signal is slow, such as a temperature, a low-pass filter removes it. Integrating converters do something finer: if they integrate for a whole number of mains cycles, the 50 Hz interference and all its harmonics average to zero. \\[ NMRR = \\left| \\frac{\\sin(\\pi f T)}{\\pi f T} \\right|^{-1} \\] \\( T \\) is the integration time. With \\( T = 20 \\) ms, a multiple of the 50 Hz period, the rejection is theoretically infinite. A multimeter set for 60 Hz (16.7 ms) on a 50 Hz mains rejects barely 14 dB. Lab · measuring 5 mV in an environment with 50 Hz A thermocouple gives 5 mV, about 120 °C for type K. Between its ground and the instrument’s there is a 50 Hz common-mode voltage, the loop of the cables picks up 10 mV in normal mode and each input has 10 pF and 1 GΩ to ground. The bars, on a logarithmic scale, compare each error with the signal and with 41 µV, which is 1 °C. 0.1 V common mode 1 V common mode 10 V common mode CMRR of 60 dB CMRR of 80 dB CMRR of 100 dB CMRR of 120 dB 1 ms integration 16.7 ms (60 Hz) integration 20 ms (50 Hz) integration 100 ms integration Imbalance between the lines ΔR ="},{"t":"In the laboratory","d":"Normal-mode and common-mode interference","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/interferencias-medicion#laboratorio","x":"06 In the laboratory Activity 1 · Shield and twisted pair Measure with the oscilloscope the 50 Hz picked up by two parallel 1 m wires, by a twisted pair and by a shielded cable, near a transformer. Compare the protection against the electric and the magnetic field. Activity 2 · The imbalance With an instrumentation amplifier, inject 1 V of common mode and measure the output with balanced lines and with 10 kΩ in series in only one of them. Activity 3 · The integration time Set a bench multimeter to 1, 10 and 100 mains cycles of integration and measure a DC voltage with 50 Hz superimposed."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Normal-mode and common-mode interference","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/interferencias-medicion#errores","x":"07 Common mistakes Grounding the shield at both ends. It closes a ground loop through the shielding. Relying only on the amplifier’s CMRR. The imbalance of the lines can dominate. Setting the instrument for 60 Hz on a 50 Hz mains. Leaving high-impedance inputs open. They pick up all the electric field of the environment."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Normal-mode and common-mode interference","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/interferencias-medicion#autoevaluacion","x":"08 Self-assessment What error does 1 V of common mode produce in an amplifier with 80 dB of CMRR? \\( 1\\ \\text{V} / 10^4 = 100\\ \\mu\\text{V} \\), about 2.4 °C on a type K thermocouple. What 50 Hz rejection does a converter that integrates for 16.7 ms have? \\( |\\sin(0.833\\pi)/(0.833\\pi)| = 0.19 \\): about 14 dB. Why does the twisted pair reduce inductive coupling? Because the voltages induced in successive loops have opposite signs and cancel. Where is the shield of a low-frequency signal cable grounded? At a single end, usually the instrument’s, so as not to form a ground loop."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Normal-mode and common-mode interference","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/interferencias-medicion#ampliar","x":"09 Further reading Ralph Morrison. Grounding and Shielding: Circuits and Interference. 6th ed., Wiley-IEEE Press, 2016. The reference book on grounding, shielding and ground loops. Henry W. Ott. Electromagnetic Compatibility Engineering. Wiley, 2009. The coupling mechanisms and their remedies, with numbers. Keithley Instruments. Low Level Measurements Handbook. 7th ed., Keithley, 2014. Guarding, ground loops and interference rejection in very low-level measurements."},{"t":"From the specification sheet to silicon","d":"Introduction to analog integrated circuit design","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/introduccion-ci-analogicos#flujo","x":"01 From the specification sheet to silicon Designing an analog integrated circuit means sharing out a budget: with a limited current and area you have to obtain gain, bandwidth, phase margin, output swing, noise and offset. Applied Electronics I introduced the basics, matching, Pelgrom's law and design with \\( g_m/I_D \\), in analog integrated circuit design. Here we go through the design of a complete block using everything seen in the subject: feedback, frequency response and compensation. Stage What is decided Tool Specification Gain, GBW, phase margin, slew rate, power, swing The system that uses the block Topology One or two stages, folded cascode, rail-to-rail input Hand calculation Sizing Currents, \\( g_m/I_D \\), lengths and widths \\( g_m/I_D \\) curves of the technology Verification Process, voltage and temperature corners; Monte Carlo SPICE simulator with the manufacturer's models Layout Matching, common centroid, guard rings, shielding Layout editor and parasitic extraction"},{"t":"The two-stage op amp","d":"Introduction to analog integrated circuit design","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/introduccion-ci-analogicos#dosetapas","x":"02 The two-stage op amp The most widely taught CMOS op amp has a differential pair with a mirror load, a differential stage that provides gain and converts to a single-ended output, followed by a common source with an active load. Each stage contributes a gain on the order of the intrinsic gain of the transistor, and the product easily exceeds 70 dB: \\[ A_0 = g_{m1}\\,(r_{o2} \\parallel r_{o4}) \\cdot g_{m6}\\,(r_{o6} \\parallel r_{o7}) \\] \\[ \\mathrm{GBW} = \\frac{g_{m1}}{2\\pi C_c} \\] The Miller capacitor \\( C_c \\), between the input and the output of the second stage, sets the dominant pole. The gain–bandwidth product depends only on the input transconductance and on \\( C_c \\)."},{"t":"Miller compensation and the zero","d":"Introduction to analog integrated circuit design","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/introduccion-ci-analogicos#miller","x":"03 Miller compensation and the zero Miller compensation splits the poles: the first goes down and the second goes up to near \\( g_{m6}/C_L \\). But \\( C_c \\) also creates a direct path from the input to the output of the second stage, which produces a right-half-plane zero: it adds phase lag like a pole, and takes margin away from the amplifier: \\[ p_2 \\approx \\frac{g_{m6}}{C_L} \\] \\[ z = \\frac{1}{C_c\\,(1/g_{m6} - R_z)} \\] Without a resistor \\( R_z \\), the zero is at \\( g_{m6}/C_c \\), in the right half-plane. With \\( R_z = 1/g_{m6} \\) in series with \\( C_c \\) it goes to infinity, and with a larger \\( R_z \\) it moves to the left half-plane, where it adds phase. The classic design rules, from Allen and Holberg, call for \\( g_{m6} \\ge 10\\, g_{m1} \\) to push the zero away and \\( C_c \\ge 0.22\\, C_L \\) so that the second pole lies at more than 2.2 times the GBW: with that the phase margin is around 60°. The slew rate is set by the tail current charging \\( C_c \\), \\( I_5/C_c \\), unless the second stage cannot charge the load fast enough: \\( (I_6 - I_5)/C_L \\). Lab · sizing a two-stage op amp A two-stage op amp in a 3.3 V process, with \\( g_m/I_D = 10\\ \\mathrm{V^{-1}} \\) in all the signal transistors, DC gain 5000 and 0.2 pF of parasitic capacitance at the output of the first stage. It is used as a follower. The curve is the loop gain; the dotted line marks 0 dB. Tail current I 5 = Second-stage current I 6 = Miller capacitor C c = Load C L = No nulling resistor R z = 1/g m6 R z = 2/g "},{"t":"Other topologies and blocks","d":"Introduction to analog integrated circuit design","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/introduccion-ci-analogicos#otras","x":"04 Other topologies and blocks The folded cascode obtains high gain in a single stage: the only high-impedance node is the output, the load itself compensates it and there is no Miller zero. It is the usual choice for capacitive loads, as in switched-capacitor circuits. With a class AB output stage, rail-to-rail swing is obtained, and with two complementary pairs at the input, a rail-to-rail input. Around the amplifiers, an analog chip needs other blocks: a bandgap reference, which combines the base–emitter voltage, which falls with temperature, with a voltage proportional to absolute temperature to obtain about 1.2 V almost independent of it; a constant-g m bias; internal LDO regulators and data converters. All are designed with the same method: specify, choose the topology, size by hand with \\( g_m/I_D \\) and verify in the simulator."},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Introduction to analog integrated circuit design","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/introduccion-ci-analogicos#laboratorio","x":"05 In the lab Activity 1 · Simulation Size the two-stage op amp in a SPICE simulator with models of an open technology, such as SKY130, and compare the simulated phase margin with the one calculated by hand. Activity 2 · Corners and Monte Carlo Repeat the simulation at the process and temperature corners and with Monte Carlo of the offset. Identify which specification is lost first. Activity 3 · Slew rate Simulate the response to a large step and measure the rising and falling slew rate. Explain the asymmetry."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Introduction to analog integrated circuit design","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/introduccion-ci-analogicos#errores","x":"06 Common mistakes Forgetting the right-half-plane zero. With small \\( g_{m6} \\), the zero gets closer and the phase margin calculated with two poles is optimistic. Designing for the nominal load. The second pole depends on \\( C_L \\); with more load the margin falls. Shrinking \\( C_c \\) to gain GBW without looking at the second pole. The GBW goes up and the phase margin goes down. Trusting the hand calculation without simulating the corners. Currents and capacitances change with process and temperature."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Introduction to analog integrated circuit design","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/introduccion-ci-analogicos#autoevaluacion","x":"07 Self-assessment What GBW does an op amp with g m1 = 100 μS and C c = 2 pF have? \\( 10^{-4}/(2\\pi \\cdot 2\\cdot10^{-12}) \\approx 8 \\) MHz. With g m6 = 2 mS and C c = 2 pF, where is the zero without R z ? At \\( 2\\cdot10^{-3}/(2\\pi \\cdot 2\\cdot10^{-12}) \\approx 159 \\) MHz, in the right half-plane. What slew rate does a 20 μA tail give with C c = 2 pF? 10 V/μs. What value of R z eliminates the zero? \\( R_z = 1/g_{m6} \\)."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Introduction to analog integrated circuit design","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/introduccion-ci-analogicos#ampliar","x":"08 Further reading Phillip E. Allen and Douglas R. Holberg. CMOS Analog Circuit Design. 3rd ed., Oxford University Press, 2012. The design procedure for the two-stage op amp, step by step. Behzad Razavi. Design of Analog CMOS Integrated Circuits. 2nd ed., McGraw-Hill, 2017. Op amps, compensation and the bandgap reference with physical intuition. Paul G. A. Jespers and Boris Murmann. Systematic Design of Analog CMOS Circuits Using Pre-Computed Lookup Tables. Cambridge University Press, 2017. Design with \\( g_m/I_D \\) taken to a systematic method."},{"t":"Two complementary transistors","d":"CMOS inverter","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/inversor-cmos#estructura","x":"01 Two complementary transistors The CMOS inverter is a PMOS between the supply and the output and an NMOS between the output and ground, with both gates tied to the input. With the input at 0, the PMOS conducts and the NMOS is off: the output sits at \\( V_{DD} \\). With the input at \\( V_{DD} \\), the reverse. In either state one of the two transistors is off and there is no current path between the supply and ground: the inverter at rest consumes nothing but leakage. That property, more than any other, explains why CMOS replaced all the earlier logic families. A chip with billions of transistors would be impossible if each gate consumed at rest like a TTL gate. The equations of each transistor are those of the previous unit."},{"t":"The transfer characteristic","d":"CMOS inverter","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/inversor-cmos#transferencia","x":"02 The transfer characteristic As the input is swept from 0 to \\( V_{DD} \\), the output passes through five regions depending on the region each transistor is in. At the extremes one is off and the output is exactly \\( V_{DD} \\) or 0; in the middle both conduct, both are in saturation and the gain is enormous: the output falls almost vertically. The point where the input and the output are equal is the switching threshold \\( V_M \\). With the long-channel model, equating the two saturation currents: \\[ V_M = \\frac{V_{thn} + r\\,(V_{DD} - |V_{thp}|)}{1 + r} \\] \\[ r = \\sqrt{\\frac{k_p}{k_n}} = \\sqrt{\\frac{\\mu_p\\, W_p}{\\mu_n\\, W_n}} \\] With the two thresholds equal, \\( V_M = V_{DD}/2 \\) if \\( r = 1 \\): the PMOS has to be \\( \\mu_n/\\mu_p \\) times wider, between 2 and 3 times, to compensate for the lower mobility of holes. A symmetric inverter has its threshold in the middle and both noise margins equal. Making the PMOS smaller saves area and capacitance, at the cost of shifting \\( V_M \\) downward and making the rise slower."},{"t":"Noise margins","d":"CMOS inverter","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/inversor-cmos#margenes","x":"03 Noise margins A gate has to interpret correctly an input that is somewhat contaminated. The points where the slope of the characteristic equals −1 separate the regions where noise is attenuated from the region where it is amplified; they are called \\( V_{IL} \\) and \\( V_{IH} \\): \\[ NM_L = V_{IL} - V_{OL} \\qquad NM_H = V_{OH} - V_{IH} \\] Since in CMOS \\( V_{OL} = 0 \\) and \\( V_{OH} = V_{DD} \\), the margins are large: in a symmetric inverter, close to 40 % of the supply each. A second virtue, just as important: the gain in the transition region is greater than 1, so a chain of gates regenerates the levels. A degraded signal returns to exactly 0 or \\( V_{DD} \\) after passing through a few gates. Lab · the inverter transfer curve An inverter with thresholds of 0.5 V in both transistors, \\( \\mu_n / \\mu_p = 2.5 \\) and a long-channel model with slight channel-length modulation. You choose the supply and the width ratio \\( W_p / W_n \\); the program solves for the output at each input by equating the two currents, and also plots the current flowing from the supply to ground. \\( V_{DD} \\) = \\( W_p / W_n \\) ="},{"t":"Delay","d":"CMOS inverter","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/inversor-cmos#retardo","x":"04 Delay The output of an inverter drives a capacitance: the gates that follow it, the wiring and its own drain junctions. Changing state means charging or discharging that capacitance with the current of the conducting transistor, so the delay is approximately \\[ t_p \\approx \\frac{C_L\\, V_{DD}}{2\\, I_{\\text{on}}} \\] \\( I_{\\text{on}} \\) is the current of the transistor that is on, with \\( V_{GS} = V_{DD} \\). Wider transistors lower the intrinsic delay but load the previous stage more. To drive a large load (a bus, an output pin) a chain of inverters is used, each one wider than the previous. The optimal design makes the width grow by a factor close to 4 per stage, and the number of stages grows with the logarithm of the load."},{"t":"Power","d":"CMOS inverter","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/inversor-cmos#potencia","x":"05 Power Although it consumes nothing at rest, the inverter consumes every time it switches. There are three components: \\[ P = \\alpha\\, C_L\\, V_{DD}^2\\, f + P_{\\text{sc}} + V_{DD}\\, I_{\\text{leak}} \\] \\( \\alpha \\) is the activity factor: the fraction of clock cycles in which the output changes. \\( P_{\\text{sc}} \\) is the current spike that flows through both transistors while the input passes through the transition region. The dynamic term explains the strategy of all low-power electronics: the power falls with the square of the voltage, so lowering \\( V_{DD} \\) is the most effective measure. With 1 fF per gate, 0.8 V, 3 GHz and an activity factor of 0.1, each gate consumes 0.19 µW; a hundred million gates, 19 W. With the end of Dennard scaling, leakage current became an important part of the total, and processors completely shut off the blocks they are not using."},{"t":"The inverter in real integrated circuits","d":"CMOS inverter","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/inversor-cmos#familias","x":"06 The inverter in real integrated circuits Topic What you need to know 4000 series The first CMOS family, from 3 to 18 V, slow but with minimal consumption 74HC and 74HCT Fast CMOS with the pinout of the 74 series; the HCT accepts TTL input levels Floating inputs An unconnected CMOS input sits in the transition region: both transistors conduct, the gate oscillates and heats up Latch-up The P and N regions of the inverter form a parasitic thyristor which, triggered by an overvoltage on a pin, short-circuits the supply Electrostatic discharge Each pin has diodes to the supply and to ground that protect the gates The parasitic thyristor of latch-up is the same four-layer device as in the unit on multijunction devices, only here it appears without anyone wanting it."},{"t":"In the lab","d":"CMOS inverter","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/inversor-cmos#laboratorio","x":"07 In the lab Activity 1 · Plotting the transfer curve With a 74HC04 powered at 5 V and at 3.3 V, sweep the input with a variable source and measure the output. Locate \\( V_M \\), \\( V_{IL} \\) and \\( V_{IH} \\) and compare them with those in the datasheet. Activity 2 · The short-circuit current With an ammeter in the supply of the same integrated circuit, slowly sweep the input and measure the current spike in the transition region. Repeat with the input left floating. Activity 3 · Power versus frequency Drive an inverter with a square wave from 100 kHz to 10 MHz with a 100 pF load and measure the average supply current. Verify that it grows in proportion to the frequency and estimate \\( C_L \\) from the slope."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"CMOS inverter","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/inversor-cmos#errores","x":"08 Common mistakes Making the PMOS the same width as the NMOS expecting symmetry. Holes are slower; the threshold shifts downward. Leaving inputs unconnected. The gate sits in the transition region, consumes power and can oscillate. Believing that CMOS does not consume. It does not consume at rest; while switching, the power grows with frequency and with \\( V_{DD}^2 \\). Driving a large load with a single minimum-size gate. The delay grows with the load; a chain of progressively larger inverters is used. Applying voltage to an input while the integrated circuit is unpowered. The protection diodes conduct and can trigger latch-up."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"CMOS inverter","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/inversor-cmos#autoevaluacion","x":"09 Self-assessment Why does the CMOS inverter not consume at rest? Because in either of the two states one of the two transistors is off and there is no path between the supply and ground. With \\( \\mu_n / \\mu_p = 2.5 \\) and equal thresholds, what width ratio puts \\( V_M \\) at \\( V_{DD}/2 \\)? \\( W_p / W_n = 2.5 \\), so that \\( k_p = k_n \\). By how much does the dynamic power drop if \\( V_{DD} \\) goes from 1.2 to 0.9 V? To \\( (0.9/1.2)^2 = 56 \\) %: almost half. What is latch-up? The triggering of a parasitic thyristor formed by the P and N regions of the CMOS circuit, which short-circuits the supply until it is switched off. Why does a chain of inverters regenerate logic levels? Because the gain in the transition region is much greater than 1 and outside it is smaller: intermediate levels are pushed toward 0 or toward \\( V_{DD} \\) at each stage."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"CMOS inverter","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/inversor-cmos#ampliar","x":"10 Further reading Jan M. Rabaey, Anantha Chandrakasan and Borivoje Nikolić. Digital Integrated Circuits: A Design Perspective. 2nd ed., Prentice Hall, 2003. The chapter on the CMOS inverter is the reference: transfer curve, margins, delay and power. Neil H. E. Weste and David Money Harris. CMOS VLSI Design: A Circuits and Systems Perspective. 4th ed., Addison-Wesley, 2011. Logical effort for sizing chains of gates, and the problems of real circuits. Adel S. Sedra and Kenneth C. Smith. Microelectronic Circuits. 7th ed., Oxford University Press, 2014. The inverter derived from the MOS curves, with numerical examples."},{"t":"The bridge inverter","d":"Inverters","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/inversores#puente","x":"01 The bridge inverter An inverter generates AC voltage from a DC source. The single-phase H-bridge has two legs of two switches; with the load connected diagonally, between the midpoints, it can apply \\( +V_{dc} \\), \\( -V_{dc} \\) or zero. The three-phase one has three legs, one per phase. Each switch carries an antiparallel diode, because with an inductive load the current keeps flowing when the switch opens. Between the turn-off of one switch and the turn-on of the other in the same leg, a dead time of a fraction of a microsecond is left so as not to short-circuit the supply. Switching once per half-cycle gives a square wave, with the maximum fundamental, \\( \\frac{4}{\\pi} V_{dc} \\), but with all the odd harmonics, of amplitude \\( 1/h \\), and with no way to vary the voltage."},{"t":"Sinusoidal pulse-width modulation","d":"Inverters","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/inversores#spwm","x":"02 Sinusoidal pulse-width modulation In sinusoidal PWM a sinusoidal reference at the desired frequency is compared with a much faster triangular carrier. The amplitude modulation index \\( m_a \\) is the ratio of their amplitudes and the frequency modulation index \\( m_f \\), the ratio of their frequencies. In the linear region, the fundamental of the output is proportional to \\( m_a \\): \\[ \\hat{V}_1 = m_a V_{dc}, \\quad m_a \\le 1 \\] In bipolar modulation, the output jumps between \\( +V_{dc} \\) and \\( -V_{dc} \\) and the harmonics appear around \\( m_f \\). In unipolar modulation, each leg is compared separately with the reference and with its opposite; the output passes through zero and the harmonics are grouped around \\( 2 m_f \\). With \\( m_a > 1 \\), overmodulation, the fundamental grows less than proportionally and low-order harmonics reappear. Lab · sinusoidal PWM An H-bridge fed with 400 V that generates 50 Hz. Top, the output voltage over one cycle with the scaled reference (yellow); bottom, the odd harmonics as a percentage of the fundamental, up to a little beyond \\( 2 m_f \\). Modulation Bipolar Unipolar Amplitude index m a = Frequency index m f 9 15 21 33 The voltage distortion looks enormous, but what matters is the current: an inductive load, such as a motor, attenuates each harmonic in proportion to its order, and high-order ones hardly produce any current. That is why a fast carrier is desirable, up to the limit set by the switching losses seen in power devices."},{"t":"Three-phase inverter and other techniques","d":"Inverters","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/inversores#trifasico","x":"03 Three-phase inverter and other techniques In the three-phase inverter, three references shifted by 120° are used. The harmonics that are multiples of three cancel in the line voltages, and with \\( m_f \\) a multiple of three the ones around the carrier also disappear. Space-vector modulation (SVPWM) chooses in each period the bridge states that synthesize the desired voltage vector, and makes use of 15 % more of the DC bus than sinusoidal modulation. For high voltages, multilevel inverters are used —neutral-point-clamped or cascaded H-bridge— whose output has more steps, fewer harmonics and switches that withstand only a fraction of the voltage."},{"t":"In the laboratory","d":"Inverters","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/inversores#laboratorio","x":"04 In the laboratory Activity 1 · H-bridge with a microcontroller Generate sinusoidal PWM with a microcontroller on a low-voltage H-bridge and observe the current in an RL load. Activity 2 · Spectrum With the oscilloscope FFT, measure the voltage spectrum with bipolar and unipolar modulation. Activity 3 · Dead time Increase the dead time and observe the distortion of the current near its zero crossings."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Inverters","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/inversores#errores","x":"05 Common mistakes Judging the inverter by the voltage distortion. What heats the motor is the harmonic current. Forgetting the dead time. Without it, the two switches of a leg overlap; with too much, the current is distorted. Expecting linear gain in overmodulation. The fundamental saturates toward that of the square wave. Choosing an even m f. Even harmonics appear and the waveform loses its symmetry."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Inverters","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/inversores#autoevaluacion","x":"06 Self-assessment What fundamental does a 400 V bridge give with m a = 0.8? 320 V peak, about 226 V RMS. And with a square wave? \\( \\frac{4}{\\pi} \\cdot 400 \\approx 509 \\) V peak. With m f = 21, where are the first important harmonics in unipolar modulation? Around 2 m f: at 41 and 43. What are the antiparallel diodes across the switches for? To give a path to the inductive load current when the switch opens."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Inverters","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/inversores#ampliar","x":"07 Further reading Ned Mohan, Tore M. Undeland and William P. Robbins. Power Electronics: Converters, Applications, and Design. 3rd ed., Wiley, 2003. Inverters with sinusoidal modulation, including their harmonic tables. D. Grahame Holmes and Thomas A. Lipo. Pulse Width Modulation for Power Converters: Principles and Practice. Wiley-IEEE Press, 2003. The reference on all the modulation techniques. Bin Wu and Mehdi Narimani. High-Power Converters and AC Drives. 2nd ed., Wiley-IEEE Press, 2017. Multilevel inverters and high-power drives."},{"t":"A channel that pinches off","d":"JFET: DC, small signal and switching","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/jfet#estructura","x":"01 A channel that pinches off The junction field-effect transistor (JFET) is the most direct idea for controlling a current with a voltage. An N-type silicon channel joins two terminals, the drain and the source, and on its sides there are P⁺ regions connected together that form the gate. The gate-channel junction is reverse biased, and its depletion region, which does not conduct, extends into the channel and narrows it. More reverse voltage, narrower channel, less current. Unlike the bipolar transistor, the gate does not inject anything: only the reverse current of a junction flows through it, on the order of picoamperes. The JFET is a voltage-controlled majority-carrier device with a very high input impedance. The physics of the depletion region is the same as in the PN junction: its width grows with the square root of the reverse voltage."},{"t":"Pinch-off and the Shockley equation","d":"JFET: DC, small signal and switching","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/jfet#curvas","x":"02 Pinch-off and the Shockley equation When the gate-source voltage reaches the pinch-off voltage \\( V_P \\) (datasheets call it \\( V_{GS(\\text{off})} \\)), the two depletion regions touch and the channel closes. With \\( V_{GS} = 0 \\) the maximum current flows, \\( I_{DSS} \\). Between those extremes, and with enough drain voltage, the current follows a parabola: \\[ I_D = I_{DSS} \\left( 1 - \\frac{V_{GS}}{V_P} \\right)^2 \\ \\text{if } V_{DS} \\geq V_{GS} - V_P \\] For an N channel, \\( V_P \\) is negative and \\( V_{GS} \\) ranges from \\( V_P \\) to 0. The condition on \\( V_{DS} \\) defines the saturation region, which in the JFET is the amplifying one: do not confuse it with the saturation of the bipolar transistor. With small \\( V_{DS} \\) the channel behaves as a resistor controlled by the gate (the ohmic or triode region): \\[ I_D = \\frac{I_{DSS}}{V_P^2} \\left[ 2\\,(V_{GS} - V_P)\\,V_{DS} - V_{DS}^2 \\right] \\ r_{DS} \\approx \\frac{V_P^2}{2\\, I_{DSS}\\, (V_{GS} - V_P)} \\] The resistance is at a minimum with \\( V_{GS} = 0 \\) and grows without limit as pinch-off is approached. The practical problem of the JFET is parameter spread. \\( I_{DSS} \\) and \\( V_P \\) depend on the doping and the thickness of the channel, which are poorly controlled: the 2N5457 datasheet allows \\( I_{DSS} \\) from 1 to 5 mA and \\( V_{GS(\\text{off})} \\) from −0.5 to −6 V. Two transistors from the same bag can be very different."},{"t":"Biasing","d":"JFET: DC, small signal and switching","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/jfet#polarizacion","x":"03 Biasing Since the gate is reverse biased, the simplest circuit is self-bias: the gate goes to ground through a large resistor and a resistor \\( R_S \\) is placed in the source. The drain current lifts the source, and the gate ends up negative with respect to it: \\[ V_{GS} = -I_D R_S \\ I_D = I_{DSS} \\left( 1 - \\frac{I_D R_S}{\\left| V_P \\right|} \\right)^2 \\] With \\( I_{DSS} = 4 \\) mA, \\( V_P = -2 \\) V and \\( R_S = 500 \\) Ω, the quadratic gives \\( I_D = 1.53 \\) mA and \\( V_{GS} = -0.76 \\) V. The source resistor provides feedback: if the current rises, \\( V_{GS} \\) becomes more negative and holds it back. But with the spread of the 2N5457 it is not enough, as the lab shows. The design solution is a divider at the gate and a large \\( R_S \\), so that the current is set by \\( (V_G - V_{GS})/R_S \\) with \\( V_G \\) much larger than the variation of \\( V_{GS} \\); or simply selecting the transistors, which is what is done in audio preamplifiers. Lab · self-bias with a real JFET The transistor's transfer curve \\( I_D(V_{GS}) \\) and the bias line \\( V_{GS} = -I_D R_S \\): the operating point is where they cross. You choose \\( I_{DSS} \\), \\( V_P \\), the resistors and the supply. With the spread option, the two extreme samples allowed by the 2N5457 datasheet are overlaid in the same circuit. \\( I_{DSS} \\) = \\( V_P \\) = \\( R_S \\) = \\( R_D \\) = a single transistor overlay the 2N5457 extremes"},{"t":"Small-signal model","d":"JFET: DC, small signal and switching","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/jfet#senal","x":"04 Small-signal model In saturation, the JFET is a current source controlled by \\( v_{gs} \\), with a transconductance that comes from differentiating the parabola: \\[ g_m = \\frac{2\\, I_{DSS}}{\\left| V_P \\right|} \\left( 1 - \\frac{V_{GS}}{V_P} \\right) = \\frac{2 \\sqrt{I_{DSS}\\, I_D}}{\\left| V_P \\right|} \\ r_o \\approx \\frac{1}{\\lambda\\, I_D} \\] In the previous example, \\( g_m = 2.47 \\) mS. \\( \\lambda \\) models the slope of the curves in saturation, the equivalent of the Early effect. The comparison with the bipolar transistor is instructive: at 1.5 mA a bipolar has \\( g_m = 58 \\) mS, more than twenty times more. The JFET amplifies less per milliampere, but in exchange its input draws no current and it has very little noise when the signal source has a high impedance."},{"t":"The JFET as an analog switch","d":"JFET: DC, small signal and switching","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/jfet#conmutacion","x":"05 The JFET as an analog switch In the ohmic region and with \\( V_{GS} = 0 \\), the channel is a small resistance that lets the signal through in both directions; with \\( V_{GS} \\) beyond \\( V_P \\), the channel opens up and the resistance is in the gigaohms. That makes it an analog switch: the J111 is specified with \\( r_{DS(\\text{on})} \\leq 30 \\) Ω. It was widely used in analog multiplexers, samplers and automatic gain control, where the channel works as a voltage-controlled variable resistor. Today those functions are mostly performed by integrated CMOS switches."},{"t":"Where it appears in electronics","d":"JFET: DC, small signal and switching","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/jfet#electronica","x":"06 Where it appears in electronics Case Why a JFET Electret microphone The capsule has a JFET inside that matches the very high impedance of the charged diaphragm Op amps with JFET inputs (TL072) Bias currents of tens of picoamperes, versus nanoamperes for a bipolar Preamplifiers for piezoelectric sensors and photodiodes Low noise with high-impedance sources Two-terminal current sources A JFET with its gate tied to the source conducts \\( I_{DSS} \\) at almost any voltage: the “constant-current diode” Silicon carbide power devices SiC JFETs for switching hundreds of volts with low losses"},{"t":"In the lab","d":"JFET: DC, small signal and switching","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/jfet#laboratorio","x":"07 In the lab Activity 1 · Measuring \\( I_{DSS} \\) and \\( V_P \\) With a 2N5457, measure the drain current with the gate tied to the source and 10 V on the drain: that is \\( I_{DSS} \\). Then lower \\( V_{GS} \\) until the current drops to 10 µA: this is a good estimate of \\( V_{GS(\\text{off})} \\). Compare three or four transistors from the same bag. Activity 2 · The transfer curve Measure \\( I_D \\) for five values of \\( V_{GS} \\) and fit the Shockley parabola. Calculate \\( g_m \\) at two points and verify that it grows with the square root of the current. Activity 3 · A voltage-controlled resistor With \\( V_{DS} \\) of a few hundred millivolts, measure the channel resistance for several values of \\( V_{GS} \\) and use it in a divider to build a voltage-controlled attenuator."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"JFET: DC, small signal and switching","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/jfet#errores","x":"08 Common mistakes Forward biasing the gate. The junction conducts and the high input impedance is lost. Designing with the typical values of \\( I_{DSS} \\) and \\( V_P \\). The spread is enormous; the circuit has to be checked with the extremes. Confusing the saturation of the JFET with that of the bipolar. In the JFET, saturation is the amplifying region. Forgetting the condition \\( V_{DS} \\geq V_{GS} - V_P \\). If it is not met, the transistor is in the ohmic region and the Shockley equation does not apply. Using the wrong sign of \\( V_P \\). In an N channel it is negative; in a P channel, positive."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"JFET: DC, small signal and switching","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/jfet#autoevaluacion","x":"09 Self-assessment Why is the gate current of a JFET so small? Because the gate-channel junction is reverse biased and only its leakage current flows, on the order of picoamperes. With \\( I_{DSS} = 8 \\) mA and \\( V_P = -4 \\) V, what is \\( I_D \\) with \\( V_{GS} = -2 \\) V? \\( 8 \\times (1 - 0.5)^2 = 2 \\) mA. And the transconductance at that point? \\( g_m = \\dfrac{2 \\times 8}{4} \\times 0.5 = 2 \\) mS. What source resistor self-biases that point? \\( R_S = 2 \\text{ V} / 2 \\text{ mA} = 1 \\) kΩ. Why is a JFET used inside an electret microphone? Because the capsule has a very high impedance and the JFET, whose gate draws no current, matches it to a low-impedance output."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"JFET: DC, small signal and switching","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/jfet#ampliar","x":"10 Further reading Paul Horowitz and Winfield Hill. The Art of Electronics. 3rd ed., Cambridge University Press, 2015. The chapter on field-effect transistors, with the measured spread of JFETs and the circuits that tolerate it. Adel S. Sedra and Kenneth C. Smith. Microelectronic Circuits. 7th ed., Oxford University Press, 2014. The JFET in the same framework as the MOSFET, with biasing and the small-signal model. Ben G. Streetman and Sanjay Kumar Banerjee. Solid State Electronic Devices. 7th ed., Pearson, 2015. The derivation of the JFET curves from the width of the depletion region."},{"t":"The junction at equilibrium","d":"PN junction and diodes","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/juntura-pn#equilibrio","x":"01 The junction at equilibrium When a P region is joined to an N region, holes from the P side diffuse into the N side and electrons from the N side into the P side, following the concentration gradient described in the previous unit. Each carrier that crosses leaves behind an uncompensated fixed ion: negative acceptors on the P side, positive donors on the N side. That fixed charge creates an electric field that opposes diffusion, and the process stops when the drift current produced by the field exactly equals the diffusion current. What remains is a strip almost empty of carriers, the depletion region or space-charge region, and between its edges a potential difference, the built-in potential: \\[ V_{bi} = V_T \\ln \\frac{N_A\\, N_D}{n_i^2} \\] With \\( N_A = 10^{17} \\), \\( N_D = 10^{16} \\) cm⁻³ and \\( n_i = 10^{10} \\) cm⁻³, \\( V_{bi} \\approx 0.774 \\) V. It cannot be measured with a voltmeter: the contact potentials at the metal terminals exactly compensate it. The Electronics Technician program page on semiconductors and diodes tells the same story without equations; here it is quantified."},{"t":"The width of the depletion region","d":"PN junction and diodes","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/juntura-pn#agotamiento","x":"02 The width of the depletion region Solving Poisson's equation with uniform charge on each side (the depletion approximation), the field is triangular, with its maximum at the junction, and the potential grows as two parabolas. The total charge on each side has to be the same, so the region extends further into the less doped side: \\[ W = \\sqrt{\\frac{2\\,\\varepsilon_s\\,(V_{bi} - V)}{q} \\left( \\frac{1}{N_A} + \\frac{1}{N_D} \\right)} \\] \\[ N_A\\, x_p = N_D\\, x_n \\qquad E_{\\max} = \\frac{2\\,(V_{bi} - V)}{W} \\] \\( V \\) is the applied voltage, positive in forward bias. \\( \\varepsilon_s = 11.7\\,\\varepsilon_0 \\) in silicon. With the doping levels of the example and no applied voltage, \\( W \\approx 0.33 \\) µm, of which 0.30 µm lie on the N side. With 10 V in reverse, \\( W \\) grows to 1.24 µm and the maximum field reaches \\( 1.7 \\times 10^5 \\) V/cm. Almost all real junctions are asymmetric, with one side much more heavily doped than the other: the depletion region lies entirely in the lightly doped side, and its doping sets the breakdown voltage and the capacitance. Lab · inside the junction You choose the two doping levels and the applied voltage. The three plots show, on the same axis, the fixed charge, the electric field and the potential along the junction; the horizontal scale adjusts itself. With a large reverse bias you can see when the field approaches the avalanche breakdown value. P-side doping, \\( N_A \\) = N-side doping, \\( N_D \\) = Applied voltage \\( V \\) ="},{"t":"The diode equation","d":"PN junction and diodes","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/juntura-pn#ecuacion","x":"03 The diode equation A forward voltage lowers the barrier, and diffusion wins out over drift: minority carriers are injected on each side. At the edge of the depletion region the minority-carrier concentration rises by the factor \\( e^{V/V_T} \\), and from there it decays with the diffusion length, exactly the exponential profile of the continuity equation. The current is the diffusion of that excess, and adding the two sides gives the Shockley equation: \\[ I = I_S \\left( e^{V / n V_T} - 1 \\right) \\] \\[ I_S = q\\,A\\,n_i^2 \\left( \\frac{D_p}{L_p\\,N_D} + \\frac{D_n}{L_n\\,N_A} \\right) \\] \\( n \\) is the ideality factor: 1 when diffusion dominates, close to 2 when recombination inside the depletion region matters, at low currents. With the previous example, \\( \\tau = 1 \\) µs on both sides and an area of 1 mm², \\( I_S \\approx 6 \\times 10^{-14} \\) A: the diode conducts 1 µA at 0.43 V, 1 mA at 0.61 V and 100 mA at 0.73 V. Each decade of current costs \\( V_T \\ln 10 \\approx 60 \\) mV, which is why the “0.6 V” of the Electronics Technician program is such a good approximation over such a wide range. 89 % of that current is carried by holes injected into the N side: the current is dominated by the less doped side, something the bipolar transistor exploits so that the emitter injects almost only electrons. Temperature \\( I_S \\) is proportional to \\( n_i^2 \\), which grows very fast with temperature: at constant current, the diode voltage drops about 2 mV per degree. This is the basis of integr"},{"t":"The two capacitances of the diode","d":"PN junction and diodes","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/juntura-pn#capacidades","x":"04 The two capacitances of the diode The junction stores charge in two different ways, and each one dominates in one bias condition: Junction capacitance Diffusion capacitance Origin The fixed charge of the depletion region, which changes with its width The excess of injected minority carriers, proportional to the current Expression \\( C_j = \\dfrac{\\varepsilon_s A}{W} = \\dfrac{C_{j0}}{\\sqrt{1 - V/V_{bi}}} \\) \\( C_d = \\dfrac{\\tau_T\\, I}{V_T} \\) Dominates in Reverse bias Forward bias Put to use in Varicap diode: electronic tuning Limits the switching speed The exponent \\( 1/2 \\) is that of an abrupt junction; in a graded junction it is \\( 1/3 \\), and hyperabrupt varactors use special doping profiles so that the capacitance changes more with voltage."},{"t":"Reverse breakdown","d":"PN junction and diodes","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/juntura-pn#ruptura","x":"05 Reverse breakdown In reverse bias only about \\( I_S \\) flows, until the maximum field reaches a critical value, on the order of \\( 3 \\) to \\( 5 \\times 10^5 \\) V/cm in silicon. There are two mechanisms: Avalanche: carriers gain enough energy from the field to knock loose new pairs when they collide, and the multiplication runs away. It dominates in lightly doped junctions, with breakdown above about 7 V, and its voltage rises with temperature. Zener effect: with very high doping the depletion region is so thin that electrons cross it by tunneling. It dominates below about 5 V and its voltage falls with temperature. Between 5 and 6 V the two coefficients cancel: that is why the reference diodes with the best stability sit in that range. In an asymmetric junction, the avalanche breakdown voltage is set by the doping of the lightly doped side: \\[ BV \\approx \\frac{\\varepsilon_s\\, E_c^2}{2\\, q\\, N} \\] With \\( N = 10^{16} \\) cm⁻³ and \\( E_c = 4 \\times 10^5 \\) V/cm, \\( BV \\approx 52 \\) V. For a power diode to withstand 1000 V, its lightly doped region has to be doped close to \\( 10^{14} \\) cm⁻³ and be tens of microns thick."},{"t":"Switching and reverse recovery","d":"PN junction and diodes","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/juntura-pn#conmutacion","x":"06 Switching and reverse recovery A forward-biased diode has the charge of the injected minority carriers stored in it. If the voltage is suddenly reversed, the diode keeps conducting in reverse until that charge is removed: first with a reverse current limited by the circuit during the storage time, and then decaying as the depletion region re-forms: \\[ t_s \\approx \\tau \\ln \\left( 1 + \\frac{I_F}{I_R} \\right) \\] With \\( \\tau = 1 \\) µs and \\( I_R = I_F \\), \\( t_s \\approx 0.69 \\) µs. That is why ordinary rectifier diodes are not suitable at high frequency. Diode Recovery Use Ordinary rectifier (1N400x) Microseconds 50 or 60 Hz Signal (1N4148) 4 ns maximum Logic, detectors, protection Fast and ultrafast Tens of nanoseconds Switching power supplies Schottky (metal-semiconductor) Almost none: they conduct with majority carriers, so there are no stored minority carriers Low-voltage switching power supplies, with lower forward drop"},{"t":"Models for circuit analysis","d":"PN junction and diodes","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/juntura-pn#modelos","x":"07 Models for circuit analysis Model What it keeps When it is enough Ideal Only the direction of conduction Circuit voltages much larger than 0.7 V Constant drop A 0.6 to 0.7 V source in forward bias Everyday bias analysis Piecewise linear The drop plus a series resistance Large currents, where the resistance of the material matters Exponential The Shockley equation Precision design, simulation Small signal The slope at the operating point: \\( r_d = n V_T / I_D \\) Small variations around the bias point The small-signal resistance is remarkable: at 1 mA it is 25.85 Ω, whatever the diode, because it depends only on the current and the temperature. The same idea gives the transconductance of the bipolar transistor in the next unit."},{"t":"In the lab","d":"PN junction and diodes","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/juntura-pn#laboratorio","x":"08 In the lab Activity 1 · Sixty millivolts per decade Measure the voltage of a 1N4148 at currents of 1 µA, 10 µA, 100 µA, 1 mA and 10 mA. Plot \\( V \\) against \\( \\log I \\), obtain the slope and deduce the ideality factor \\( n \\). Activity 2 · The capacitance of a varicap Use an LCR meter to measure the capacitance of a varicap diode (or of a reverse-biased 1N4007) between 0 and 20 V. Plot \\( 1/C^2 \\) against \\( V \\): the line crosses the axis at \\( V_{bi} \\) and its slope gives the doping of the lightly doped side. Activity 3 · Reverse recovery Drive a 1N4007 and a 1N4148 with a ±5 V square wave at 100 kHz through a 1 kΩ resistor, and use the oscilloscope to measure the reverse-current pulse across the resistor in each one."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"PN junction and diodes","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/juntura-pn#errores","x":"09 Common mistakes Believing that the built-in potential can be measured. The metal contacts compensate it. Assuming that the depletion region is split equally. It extends into the less doped side. Treating the 0.7 V drop as a constant of silicon. It depends on the current, the area and the temperature. Forgetting reverse recovery in a switching power supply. The slow diode conducts in reverse on every cycle and heats the transistor. Confusing the two capacitances. The junction capacitance is the reverse-bias one; the diffusion capacitance, the forward-bias one."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"PN junction and diodes","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/juntura-pn#autoevaluacion","x":"10 Self-assessment What equilibrium is reached in a junction with no applied voltage? The diffusion current of each carrier equals the drift current produced by the field of the depletion region, and the net current is zero. In a P⁺N junction, on which side does almost the entire depletion region lie? On the N side, the less doped one, because the charge on each side has to be equal. How much does the voltage of a diode increase if the current is multiplied by one hundred? \\( V_T \\ln 100 \\approx 119 \\) mV, with \\( n = 1 \\). Why does a Schottky diode switch faster than a junction diode? Because it conducts with majority carriers and does not accumulate minority-carrier charge that has to be removed when the voltage is reversed. How does the capacitance of an abrupt junction change when \\( V_{bi} - V \\) is multiplied by four? It is halved, because the width \\( W \\) grows with the square root."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"PN junction and diodes","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/juntura-pn#ampliar","x":"11 Further reading Robert F. Pierret. Semiconductor Device Fundamentals. Addison-Wesley, 1996. The electrostatics of the junction and the derivation of the diode equation, step by step. Ben G. Streetman and Sanjay Kumar Banerjee. Solid State Electronic Devices. 7th ed., Pearson, 2015. Very clear on switching, reverse recovery and the breakdown mechanisms. Richard S. Muller, Theodore I. Kamins and Mansun Chan. Device Electronics for Integrated Circuits. 3rd ed., Wiley, 2003. The junction seen from the integrated circuit, with the models that simulators use."},{"t":"What for, if we already have Fourier","d":"The Laplace transform","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/laplace#porque","x":"01 What for, if we already have Fourier The Fourier transform requires the signal to be absolutely integrable, and that leaves out such common things as the step or a growing exponential. Laplace’s solution is to multiply by a convergence factor \\( e^{-\\sigma t} \\) before transforming: \\[ X(s) = \\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} x(t)\\,e^{-st}\\,dt, \\qquad s = \\sigma + j\\omega \\] With \\( \\sigma = 0 \\) you get exactly the Fourier transform. Laplace contains it as a particular case. That \\( \\sigma \\) is what does the work: if the signal grows, you choose a \\( \\sigma \\) large enough for the product to decay and the integral to converge. The price is that now you have to state for which values of \\( \\sigma \\) it converges, and that is the region of convergence. What Laplace adds Why it matters Transforms growing signals Makes it possible to analyze unstable systems Handles initial conditions A charged capacitor enters the calculation Turns the differential equation into an algebraic one Solving circuits is just solving for the unknown Gives poles and zeros The whole reading of stability and response"},{"t":"The region of convergence, which is not a detail","d":"The Laplace transform","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/laplace#roc","x":"02 The region of convergence, which is not a detail Here is what is hardest about Laplace and what gets skipped most: \\( X(s) \\) alone does not determine the signal. The region of convergence is also needed, and with the same algebraic expression and different regions you get completely different signals. Lab · the same X(s), three signals On the left the \\( s \\)-plane with the poles and the region of convergence shaded; on the right, the corresponding signal in time. Change the region and watch how the signal changes without a single pole moving. X(s) = 1 / ((s+1)(s−2)) X(s) = 1 / (s+1) X(s) = 1 / (s−1) region to the right of all the poles the strip in the middle region to the left of all of them It pays to keep the three rules that summarize all this together: If the region… Then the signal… Is to the right of all the poles Is causal: it is zero before \\( t = 0 \\) Is to the left of all of them Is anticausal: it is zero after \\( t = 0 \\) Is a strip between poles Is two-sided: it extends to both sides Contains the \\( j\\omega \\) axis Has a Fourier transform, and the system is stable The last row is the connection between the two transforms, and also the stability criterion: a causal system is stable if and only if all of its poles are to the left of the imaginary axis, because only then does the causal region —which starts at the rightmost pole— manage to cover it."},{"t":"The one-sided transform, which is the one used in circuits","d":"The Laplace transform","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/laplace#unilateral","x":"03 The one-sided transform, which is the one used in circuits For problems with initial conditions, the version that integrates from zero is used: \\[ X(s) = \\int_{0^{-}}^{\\infty} x(t)\\,e^{-st}\\,dt \\] Its appeal lies in how it transforms the derivative, because that is where the past of the circuit enters: \\[ \\mathcal{L}\\left\\{\\frac{dx}{dt}\\right\\} = s\\,X(s) - x(0^{-}), \\qquad \\mathcal{L}\\left\\{\\int_{0}^{t} x\\,d\\tau\\right\\} = \\frac{X(s)}{s} \\] The \\( x(0^-) \\) is the initial condition. That is why a charged capacitor or an inductor carrying current appear in the calculation without adding anything by hand. With that, a circuit is solved as if it were resistive, using generalized impedances: Element Impedance The initial condition appears as Resistor \\( R \\) It has none Capacitor \\( 1/(sC) \\) A voltage source \\( v_C(0)/s \\) in series Inductor \\( sL \\) A voltage source \\( L\\,i_L(0) \\) in series The initial and final value theorems Two shortcuts that avoid inverse transforming when only the endpoints matter: \\( x(0^+) = \\lim_{s\\to\\infty} sX(s) \\) and \\( x(\\infty) = \\lim_{s\\to 0} sX(s) \\). The second has a condition that is often forgotten: it holds only if the system is stable. Applied to something that oscillates or grows, it gives a number that means nothing."},{"t":"Back to the time domain","d":"The Laplace transform","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/laplace#antitransformar","x":"04 Back to the time domain For rational functions, which is what comes out of any circuit, the way is partial fractions: you factor the denominator, split into terms of one pole each, and inverse transform each term using the table. And we have already seen that the coefficients of those fractions are the residues, so the two techniques are the same. \\( X(s) \\) \\( x(t) \\) for \\( t \\ge 0 \\) \\( 1/s \\) \\( 1 \\), the step \\( 1/s^2 \\) \\( t \\), the ramp \\( 1/(s+a) \\) \\( e^{-at} \\) \\( 1/(s+a)^2 \\) \\( t\\,e^{-at} \\) \\( \\omega/(s^2+\\omega^2) \\) \\( \\operatorname{sin}\\omega t \\) \\( \\dfrac{\\omega}{(s+a)^2+\\omega^2} \\) \\( e^{-at}\\operatorname{sin}\\omega t \\) \\( e^{-sT}X(s) \\) \\( x(t-T) \\), the delayed signal The fourth row explains where the \\( t\\,e^{-at} \\) of a double pole comes from, and the last one is the one that makes it possible to handle delays: an \\( e^{-sT} \\) in the transfer function is a dead time, and it is what makes a system with transport delay non-rational."},{"t":"In the lab","d":"The Laplace transform","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/laplace#laboratorio","x":"05 In the lab Activity 1 · Solving an RLC with initial conditions Set up a series RLC with the capacitor charged to 5 V, write the transformed circuit with the initial-condition sources, solve for \\( V_C(s) \\) and inverse transform. Compare with the measurement on the oscilloscope. Activity 2 · Initial and final value For \\( V(s) = \\dfrac{10(s+2)}{s(s^2+3s+2)} \\), apply the two theorems and then inverse transform to verify. Repeat with a denominator that has a pole in the right half-plane and explain why the final value theorem fails. Activity 3 · The region matters For \\( X(s) = \\dfrac{1}{(s+1)(s-2)} \\), write the three possible signals according to the region of convergence and decide which of the three has a Fourier transform."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"The Laplace transform","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/laplace#errores","x":"06 Common mistakes Giving \\( X(s) \\) without the region of convergence. The expression alone does not determine the signal. Forgetting the initial condition when transforming a derivative in the one-sided version. Applying the final value theorem to an unstable system. It returns a meaningless number. Confusing a pole with a zero of the transformed denominator. It is best to always factor. Believing that Fourier and Laplace are different things. Fourier is Laplace on the imaginary axis, when that axis is inside the region."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"The Laplace transform","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/laplace#autoevaluacion","x":"07 Self-assessment What does Laplace add compared with Fourier? The factor \\( e^{-\\sigma t} \\), which makes it possible to transform growing signals and to handle initial conditions. Why is the region of convergence needed? Because the same \\( X(s) \\) with different regions corresponds to different signals. When does the Fourier transform exist? When the region of convergence contains the imaginary axis. For a causal system, that is equivalent to its being stable. How does a charged capacitor enter the calculation? As an impedance \\( 1/(sC) \\) in series with a source \\( v_C(0)/s \\). What is \\( \\mathcal{L}^{-1}\\{1/(s+a)^2\\} \\)? \\( t\\,e^{-at} \\). That is the term contributed by a double pole. What does an \\( e^{-sT} \\) represent in a transfer function? A pure delay of \\( T \\) seconds. It is not rational, and that is why it is approximated with a Padé approximant."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"The Laplace transform","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/laplace#ampliar","x":"08 Further reading Alan V. Oppenheim and Alan S. Willsky. Signals and Systems. 2nd ed. (Spanish edition, “Señales y sistemas”), Prentice Hall, 1997. Chapter 9, with the careful treatment of the region of convergence that most books omit. Charles K. Alexander and Matthew N. O. Sadiku. Fundamentals of Electric Circuits. 7th ed. (Spanish edition, “Fundamentos de circuitos eléctricos”), McGraw-Hill, 2021. Laplace applied to circuits, with the initial-condition sources and many examples. Katsuhiko Ogata. Modern Control Engineering. 5th ed. (Spanish edition, “Ingeniería de control moderna”), Prentice Hall, 2010. The table of transforms and the initial and final value theorems, with their conditions of validity."},{"t":"Why an engineer studies law","d":"Legislation: the law around engineering","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/legislacion-introduccion#por-que","x":"01 Why an engineer studies law Almost everything an engineer does at work has a legal side, even when it does not look like it. Accepting a quotation means entering into a contract. Signing the drawing of an installation means taking on a responsibility that can last for years. Hiring a technician means entering into an employment relationship with rules of its own. Testifying in a trial as an expert witness means acting as an auxiliary of the court. And setting up a company with a partner means choosing, knowingly or not, how much of your personal assets is exposed. This subject does not train lawyers. It trains engineers who know when a problem has a legal dimension, what questions to ask a lawyer and what mistakes to avoid before it becomes necessary to call one. The Engineering and Society page on the profession and professional ethics introduced the topic at the first level; here it is developed with the specific rules. Educational content, not legal advice These pages explain the general framework and cite the rules by their number. Laws change —labor law went through major reforms in 2024— and every real case has details that change the answer. For a specific situation, the current text of the law and a licensed lawyer have the final word."},{"t":"Legal rules and technical standards","d":"Legislation: the law around engineering","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/legislacion-introduccion#normas","x":"02 Legal rules and technical standards Every day an engineer works with two kinds of rules that use the same word (“norma” in Spanish) and are very different things: Legal rule Technical standard Examples A statute, a decree, a municipal ordinance An IRAM, IEC or ISO standard; the AEA (Argentine Electrotechnical Association) wiring regulations for electrical installations Who issues it A state body with the competence to do so A standards body, by consensus among manufacturers, users and specialists Whether it is mandatory Binds everyone from its official publication It is voluntary, unless a legal rule makes it mandatory If it is not complied with A penalty is provided for There is no penalty of its own; but departing from it can show that due diligence was not exercised The last row is the one that matters most in practice. Even if a technical standard is not mandatory, in a trial over an accident it is used as the measure of what a prudent professional would have done. Following it protects; departing from it without a documented reason exposes you."},{"t":"Where law comes from","d":"Legislation: the law around engineering","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/legislacion-introduccion#fuentes","x":"03 Where law comes from The sources of law are the places from which the rules that a judge applies arise: Statute law, in the broad sense: any general rule issued by the competent authority, from the Constitution down to a ministry resolution. In the Argentine system it is the main source. Custom: repeated practices that people consider binding. The Civil and Commercial Code (Código Civil y Comercial, CCyC) admits it when a statute refers to it or in situations that the statute does not regulate. Case law (“jurisprudencia”): the way the courts decide cases. In Argentina one ruling does not bind other judges, but those of the Supreme Court strongly guide all of them. Legal scholarship (“doctrina”): what jurists write. It is not binding, but it helps with interpretation. Article 8 of the Civil and Commercial Code lays down a principle worth keeping in mind: ignorance of the law is no excuse for failing to comply with it. No one can claim not to have known of a published rule."},{"t":"Where to look up the rules","d":"Legislation: the law around engineering","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/legislacion-introduccion#consultar","x":"04 Where to look up the rules Source What it has Boletín Oficial de la República Argentina (the Official Gazette) The official publication: a national rule exists from the moment it appears there InfoLEG National rules with their updated text and the rules that amended them SAIJ (Argentine Legal Information System) National and provincial legislation, case law and legal scholarship Provincial official gazettes and municipal compilations of ordinances Local rules: provincial professional-practice laws, building codes Professional councils and associations The professional-practice laws, codes of ethics and fee schedules of their jurisdiction A law cited from memory or copied from some random site may be out of date. The right habit is to look up the number of the rule in InfoLEG or SAIJ and read the updated text, which incorporates later amendments."},{"t":"The course roadmap","d":"Legislation: the law around engineering","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/legislacion-introduccion#mapa","x":"05 The course roadmap Block Contents The question it answers Foundations Public and private law, the Constitution, the legal system Which rule applies, and which one prevails over which? Economic activity Companies, contracts, labor law How do you organize, agree and employ? The profession Professional practice, ethics, rights and duties Who may sign, and what is committed by doing so? The consequences Expert evidence and arbitration, liability What happens when something goes wrong? The work Construction law How is a public or private construction project contracted and carried out?"},{"t":"Where it shows up in electronics","d":"Legislation: the law around engineering","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/legislacion-introduccion#electronica","x":"06 Where it shows up in electronics Situation The legal issues it brings Designing a medical device Registration with ANMAT (the national drug, food and medical device regulator), liability for defective products Installing a network in a building Construction contract, technical regulations, municipal permit Type-approving a transmitter ENACOM (the national communications regulator) rules on the radio spectrum Developing custom software Intellectual property, confidentiality, warranty Hiring a technician for the workshop Employment Contract Act (Ley de Contrato de Trabajo), collective bargaining agreement, occupational risk insurance"},{"t":"Activities","d":"Legislation: the law around engineering","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/legislacion-introduccion#laboratorio","x":"07 Activities Activity 1 · Look up a rule Look up Law 19,587 on occupational health and safety in InfoLEG. Note its date, which decree regulates it and which later rules amended it. Activity 2 · Technical standard versus legal rule Find out which legal rule makes the AEA wiring regulations for electrical installations in buildings mandatory in the jurisdiction where you live. Activity 3 · The legal side of a project Choose a real or imaginary electronics project and list all the acts with legal consequences that it involves, from the first quotation to after-sales service."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Legislation: the law around engineering","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/legislacion-introduccion#errores","x":"08 Common mistakes Believing that law is a matter for lawyers. The decisions that create legal problems are made by the engineer, long before a lawyer gets involved. Treating a technical standard as simply optional. It is voluntary, but it is the yardstick used to measure diligence. Citing a law without checking its current text. Many have been amended several times. Thinking that a verbal agreement is not binding. Most contracts do not require written form; the difficulty is proving them."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Legislation: the law around engineering","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/legislacion-introduccion#autoevaluacion","x":"09 Self-assessment What is the difference between an IRAM standard and a statute as regards whether they are mandatory? A statute binds from its official publication; an IRAM standard is voluntary unless a legal rule makes it mandatory. What are the sources of law? Statute law, custom, case law and legal scholarship; in the Argentine system, statute law is the main one. Can you claim that you did not know about a published law? No: under Article 8 of the Civil and Commercial Code, ignorance of the law is no excuse. Where do you look up the updated text of a national law? In InfoLEG or SAIJ, which incorporate later amendments."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Legislation: the law around engineering","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/legislacion-introduccion#ampliar","x":"10 Further reading Julio César Rivera and Graciela Medina. Derecho civil. Parte general. Abeledo Perrot (in Spanish). The sources of law, persons and legal acts according to the Civil and Commercial Code. Código Civil y Comercial de la Nación (Law 26,994). Título preliminar. Official text in InfoLEG (in Spanish). The first eighteen articles: sources, interpretation, entry into force and the principle of good faith. Consejo Profesional de Ingeniería de Telecomunicaciones, Electrónica y Computación (COPITEC), the Professional Council of Telecommunications, Electronics and Computer Engineering. Normas de ejercicio y código de ética. Council publications (in Spanish). The framework governing the licensing of electronic engineers at the national level."},{"t":"Public works","d":"Legislation on works","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/legislacion-obras#obra-publica","x":"01 Public works Law 13,064, of 1947, defines national public works as any construction, work or industrial service carried out with funds of the National Treasury. It remains in force, with its implementing regulations, and each province has its own law with a similar structure (in the province of Buenos Aires, Law 6021). A telecommunications network for a public hospital, a road signaling system or the electrical installation of a school are public works. What distinguishes it from a private construction project is that the State cannot freely choose whom to contract with or negotiate the terms: it has to follow a procedure that guarantees equality among bidders, publicity and the best offer for the public interest."},{"t":"How it is contracted","d":"Legislation on works","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/legislacion-obras#contratacion","x":"02 How it is contracted The rule is the public tender (licitación pública); the private tender and direct award are exceptions that the law lists, such as low amounts or emergencies. The typical sequence: Design and tender specifications: the agency prepares the design, the official budget and the specifications —general conditions, particular conditions and technical specifications—. Call and publicity: it is published with the advance notice set by the rules. Bids: bidders submit their proposal with a bid bond; the bids are opened in a public ceremony. Evaluation and award: the contract is awarded to the most advantageous bid, which is not always the cheapest. Contract: the awardee posts a performance bond and signs. Contracting system How it is paid When it is suitable Lump sum (ajuste alzado) A global price for the whole work Complete and well-defined design Unit price (unidad de medida) Unit prices for the quantities actually executed Uncertain quantities: meters of conduit, network points Cost plus (coste y costas) The actual cost plus a percentage Only exceptionally, for urgency"},{"t":"Execution: who is who","d":"Legislation on works","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/legislacion-obras#ejecucion","x":"03 Execution: who is who Role Represents What it does Site inspection (inspección de obra) The client (the State) Checks that the design and the contract are complied with, approves the work and issues service orders Technical representative The contracting company A licensed professional who answers technically for the execution and signs the request notes Designer Whoever commissioned the design Answers for the design and its calculations Formal communication is done in writing, in two books: the book of service orders (órdenes de servicio), from the inspection to the contractor, and the book of request notes (notas de pedido), from the contractor to the inspection. What was not recorded in those books is very hard to claim afterward. The work is paid for with periodic payment certificates based on the measured progress, from which a percentage is withheld as a retention fund (fondo de reparo) until acceptance. The work ends with two acts: provisional acceptance, when it is completed, which opens a warranty period in which the contractor corrects any defects that appear, and final acceptance, when that period expires, which releases the bonds and the retention fund. In long contracts with inflation, prices are updated under a regime of price redetermination, which at the national level is regulated by Decree 691/2016."},{"t":"Private construction","d":"Legislation on works","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/legislacion-obras#privada","x":"04 Private construction In private construction, the work contract of the Civil and Commercial Code applies, and the parties negotiate the terms. But the State is still present through the municipal police power: the building code requires a building permit with drawings signed by licensed professionals, inspections during construction and a final inspection in order to authorize occupancy. The roles are similar to those in public works: the construction supervisor represents the client, the builder executes and their technical representative answers for them."},{"t":"Standards for installations","d":"Legislation on works","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/legislacion-obras#instalaciones","x":"05 Standards for installations Topic Reference standards Electrical installations in buildings AEA regulations (90364 series), mandatory where a legal rule adopts them Safety on the construction site Law 19,587 and Decree 911/96 for construction Structured cabling TIA-568 and ISO/IEC 11801 standards, with channel certification Antennas and radio systems ENACOM rules and, for the structures, municipal codes Fire detection and alarm IRAM standards and the requirements of the building code and of the local fire department"},{"t":"Where it shows up in electronics","d":"Legislation on works","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/legislacion-obras#electronica","x":"06 Where it shows up in electronics Situation What you need to know Quoting the installation of a municipality’s cameras Read the three sets of specifications, the bid bond and the contracting system Being the technical representative of an installation company Signing the request notes and answering for the execution A change requested by the inspection during the work Require it by service order and record its cost by request note Installing an alarm system in a private building Work contract, regulations and, where applicable, a municipal permit"},{"t":"Activities","d":"Legislation on works","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/legislacion-obras#laboratorio","x":"07 Activities Activity 1 · Read a real tender specification Find on the national or a provincial public procurement portal a tender for an electronic installation, and identify the contracting system, the bonds, the deadlines and the technical requirements. Activity 2 · A service order Write a service order requesting a change in the location of a rack, and the request note with which the contractor responds, indicating the additional cost. Activity 3 · The municipal permit Find out what the local building code requires to authorize an antenna on the roof of a building."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Legislation on works","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/legislacion-obras#errores","x":"08 Common mistakes Bidding without reading the complete specification. What was not priced but the specification required is built anyway. Accepting verbal changes from the inspection. Without a service order, the additional cost is hard to collect. Confusing provisional and final acceptance. The bond and the retention fund are only released with the final one. Negotiating with the State as with a private party. The procedure and the specification govern."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Legislation on works","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/legislacion-obras#autoevaluacion","x":"09 Self-assessment Which law regulates national public works? Law 13,064, of 1947. What is the difference between the site inspection and the technical representative? The inspection represents the client and oversees; the technical representative represents the contractor and answers for the execution. Which contracting system is suitable when quantities are uncertain? The unit-price system. What is released with final acceptance? The bonds and the retention fund."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Legislation on works","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/legislacion-obras#ampliar","x":"10 Further reading Roberto Dromi. Licitación pública. Ciudad Argentina (in Spanish). The procedure for selecting the State’s contractor, step by step. Juan Carlos Cassagne. El contrato administrativo. Abeledo Perrot (in Spanish). The State’s contracts, including public works, with their prerogatives and guarantees. Law 13,064 and Decree 691/2016. Obras públicas y redeterminación de precios. InfoLEG (in Spanish). The national regime now in force, which is worth reading in full before the first tender."},{"t":"Why functions are not enough","d":"Introduction to an advanced language","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/lenguaje-avanzado#porque","x":"01 Why functions are not enough In C a program grows as a list of functions that pass structures to each other. It works until the project reaches a few thousand lines and three problems appear: anyone can touch any field, the same piece of data is validated in twenty places, and adding a case forces you to hunt down all the scattered if s. The three ideas that solve this Encapsulation. The data and the operations that touch it live together, and from outside you only see what was chosen to be shown. If the state can only be changed through methods, it is always valid. Inheritance. A class starts from another and adds or changes what it needs, without repeating what is common. Polymorphism. The same method is called on different objects and each one does its own thing. It is what lets you add a new instrument without touching the code that uses them. It is neither a fad nor an obligation: it is a way to keep a ten-thousand-line project understandable. For a two-hundred-line firmware, C and a structure remain the right answer."},{"t":"From structure to class","d":"Introduction to an advanced language","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/lenguaje-avanzado#clases","x":"02 From structure to class In C In C++ or Python struct Channel with its fields The class, with the same private fields channel_init(&c,...) The constructor channel_read(&c) A method: the object is the implicit first parameter channel_free(&c) The destructor, or the with block A function pointer in the structure A virtual method class Channel: def __init__(self, name, scale): self.name = name self._scale = scale # the underscore says “do not touch me” def read(self): return self._raw() * self._scale That explicit self in Python is the same as the implicit this in C++ and the same pointer to the structure that was passed by hand in C. The syntax changes, not the mechanism. Lab · inheritance and polymorphism The same code calls measure() on different objects. Choose which one it is and see which implementation runs. Instrument (base class) Multimeter Oscilloscope Power supply measure() describe() connect()"},{"t":"Dynamic dispatch","d":"Introduction to an advanced language","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/lenguaje-avanzado#polimorfismo","x":"03 Dynamic dispatch When a method is called on an object, the language has to decide which of the implementations to run. If that decision is made at compile time, it is static dispatch; if it is made at run time, by looking at the object’s real type, it is dynamic, and that is what makes polymorphism possible. for device in bench: # multimeter, oscilloscope, power supply… print(device.describe()) # each one answers in its own way The loop does not know and does not care what is inside the list. Adding a new instrument does not force you to touch it: it is enough for the new class to implement the same methods. That is what you gain. How it works inside In C++, each class with virtual methods has a virtual function table, and each object stores a pointer to its class’s table. Calling a virtual method means reading that pointer, looking up the entry and jumping: two more indirections than an ordinary call. In Python it is even more dynamic —the name is looked up in a dictionary— and that is why it is more flexible and slower."},{"t":"Abstract types and exceptions","d":"Introduction to an advanced language","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/lenguaje-avanzado#abstractos","x":"04 Abstract types and exceptions An abstract data type is defined by what can be done with it, not by how it is built. A stack is “push, pop and look at the top”, regardless of whether inside there is an array or a linked list. The standard library is exactly that: tested containers that no longer have to be written. Need C++ Python A sequence that grows std::vector list Look up by key std::map, unordered_map dict No duplicates std::set set Double-ended queue std::deque collections.deque Memory that frees itself unique_ptr, shared_ptr Garbage collector Exceptions In C, a function reports an error by returning a code that almost nobody checks. Exceptions separate the normal path from the error path: try: v = instrument.measure() except TimeoutError: log(\"the device did not respond\") finally: instrument.close() # this runs no matter what Three rules: catch only what you know how to handle —an empty except hides programming errors—, always release with finally or with with, and do not use exceptions for normal flow, because they are expensive and confusing."},{"t":"Which one to use in electronics","d":"Introduction to an advanced language","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/lenguaje-avanzado#cual","x":"05 Which one to use in electronics Job Reasonable tool Firmware for a small microcontroller C, and C++ without exceptions or dynamic allocation Large firmware, with layers Modern C++: classes, templates, constexpr Lab tool, interface, analysis Python, for how quickly it can be written Processing measurements and plotting Python with NumPy and Matplotlib Whatever has to fly C or C++, or the critical part in C called from Python The most useful combination for an electronics engineer is to know both: C or C++ for the device and Python for everything around it —automating the bench, processing the measurements, putting together the report—. They do not compete: they are used in different layers of the same project."},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Introduction to an advanced language","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/lenguaje-avanzado#laboratorio","x":"06 In the lab Exercise 1 · The same thing, twice Implement a stack in C with a structure and functions, and in C++ or Python as a class. Compare how many ways there are to misuse it in each case. Exercise 2 · An instrument bench Define a base class Instrument with connect, measure and describe, and three derived classes. Traverse a mixed list calling the same methods, and then add a fourth class without touching the loop. Exercise 3 · Errors that cannot be ignored Write a function that reads the serial port and raises an exception if the device does not respond. Check with finally that the port ends up closed even when it fails."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Introduction to an advanced language","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/lenguaje-avanzado#errores","x":"07 Common mistakes Making classes for everything. If there is no state to protect, a function is enough. Deep inheritance. Four levels are harder to follow than a little repetition. Exposing every attribute and losing the one guarantee that encapsulation gave. Catching Exception and carrying on as if nothing had happened, hiding real errors. Using exceptions for normal flow, such as exiting a loop. Assuming that Python is slow at everything: what is slow is the hand-written loop; with NumPy the calculation runs in C."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Introduction to an advanced language","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/lenguaje-avanzado#autoevaluacion","x":"08 Self-assessment What is encapsulation and what does it guarantee? Keeping the data and the operations together, and exposing only what is necessary. It guarantees that the state changes only through validated paths. What does polymorphism allow that would otherwise be costly? Adding a new type without touching the code that uses it: the loop that traverses the list of instruments stays the same. How does C++ resolve a call to a virtual method? Through the virtual function table: the object stores a pointer to its class’s table and the call jumps to the corresponding entry, at run time. Why is except: pass wrong? Because it catches everything, including programming errors, and hides them. You should catch only the exceptions you know how to handle. What does finally do? It runs that block whether or not there is an exception: it is where the port, the file or the connection is closed. Is it a good idea to use objects on a microcontroller with 2 kB of RAM? Simple classes, yes; exceptions and dynamic allocation, no. The tool is chosen for the problem, not out of habit."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Introduction to an advanced language","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/lenguaje-avanzado#ampliar","x":"09 Further reading Bjarne Stroustrup. A Tour of C++. 3rd ed., Addison-Wesley, 2022. Modern C++ in two hundred pages, written by its creator. Mark Lutz. Learning Python. 5th ed., O'Reilly, 2013. Thorough and careful about the language’s object model. Erich Gamma et al. Design Patterns. Addison-Wesley, 1994. The classic patterns. It is best read after having suffered the problems they solve. Robert C. Martin. Clean Code. Prentice Hall, 2008. Debatable on several points, but it installs the right question: will this still be understood a year from now."},{"t":"Describing hardware, not programming it","d":"Hardware description languages","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/lenguajes-hdl#describir","x":"01 Describing hardware, not programming it A hardware description language (HDL) looks like a programming language and is not one. A C program is a list of instructions that a processor executes one at a time, in order. A description in VHDL or Verilog is the description of a circuit: each statement is a piece of hardware that exists and works at the same time as all the others. There is no “now this line runs”; there are gates and flip-flops that all react at once to their inputs. That change of mindset is the main difficulty of this unit, and almost all the mistakes of someone learning HDL come from writing as if it were C: expecting things to happen in order, using variables as in a program, or writing a loop expecting it to repeat in time when in fact it will be unrolled into copied hardware."},{"t":"VHDL and Verilog","d":"Hardware description languages","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/lenguajes-hdl#lenguajes","x":"02 VHDL and Verilog Two languages have dominated digital design since the 1980s, and every FPGA tool accepts both: VHDL Verilog / SystemVerilog Origin U.S. Department of Defense, 1983; IEEE 1076 standard Private industry, 1984; IEEE 1364 standard, today IEEE 1800 Style Strongly typed and verbose, derived from Ada Compact, C-like syntax Where it is used most Europe, aerospace, defense, many universities Semiconductor industry in the U.S. and Asia, verification The same four-bit counter with synchronous reset, in both: library ieee; use ieee.std_logic_1164. all; use ieee.numeric_std. all; entity counter is port (clk, rst: in std_logic; q: out unsigned ( 3 downto 0 )); end; architecture rtl of counter is signal c: unsigned ( 3 downto 0 ); begin process (clk) begin if rising_edge (clk) then if rst = '1' then c <= ( others => '0' ); else c <= c + 1; end if; end if; end process; q <= c; end; VHDL module counter ( input clk, rst, output reg [ 3: 0 ] q ); always @( posedge clk) if (rst) q <= 4'd0; else q <= q + 4'd1; endmodule Verilog Both describe exactly the same circuit: four D flip-flops, a four-bit adder that adds one, and a multiplexer that chooses between the sum and zero depending on the reset. Neither of them “counts” in the sense of a program: they describe the connections that, edge by edge, make the hardware count."},{"t":"Blocking and nonblocking","d":"Hardware description languages","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/lenguajes-hdl#asignaciones","x":"03 Blocking and nonblocking In Verilog there are two ways to assign inside a clocked block, and confusing them produces circuits different from the ones intended: Assignment Semantics Correct use Nonblocking b <= a; All right-hand sides are read before the edge and all left-hand sides are updated together afterward: like real flip-flops Sequential, clocked logic Blocking b = a; Executes in order: the next line already sees the new value, as in C Combinational logic Lab · the same code, two circuits You choose the example and the type of assignment, and step through one clock edge at a time. The table shows the value of each register after each edge, and the note below explains which circuit the tool would synthesize. shift register a → b → c swap of two registers a ↔ b nonblocking (<=) blocking (=) clock edge ↑ reset"},{"t":"Simulation and synthesis","d":"Hardware description languages","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/lenguajes-hdl#sintesis","x":"04 Simulation and synthesis One HDL file has two destinations. The simulator runs it to see how it behaves over time; the synthesizer translates it into real gates and flip-flops. Not everything that simulates can be synthesized: Construct In simulation In synthesis Delays such as #10 or after 10 ns Respected Ignored: the delay comes from the silicon Reading and writing files Useful in testbenches Does not exist if without else in combinational logic Keeps the previous value Generates an unwanted latch for loop of 8 iterations Repeats eight times It is unrolled: eight copies of the hardware in parallel That is why every design is verified with a testbench ( test bench ): a module that is not synthesized, that generates the stimuli, observes the outputs and compares them with what is expected. In industry, writing the verification takes more work than writing the design."},{"t":"Differences from a procedural language","d":"Hardware description languages","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/lenguajes-hdl#procedural","x":"05 Differences from a procedural language C, Python VHDL, Verilog Execution Sequential, one instruction at a time Concurrent: everything works at the same time Time Implicit, set by the processor Explicit: clock edges and delays What it produces Instructions for a processor that already exists The circuit itself A loop Repeats in time Replicates in space Dynamic memory, recursion Natural Impossible: the hardware does not grow while it runs Typical error A runtime exception A circuit that simulates fine and does something else on the board"},{"t":"Where it shows up in electronics","d":"Hardware description languages","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/lenguajes-hdl#electronica","x":"06 Where it shows up in electronics Case The role of HDL FPGA design The whole design is written in HDL and the tool synthesizes it for the device Custom integrated circuits (ASIC) The same HDL, synthesized onto a foundry’s cell library Processor verification SystemVerilog with randomized testbenches and assertions High-level synthesis (HLS) You write in C or C++ and a tool generates the HDL: useful for filters and signal processing"},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Hardware description languages","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/lenguajes-hdl#laboratorio","x":"07 In the lab Activity 1 · Simulating the counter Simulate the counter from section 2 with a free simulator (GHDL or Icarus Verilog) and a testbench that applies reset and twenty clock edges. See in GTKWave that it returns to zero after 15. Activity 2 · Provoking a latch Write a combinational multiplexer with an if without else, synthesize it and look in the report for the inferred-latch warning. Fix it and compare. Activity 3 · Blocking versus nonblocking Synthesize the two versions of the lab’s shift register and compare in the schematic viewer how many flip-flops each one generates."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Hardware description languages","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/lenguajes-hdl#errores","x":"08 Common mistakes Thinking of statements as steps in time. They are parts of a circuit that work at the same time. Using blocking assignment in a clocked block. It collapses registers and breaks shifts and swaps. Leaving an if without else in combinational logic. It generates a latch. Expecting #10 to produce a delay in the FPGA. The synthesizer ignores it. Not writing a testbench. What was not simulated fails on the board."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Hardware description languages","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/lenguajes-hdl#autoevaluacion","x":"09 Self-assessment What is the fundamental difference between a program in C and a description in VHDL? The program runs in order on a processor; the description defines a circuit whose parts all work at the same time. What does b <= a; c <= b; do in a clocked block? A shift register: on each edge, c takes the previous value of b and b that of a. And with blocking assignments? c sees the new value of b, which is already a: both registers take a on the same edge and the shift disappears. What does an if without else generate in combinational logic? A latch, because the output has to remember its value when the condition is not met. What is a testbench? A module that is not synthesized, that applies stimuli to the design and checks its outputs in simulation."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Hardware description languages","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/lenguajes-hdl#ampliar","x":"10 Further reading Pong P. Chu. FPGA Prototyping by VHDL Examples. 2nd ed., Wiley, 2017. VHDL learned by building real circuits on a board, with synthesis always in view. Samir Palnitkar. Verilog HDL: A Guide to Digital Design and Synthesis. 2nd ed., Prentice Hall, 2003. The classic Verilog text, with its chapter on blocking and nonblocking assignments. Clifford E. Cummings. Nonblocking Assignments in Verilog Synthesis, Coding Styles That Kill!. SNUG San José, 2000. The paper that set the style rules the whole industry uses today."},{"t":"A limit with infinitely many paths","d":"Limit, continuity and the complex derivative","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/limite-derivada-compleja#limite","x":"01 A limit with infinitely many paths On the real line, approaching a point allows only two paths: from the left and from the right. That is why the two one-sided limits are enough. In the complex plane, \\( z \\) can approach \\( z_0 \\) along infinitely many directions and also along spiral curves. The limit exists only if all those paths give the same number. \\[ \\lim_{z \\to z_0} f(z) = L \\iff \\forall\\,\\varepsilon \\gt 0\\ \\exists\\,\\delta \\gt 0: 0 \\lt |z - z_0| \\lt \\delta \\implies |f(z) - L| \\lt \\varepsilon \\] The same definition as always, but now \\( |z - z_0| \\lt \\delta \\) describes a disk, not an interval. The change from interval to disk is what makes everything that follows more demanding. Checking a complex limit by trying two or three directions proves nothing: to show that it does not exist it is enough to find two paths that give different results, but to assert that it does exist you have to bound the modulus. Why it matters to an electronics engineer The transfer function of a circuit, \\( H(s) \\), is a complex function. The behavior near a pole —what happens as \\( s \\) approaches the resonant frequency— is literally a limit problem in the plane. And all the machinery that comes later —Cauchy, residues, Nyquist— rests on those functions being differentiable in the complex sense."},{"t":"Continuity","d":"Limit, continuity and the complex derivative","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/limite-derivada-compleja#continuidad","x":"02 Continuity \\( f \\) is continuous at \\( z_0 \\) if the limit exists and equals the value of the function: \\( \\lim_{z \\to z_0} f(z) = f(z_0) \\). Since \\( f = u + jv \\), this is equivalent to both real functions \\( u(x,y) \\) and \\( v(x,y) \\) being continuous at \\( (x_0, y_0) \\). Nothing new, then: polynomials, the exponential, sine and cosine are continuous on the whole plane, and quotients are continuous except where the denominator vanishes. The discontinuity you can actually measure The argument \\( \\operatorname{Arg} z \\), taken on the principal branch, is not continuous across the negative real axis: from above it tends to \\( +\\pi \\) and from below to \\( -\\pi \\). That 360° jump is exactly what a network analyzer shows when the phase “wraps,” and that is why measurement software includes an unwrapping function."},{"t":"The derivative, and why it demands so much","d":"Limit, continuity and the complex derivative","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/limite-derivada-compleja#derivada","x":"03 The derivative, and why it demands so much The definition is identical to the real one: \\[ f'(z_0) = \\lim_{\\Delta z \\to 0} \\frac{f(z_0 + \\Delta z) - f(z_0)}{\\Delta z} \\] But now \\( \\Delta z \\) is a complex number that can tend to zero from any direction, and the quotient has to give the same value in all of them. That is the fundamental difference from real calculus: it is not the same definition with different symbols, it is a far more restrictive condition. Let us write \\( \\Delta z = r\\,e^{j\\theta} \\) and keep the direction \\( \\theta \\) fixed while \\( r \\to 0 \\). Each \\( \\theta \\) gives one value of the quotient. If, as \\( r \\) shrinks, all those values gather at a single point, the derivative exists; if they stay spread out, it does not. Lab · the difference quotient, direction by direction On the left, the point \\( z_0 \\) and the circle of radius \\( |\\Delta z| \\) along which the increment approaches. On the right, the value of the quotient \\( \\Delta w / \\Delta z \\) for all directions at once. Shrink the radius and see whether that curve contracts to a point. f(z) = z² f(z) = e^z f(z) = 1/z f(z) = z̄ · the conjugate f(z) = |z|² f(z) = Re(z) Show the trace of all directions Radius |Δz| = 0.500 Direction θ = 30 °"},{"t":"The conjugate: the textbook counterexample","d":"Limit, continuity and the complex derivative","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/limite-derivada-compleja#falla","x":"04 The conjugate: the textbook counterexample Take \\( f(z) = \\bar{z} \\), which flips the sign of the imaginary part. It is continuous on the whole plane and its formula could not be simpler. The difference quotient comes out by hand: \\[ \\frac{\\overline{z_0 + \\Delta z} - \\overline{z_0}}{\\Delta z} = \\frac{\\overline{\\Delta z}}{\\Delta z} = \\frac{r\\,e^{-j\\theta}}{r\\,e^{j\\theta}} = e^{-2j\\theta} \\] The radius cancels: no matter how much \\( \\Delta z \\) shrinks, the quotient keeps the same value, and that value depends only on the direction. Approaching along the real axis (\\( \\theta = 0 \\)) the quotient is \\( 1 \\); along the imaginary axis (\\( \\theta = 90^\\circ \\)) it is \\( -1 \\). Two paths, two results: the conjugate is not differentiable at any point of the plane, despite being continuous at all of them. In the lab it shows up as a circle of radius one that never shrinks. Function Difference quotient Differentiable? \\( z^2 \\) \\( 2z_0 + \\Delta z \\) Yes, on the whole plane \\( 1/z \\) \\( \\dfrac{-1}{z_0(z_0 + \\Delta z)} \\) Yes, except at \\( z = 0 \\) \\( \\bar{z} \\) \\( e^{-2j\\theta} \\) At no point \\( |z|^2 \\) \\( \\bar{z}_0 + \\overline{\\Delta z} + z_0\\,e^{-2j\\theta} \\) Only at \\( z = 0 \\) \\( \\operatorname{Re}(z) \\) \\( \\cos\\theta\\;e^{-j\\theta} \\) At no point The pattern repeats: as soon as \\( \\bar{z} \\), or \\( |z| \\), or the real or imaginary part alone appears, the derivative falls apart. The deeper reason is given by the next topic, with the Cauchy-Riemann equations: those functions mix \\("},{"t":"Differentiable on a neighborhood: analytic function","d":"Limit, continuity and the complex derivative","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/limite-derivada-compleja#analitica","x":"05 Differentiable on a neighborhood: analytic function A function is analytic —or holomorphic — at a point if it is differentiable at that point and on a whole disk around it. The distinction matters: \\( |z|^2 \\) is differentiable at the origin but is analytic nowhere, because there is no disk on which it is. What does not happen in real calculus For real functions, a function can be differentiable once and not twice. For complex functions that is impossible: if \\( f \\) is analytic on a domain, it has derivatives of all orders and equals its Taylor series. Being differentiable once on a neighborhood, in the complex plane, forces everything else. From that rigidity come Cauchy’s theorems and the calculus of residues. The differentiation rules, on the other hand, are the same as ever and are proved the same way: sum, product, quotient and chain rule. And so are the familiar derivatives: \\[ \\frac{d}{dz}z^n = n z^{n-1}, \\qquad \\frac{d}{dz}e^{z} = e^{z}, \\qquad \\frac{d}{dz}\\operatorname{sin} z = \\cos z \\] Functions that are analytic on the whole plane are called entire: polynomials, \\( e^z \\), \\( \\operatorname{sin} z \\) and \\( \\cos z \\). A rational function \\( H(s) = N(s)/D(s) \\) —that is, any transfer function of a circuit with lumped elements— is analytic on the whole plane except at the zeros of the denominator, which are the poles. That sentence, now said in the vocabulary of complex variable, is what enables the stability analysis done in Control."},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Limit, continuity and the complex derivative","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/limite-derivada-compleja#laboratorio","x":"06 In the lab Activity 1 · Disproving a limit with two paths For \\( f(z) = \\bar{z}/z \\), compute the limit as \\( z \\to 0 \\) approaching along the real axis, along the imaginary axis and along the 45° line. Check that they give 1, −1 and −j respectively, and conclude that the limit does not exist. Repeat with \\( f(z) = z^2/|z|^2 \\). Activity 2 · The derivative, measured numerically In a spreadsheet, evaluate the difference quotient of \\( f(z) = e^z \\) at \\( z_0 = 1 + j \\) for \\( |\\Delta z| = 10^{-1}, 10^{-2}, 10^{-3} \\) and eight directions. Verify that the 24 values converge to \\( e^{1+j} \\approx 1.469 + j\\,2.287 \\), and that the error falls in proportion to \\( |\\Delta z| \\). Activity 3 · Phase wrapping With a generator and an oscilloscope, measure the phase shift of an RC low-pass filter between 10 Hz and 100 kHz, and plot it. Identify where the instrument jumps from −180° to +180° and correct it by hand. It is the discontinuity of \\( \\operatorname{Arg} z \\), seen with a test lead in your hand."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Limit, continuity and the complex derivative","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/limite-derivada-compleja#errores","x":"07 Common mistakes Trying two directions and declaring that the limit exists. That works to disprove it, not to assert it: there are functions that agree along all straight lines and fail along a parabola. Confusing continuous with differentiable. Differentiable implies continuous, never the other way around; \\( \\bar{z} \\) is the textbook example. Believing that a simple formula guarantees differentiability. \\( \\bar{z} \\), \\( |z| \\) and \\( \\operatorname{Re}(z) \\) are elementary and are differentiable at no point. Saying “analytic” when there is only differentiability at one point. Analytic requires an entire neighborhood. Forgetting that \\( \\operatorname{Arg} z \\) has a branch cut. Differentiating across the negative real axis is not valid with the principal branch."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Limit, continuity and the complex derivative","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/limite-derivada-compleja#autoevaluacion","x":"08 Self-assessment Why aren’t one-sided limits enough, as they are for real functions? Because \\( z \\to z_0 \\) allows infinitely many directions and also curves. The condition is over a disk, not over an interval. What is \\( \\Delta w/\\Delta z \\) for \\( f(z) = \\bar{z} \\) if the increment goes in at 45°? \\( e^{-2j\\,45^\\circ} = e^{-j90^\\circ} = -j \\). Along the real axis it would give 1: two paths, two values, no derivative. \\( |z|^2 \\) is differentiable at \\( z = 0 \\). Is it analytic there? No. Analytic requires differentiability on an entire neighborhood, and in any disk around the origin there are points where it fails. Where is \\( H(s) = \\dfrac{1}{s^2 + 2s + 5} \\) analytic? On the whole plane except at \\( s = -1 \\pm j2 \\), the zeros of the denominator. They are the poles of the circuit, and there the function is not defined. If \\( f \\) is analytic on a domain, how many times can it be differentiated? Infinitely many. It is a property exclusive to the complex case: being differentiable once on a neighborhood forces all the derivatives to exist. What do you see in the lab when a function is not differentiable? That the trace of the difference quotient does not contract as the radius shrinks: a curve of fixed size remains, because each direction gives a different value."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Limit, continuity and the complex derivative","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/limite-derivada-compleja#ampliar","x":"09 Further reading Ruel V. Churchill and James W. Brown. Complex Variables and Applications. 7th ed. (Spanish edition, “Variable compleja y aplicaciones”), McGraw-Hill, 2004. The chapters on limits, continuity and derivatives, with the careful construction of the counterexamples. A. David Wunsch. Complex Variables with Applications. 2nd ed. (Spanish edition, “Variable compleja con aplicaciones”), Addison-Wesley Iberoamericana, 1997. Written for engineering: every concept arrives with a circuit or a field behind it. Murray R. Spiegel. Complex Variables. Schaum’s Outline Series (Spanish edition, “Variable compleja”), McGraw-Hill, 2011. Solved exercises on limits along different paths, which is where you learn to disprove them."},{"t":"What happens near the point, not at the point","d":"Limits of real functions","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/limite-funciones#idea","x":"01 What happens near the point, not at the point The limit answers a very precise question: what value does f(x) approach as x approaches a number a, without ever reaching it. What happens exactly at x = a plays no part; the function may not even be defined there. The example that fixes the idea f(x) = (x² − 1)/(x − 1) does not exist at x = 1: it gives 0/0. But for every x ≠ 1 it can be simplified, because x² − 1 = (x − 1)(x + 1), and what remains is f(x) = x + 1. So, as x approaches 1, the function approaches 2. This is written lim x→1 f(x) = 2, and it does not matter that f(1) does not exist. That distinction, “near” versus “at”, is what makes it possible to talk about instantaneous velocity, about the slope of a curve and about steady state. All of calculus rests on it."},{"t":"The formal definition","d":"Limits of real functions","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/limite-funciones#definicion","x":"02 The formal definition “Approaching” is not a mathematical phrase. The rigorous version, due to Cauchy and Weierstrass, turns the approach into a challenge between two tolerances: \\[ \\lim_{x \\to a} f (x) = L \\quad \\iff \\quad \\forall \\varepsilon \\gt 0, \\; \\exists \\delta \\gt 0: \\; 0 \\lt | x -a | \\lt \\delta \\; \\implies \\; | f (x) -L | \\lt \\varepsilon \\] Read aloud: however narrow the band ε around L, you can always find a band δ around a such that the whole function, except perhaps at a, falls inside the band. It is a two-player game One player proposes a tolerance ε —“I want the output within ±0.01”— and the other has to answer with a δ —“then keep the input within ±0.003”—. If there is an answer for every demand, the limit is L. It is the same logic as an engineering specification: how much the input can vary for the output to stay within tolerance. The 0 < |x − a| on the left is the detail that excludes the point a itself. That is why the limit can exist even if the function is not defined there, or if it takes some other value. Lab · the ε–δ game Choose a function and shrink ε. If for every ε you can find a δ that works, the limit exists. (x² − 1)/(x − 1) at a = 1 · hole 2x + 1 at a = 1 step at a = 0 · jump 1/x² at a = 0 · infinity sin(1/x) at a = 0 · oscillates ε 0.8 δ 0.6 Find the largest δ that works"},{"t":"One-sided limits","d":"Limits of real functions","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/limite-funciones#laterales","x":"03 One-sided limits You can reach a point from the left or from the right, and the result can differ: \\[ \\lim_{x \\to a^-} f (x) \\quad \\operatorname{and} \\quad \\lim_{x \\to a^+} f (x) \\] The limit exists if and only if both one-sided limits exist and are equal. The typical case is the step: a signal that goes from 0 to 5 V has a limit of 0 from the left and 5 from the right, so at the instant of the edge the limit does not exist. It is not a mathematical pathology: it is exactly what you see on the oscilloscope."},{"t":"How they are computed in practice","d":"Limits of real functions","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/limite-funciones#algebra","x":"04 How they are computed in practice If the limits of f and g exist and are finite, the limit of the sum is the sum, that of the product is the product and that of the quotient is the quotient as long as the denominator does not tend to zero. With that, most limits are solved by substitution: if the function is continuous at a, the limit is f(a). The work comes with the indeterminate forms, which are the combinations where algebra alone is not enough: Indeterminate form How to get out of it Example 0/0 Factor and simplify, or rationalize \\( \\dfrac{x^2 - 4}{x - 2} \\to x + 2 \\to 4 \\) ∞/∞ Divide numerator and denominator by the highest power \\( \\dfrac{3x^2 + x}{5x^2 - 1} \\to \\dfrac{3}{5} \\) ∞ − ∞ Factor out a common term or multiply by the conjugate \\( \\sqrt{x^{2} + x} - x \\to \\dfrac{1}{2} \\) 0 · ∞ Rewrite as a quotient \\( x \\ln x \\) as \\( x \\to 0^+ \\), tends to 0 1 ∞ Reduce to the notable limit for the number e (1 + 1/x) x → e And two notable limits worth memorizing, because they appear all the time: \\[ \\lim_{x \\to 0} \\frac{\\operatorname{sin} x}{x} = 1 \\quad \\lim_{x \\to \\infty} {\\left(1 + \\frac{1}{x} \\right)}^x = e \\] The first, with x in radians, is what justifies the approximation sin x ≈ x for small angles, so widely used when linearizing. The second defines the number e, which governs every charge and discharge."},{"t":"Infinite limits and asymptotes","d":"Limits of real functions","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/limite-funciones#infinito","x":"05 Infinite limits and asymptotes Situation Meaning On the graph lim x→a f(x) = ±∞ Grows without bound as x approaches a Vertical asymptote at x = a lim x→±∞ f(x) = L Settles at L in the long run Horizontal asymptote y = L lim x→∞ [f(x) − (mx + h)] = 0 Hugs a slanted line Oblique asymptote For a rational function, behavior at infinity is decided by comparing degrees: if the numerator’s is smaller, the horizontal asymptote is y = 0; if they are equal, it is the quotient of the leading coefficients; if the numerator exceeds the denominator by one degree, there is an oblique asymptote. This is exactly what is done when drawing a Bode plot: the transfer function is studied for ω → 0 and for ω → ∞, and the asymptotes give the lines of −20 dB per decade."},{"t":"Limits you can measure with the oscilloscope","d":"Limits of real functions","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/limite-funciones#electronica","x":"06 Limits you can measure with the oscilloscope Steady state. A charging capacitor follows v(t) = V(1 − e −t/RC ). The limit as t → ∞ is V: the final voltage. In practice it is accepted that it has “arrived” after five time constants, when less than 1% remains. Gain of a feedback amplifier. With open-loop gain A and feedback β, the gain is A/(1 + Aβ). The limit as A → ∞ is 1/β: that is why the ideal op amp is analyzed as if its gain were infinite, and why the final gain is set by the resistors. Asymptotic behavior of a filter. For ω → 0 a low-pass filter lets everything through and for ω → ∞ it attenuates; the asymptotes of that limit are the lines of the Bode plot. Edges. At an ideal edge the left-hand and right-hand limits differ: the limit does not exist. No real circuit switches in zero time, and that is precisely the difference between the model and the oscilloscope."},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Limits of real functions","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/limite-funciones#laboratorio","x":"07 In the lab Exercise 1 · The limit, with a table of values For f(x) = (√(x + 4) − 2)/x, build a table with x = ±0.1; ±0.01; ±0.001 and conjecture the limit at 0. Then compute it by rationalizing and compare. (It gives 1/4.) Exercise 2 · Measuring a time constant Charge a capacitor with a step and use the oscilloscope to measure the time to reach 63%, 95% and 99% of the final value. Check that they correspond to 1, 3 and 5 time constants, and discuss why the final value is a limit and not a value that is ever reached. Exercise 3 · Asymptotes of a filter Measure the response of an RC low-pass filter at several frequencies, plot it on a logarithmic scale and draw the two asymptotes. Check that they cross at the cutoff frequency. You can practice this in the instrument simulator."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Limits of real functions","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/limite-funciones#errores","x":"08 Common mistakes Substituting and stopping at 0/0 as if it were a result: it is an indeterminate form, and it has to be worked out. Confusing f(a) with the limit: they can differ, and the limit can exist without f(a) existing. Forgetting the one-sided limits in piecewise functions or functions with an absolute value. Writing ∞ as if it were a number and “simplifying” ∞/∞. Using sin x ≈ x with x in degrees. The notable limit holds in radians. Believing an asymptote cannot be crossed: a horizontal one can be crossed many times before it settles."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Limits of real functions","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/limite-funciones#autoevaluacion","x":"09 Self-assessment Compute lim x→3 (x² − 9)/(x − 3). Factoring: (x − 3)(x + 3)/(x − 3) = x + 3 → 6. lim x→∞ (2x³ − x)/(5x³ + 4x²). Dividing everything by x³: 2/5. Does the limit at 0 of f(x) = |x|/x exist? No: from the right it gives 1 and from the left −1. The one-sided limits exist but do not coincide. lim x→0 sin(5x)/x. Rearrange it into the notable limit: 5·sin(5x)/(5x) → 5. What asymptotes does f(x) = (2x + 1)/(x − 3) have? Vertical at x = 3 and horizontal at y = 2, the quotient of the leading coefficients. A capacitor charges to 12 V with τ = 2 ms. What is the limit of v(t) and what is its value at 10 ms? The limit is 12 V. At 10 ms, which is 5τ, v = 12(1 − e −5 ) ≈ 11.92 V: 99.3%."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Limits of real functions","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/limite-funciones#ampliar","x":"10 Further reading James Stewart. Single Variable Calculus: Early Transcendentals. 7th ed. (Spanish edition, “Cálculo de una variable. Trascendentes tempranas”), Cengage Learning, 2012. Chapter 2 develops the limit with tables, graphs and the ε–δ definition, in that order. Hebe Rabuffetti. Introducción al análisis matemático (Cálculo 1) (in Spanish). El Ateneo. Treats one-sided limits and indeterminate forms with the notation used in Argentine universities. Michael Spivak. Calculus. 3rd ed. (Spanish edition, “Calculus”), Reverté, 2012. For anyone who wants complete rigor: the definition of the limit proved in full detail."},{"t":"When a cable stops being a cable","d":"Transmission lines","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/lineas-transmision#cuando","x":"01 When a cable stops being a cable In circuit theory, a cable is a node: the voltage is the same at both of its ends at the same instant. That holds as long as the signal takes much less time to travel along it than it takes to change. When the length of the cable approaches an appreciable fraction of the wavelength —the rule of thumb is one tenth— or when the rise time of a pulse is comparable to the travel time, the cable becomes a transmission line: the signal travels along it as a wave, and what happens at one end depends on what is at the other. The page on lumped models in Circuit Theory I marked that limit."},{"t":"The distributed model","d":"Transmission lines","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/lineas-transmision#modelo","x":"02 The distributed model A short section of line is modeled with four parameters per unit length: the resistance \\( R \\) and the inductance \\( L \\) of the conductors, and the conductance \\( G \\) and the capacitance \\( C \\) between them. From these come the telegrapher's equations, which describe a wave traveling in both directions, with two parameters that characterize the line: \\[ Z_0 = \\sqrt{\\frac{R + j\\omega L}{G + j\\omega C}} \\] \\[ \\gamma = \\sqrt{(R + j\\omega L)(G + j\\omega C)} \\] \\( Z_0 \\) is the characteristic impedance: the ratio between voltage and current of a wave traveling in a single direction. In a lossless line, \\( Z_0 = \\sqrt{L/C} \\) is real and the velocity is \\( 1/\\sqrt{LC} \\). Line \\( Z_0 \\) Velocity RG-58 coaxial (polyethylene) 50 Ω 0.66 c RG-6 coaxial (television) 75 Ω 0.82 c (foam) Data twisted pair 100 Ω Around 0.65 c 1.9 mm microstrip on 1 mm FR-4 About 50 Ω Around 0.55 c In a coaxial cable, \\( Z_0 = \\dfrac{60}{\\sqrt{\\varepsilon_r}} \\ln \\dfrac{b}{a} \\), with \\( a \\) the radius of the inner conductor and \\( b \\) that of the shield: the impedance depends on the geometry and the dielectric, not on the length."},{"t":"Reflection and standing waves","d":"Transmission lines","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/lineas-transmision#reflexion","x":"03 Reflection and standing waves If the line ends in a load \\( Z_L \\) different from \\( Z_0 \\), part of the wave is reflected, just like the plane wave at the boundary between two media: \\[ \\Gamma_L = \\frac{Z_L - Z_0}{Z_L + Z_0} \\] \\[ SWR = \\frac{1 + |\\Gamma_L|}{1 - |\\Gamma_L|} \\] The standing wave ratio (SWR; ROE in Spanish) is the ratio between the maximum and the minimum of the voltage along the line. With a matched load it is 1; with a short circuit or an open circuit, infinite. The incident and the reflected waves add with phases that depend on position, and the voltage along the line has fixed maxima and minima separated by half a wavelength. For the same reason, the impedance seen from the input depends on the length: \\[ Z_{in} = Z_0\\, \\frac{Z_L + j Z_0 \\tan \\beta \\ell}{Z_0 + j Z_L \\tan \\beta \\ell} \\] A half-wave section repeats the load; a quarter-wave section inverts it, \\( Z_{in} = Z_0^2 / Z_L \\): this is the quarter-wave transformer. A shorted or open section behaves as a pure reactance. Solving this expression by hand is tedious, and that is why the Smith chart exists, which turns it into a rotation on a graph. Lab · the standing wave on the line A lossless line of impedance \\( Z_0 \\) ends in a load \\( R_L + jX_L \\). The graph shows the peak value of the voltage along the line, from the load (on the right) toward the generator, for an incident wave of amplitude 1. The dot marks the position where the input impedance is calculated. Z₀ = 50 Ω Z₀ = 75 Ω Load resistan"},{"t":"Pulses and reflectometry","d":"Transmission lines","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/lineas-transmision#pulsos","x":"04 Pulses and reflectometry In the time domain, a step entering the line travels to the load and comes back reflected with an amplitude \\( \\Gamma_L \\) times the incident one, after a time equal to twice the delay of the section. If the source is not matched either, the reflection bounces back again, and the voltage settles in a staircase of echoes. Time-domain reflectometry (TDR) uses this principle to locate faults: the time of the echo gives the distance and its shape, the type of fault. This is how a break in a buried cable or a discontinuity in a board trace is found."},{"t":"Where it appears in electronics","d":"Transmission lines","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/lineas-transmision#electronica","x":"05 Where it appears in electronics Case What matters about the line High-speed bus on a board Controlled-impedance traces and terminations to avoid reflections Antenna cable of a transmitter Low SWR so that the power reaches the antenna Adapter between 50 and 75 Ω A quarter-wave section of intermediate impedance Ethernet network 100 Ω twisted pairs terminated at each end Locating faults in cables Time-domain reflectometry"},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Transmission lines","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/lineas-transmision#laboratorio","x":"06 In the lab Activity 1 · Echoes in a coaxial cable With a fast pulse generator, 20 m of RG-58 and an oscilloscope at the input, observe the echo with the far end open, shorted and terminated in 50 Ω. Measure the delay and calculate the propagation velocity. Activity 2 · The quarter wave With a network analyzer, measure the input impedance of a section of coaxial cable terminated in 100 Ω and find the frequency at which it equals 25 Ω. Relate it to the length of the section. Activity 3 · The SWR of an antenna Measure the SWR of an amateur radio antenna with an SWR meter across its band and locate the frequency of best match."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Transmission lines","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/lineas-transmision#errores","x":"07 Common mistakes Treating as a node a cable that is long compared with the wavelength. The voltage changes along the cable. Believing that \\( Z_0 \\) depends on the length. It depends only on the cross-section and the dielectric. Measuring with a 1 MΩ probe at the end of an unterminated 50 Ω line. The pulse is reflected and the waveform lies. Forgetting the velocity of the dielectric when calculating a quarter wave. In RG-58, the physical length is 66% of the electrical one."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Transmission lines","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/lineas-transmision#autoevaluacion","x":"08 Self-assessment What SWR does a 25 Ω load produce on a 50 Ω line? \\( \\Gamma = -1/3 \\), SWR = 2. What impedance is seen a quarter wave away from a 100 Ω load on a 50 Ω line? \\( 50^2 / 100 = 25 \\) Ω. How long is a quarter wave of RG-58 at 100 MHz? \\( 0.66 \\times 3\\text{ m} / 4 \\approx 0.50 \\) m. What is seen on a TDR if the cable is cut? An echo of the same polarity as the pulse (open circuit, \\( \\Gamma = +1 \\)) at the time that corresponds to the distance of the break."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Transmission lines","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/lineas-transmision#ampliar","x":"09 Further reading David M. Pozar. Microwave Engineering. 4th ed., Wiley, 2011. Its chapter on transmission line theory is the reference, with the Smith chart and matching. Howard Johnson and Martin Graham. High-Speed Digital Design: A Handbook of Black Magic. Prentice Hall, 1993. Lines seen from digital design: reflections, terminations and crosstalk. Fawwaz T. Ulaby and Umberto Ravaioli. Fundamentals of Applied Electromagnetics. 7th ed., Pearson, 2015. Lines in the sinusoidal and transient regimes, with many figures."},{"t":"From fixed circuit to programmable","d":"Programmable logic devices","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/logica-programable#idea","x":"01 From fixed circuit to programmable With discrete 74-series gates, every design change means a new circuit board. A programmable logic device (PLD) reverses the order: the manufacturer sells an integrated circuit with many generic, unconnected logic resources, and the designer decides afterward, with a configuration file, what function each one performs and how they are connected to each other. The same chip can be a counter in the morning and a motor controller in the afternoon. The Electronics Technician program’s page on memories and programmable devices introduces the idea. Here we look at how each type is built inside, why the FPGA ended up dominating, and how to get from an HDL file like those in the previous unit to a working chip."},{"t":"AND-OR arrays: PROM, PLA and PAL","d":"Programmable logic devices","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/logica-programable#arreglos","x":"02 AND-OR arrays: PROM, PLA and PAL Every combinational function can be written as a sum of products, so the first PLDs are two planes of gates: an AND plane that forms the products of the inputs and their complements, and an OR plane that sums them for each output. What distinguishes each family is which plane can be programmed: Device AND plane OR plane Consequence PROM Fixed: all the minterms Programmable Any function, but the AND plane grows as \\(2^n\\): suitable only for few inputs PLA Programmable Programmable The most flexible, and the slowest and most expensive: two planes of fuses in series PAL, GAL Programmable Fixed: each output sums a fixed number of products Fast and cheap; if a function needs more products than are available, it does not fit The GAL is the PAL with electrically erasable cells instead of fuses, and it adds to each output a macrocell: a flip-flop that can be used or bypassed, a polarity control, and the ability to feed the output back to the AND plane. With that, a single GAL22V10 can hold a counter or a small state machine, and for years it was the logic glue of every board with a microprocessor."},{"t":"CPLD","d":"Programmable logic devices","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/logica-programable#cpld","x":"03 CPLD A CPLD ( complex PLD ) puts several PAL-type blocks on the same chip and joins them with a central interconnect matrix. It inherits the virtues of the PAL: Nonvolatile: the configuration is held in EEPROM or flash cells and the chip works from the first microsecond after power-up. Predictable timing: every signal crosses the same matrix, so the input-to-output delay is almost the same for any path and is listed in the datasheet before designing. Its limit is size: the sum-of-products structure does not scale beyond a few hundred macrocells. Today it is used for startup logic and power-supply sequencing, address decoding, and “glue” tasks where the logic has to be alive before everything else."},{"t":"FPGA: truth tables in memory","d":"Programmable logic devices","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/logica-programable#fpga","x":"04 FPGA: truth tables in memory The FPGA ( field-programmable gate array ) abandons the sum of products. Its basic element is the LUT ( look-up table ): a small memory of \\(2^k\\) bits with \\(k\\) address inputs. By storing the desired truth table in it, the LUT implements any function of \\(k\\) variables with the same delay, whether it is an AND or the most tangled function: \\[ \\text{LUT bits} = 2^k \\ \\text{possible functions} = 2^{2^k} \\] Each combination of the \\( k \\) inputs is an address; each of the \\( 2^k \\) cells can be 0 or 1 on its own. With \\(k = 4\\) that is 16 configuration bits and 65,536 different functions; current families use 6-input, 64-bit LUTs. A function of more variables is split with the Shannon expansion, which separates according to one of the variables and selects with a multiplexer: \\[ f(x_4, \\dots, x_0) = \\ \\overline{x_4}\\; f\\big|_{x_4 = 0} \\;+\\; x_4\\; f\\big|_{x_4 = 1} \\] \\( f|_{x_4=0} \\) and \\( f|_{x_4=1} \\) are the cofactors: the function with \\( x_4 \\) fixed at 0 and at 1. Each cofactor is a function of four variables, which fits in a LUT4, and the multiplexer that selects between them is built into the cell. Around the LUTs, the FPGA has: Resource What it is Logic block (CLB, LAB) A group of LUTs, each with its flip-flop at the output, plus dedicated carry chains for fast addition Interconnect A grid of programmable wires and switches that takes up most of the silicon and contributes a good part of the delay Block memory (BRAM) Dual-port blocks of"},{"t":"Which one to choose","d":"Programmable logic devices","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/logica-programable#comparacion","x":"05 Which one to choose PAL, GAL CPLD FPGA Structure One AND-OR array Several AND-OR arrays with a central matrix Thousands to millions of LUTs with grid interconnect Capacity Tens of gates Hundreds to a few thousand Thousands to millions of cells Configuration Nonvolatile Nonvolatile Almost always SRAM, loaded at power-up Delay Fixed Fixed and predictable Depends on placement and routing: it has to be analyzed Typical use Replacing discrete logic, legacy equipment Startup, decoding, power-supply sequencing Signal processing, video, communications, integrated-circuit prototypes"},{"t":"From HDL to chip","d":"Programmable logic devices","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/logica-programable#flujo","x":"06 From HDL to chip The manufacturer’s tool takes the description to the device in stages, and each one can fail on its own: Synthesis: translates the HDL into a network of generic gates, flip-flops, adders and memories. This is where unwanted latches and optimized-away registers show up. Mapping: distributes that network among the device’s resources: LUTs, flip-flops, BRAM, DSP. Placement and routing ( place and route ): chooses which physical cell each element goes in and which wires connect them. It is the slowest stage, and the one that decides the delays. Timing analysis: adds up the delays of each path between flip-flops and compares them with the clock period requested in the constraints file. If any path falls short, you have to redesign or lower the frequency, just as with the critical path in the sequential logic unit. Bitstream: generates the configuration file and loads it into the chip or its flash. The constraints file is as much a part of the design as the HDL: it assigns each signal to a pin, sets its electrical standard and declares the frequency of each clock. Without it the tool places the pins at random and does not know what speed it has to meet."},{"t":"Where it shows up in electronics","d":"Programmable logic devices","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/logica-programable#electronica","x":"07 Where it shows up in electronics Case Why a programmable device Board with a microprocessor A CPLD generates the chip selects and the power-up order of the supplies Software-defined radio The FPGA filters and demodulates in parallel samples that arrive at hundreds of millions per second Cameras and video It receives the sensor, corrects each pixel and converts between interfaces in real time Control of power converters It generates PWM with nanosecond resolution and protections that act within one cycle Integrated-circuit design The design is tested on an FPGA before paying for the ASIC fabrication Equipment updated in the field A new bitstream changes the hardware without changing the board"},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Programmable logic devices","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/logica-programable#laboratorio","x":"08 In the lab Activity 1 · First design on a board With an inexpensive board (those of the iCE40 family work with open-source tools: Yosys, nextpnr and icestorm), describe a counter that makes an LED blink at 1 Hz from the board’s clock. Write the pin file, synthesize, load it and measure the frequency with the oscilloscope. Activity 2 · Reading the resource report Synthesize the sequence detector from the previous unit and note how many LUTs and how many flip-flops it uses. Change the state assignment to one-hot and compare the two figures. Activity 3 · Finding the critical path Synthesize a 32-bit adder between registers and request an ever higher clock frequency until the timing analysis fails. Open the report and locate the path that falls short: it should be the carry chain."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Programmable logic devices","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/logica-programable#errores","x":"09 Common mistakes Forgetting the constraints file. The pins end up wherever the tool wants and the timing analysis does not know what frequency to check. Accepting a design as good because it simulates. If the timing analysis shows paths that fall short, on the board it will fail intermittently. Generating clocks with logic. Dividing the clock with a counter and using that output as the clock of other flip-flops breaks the clock tree; use the PLL or a clock enable. Assuming the FPGA starts instantly. An SRAM FPGA takes time to load its configuration, and in the meantime its pins do not do what the design says. Counting gates instead of LUTs. A two-input AND and a complicated six-input function take up the same thing: one LUT."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Programmable logic devices","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/logica-programable#autoevaluacion","x":"10 Self-assessment Which plane is programmable in a PAL and which in a PROM? In the PAL the AND plane is programmed and the OR plane is fixed; in the PROM the AND plane is fixed (it generates all the minterms) and the OR plane is programmed. How many bits does a six-input LUT have and how many different functions can it implement? \\(2^6 = 64\\) bits, and \\(2^{64}\\), about \\(1.8 \\times 10^{19}\\), different functions. What configuration value (INIT) does a LUT4 loaded with an AND of its four inputs have? Only address 1111, number 15, is 1: the value is 16'h8000. Why does a CPLD have more predictable delays than an FPGA? Because every signal crosses the same central interconnect matrix; in the FPGA the delay depends on where the tool placed each cell and on the wires it chose to join them. What happens to the configuration of an SRAM-based FPGA when the power is cut? It is lost. When power returns, the chip reads the bitstream from an external flash memory, and until it finishes it does not perform the designed function."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Programmable logic devices","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/logica-programable#ampliar","x":"11 Further reading Clive Maxfield. The Design Warrior's Guide to FPGAs. Newnes, 2004. The history of PLDs and the architecture of FPGAs told from the inside, in plain language. Stephen Brown and Zvonko Vranesic. Fundamentals of Digital Logic with VHDL Design. 3rd ed., McGraw-Hill, 2009. PLA and PAL arrays and the structure of LUTs, with the Shannon expansion applied to their synthesis. Pong P. Chu. FPGA Prototyping by Verilog Examples. Wiley, 2008. The complete flow, from HDL to the board, with constraints, timing analysis and real peripherals."},{"t":"The loop poles as a function of the gain","d":"The root locus","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/lugar-raices#idea","x":"01 The loop poles as a function of the gain The Routh criterion tells whether the loop is stable for one gain. The root locus, proposed by Walter Evans in 1948, shows how all the closed-loop poles move as the gain \\( K \\) goes from zero to infinity. They are the points of the s-plane that satisfy the characteristic equation \\( 1 + K\\,G(s) = 0 \\), that is, two conditions: \\[ |K\\,G(s)| = 1 \\] \\[ \\angle G(s) = \\pm 180° (2k + 1) \\] The angle condition defines the locus; the magnitude condition says which gain corresponds to each point. With the locus in view, design consists of choosing K, or modifying G with a compensator, so that the dominant poles fall in the region required by the transient response specifications."},{"t":"Construction rules","d":"The root locus","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/lugar-raices#reglas","x":"02 Construction rules Evans gave rules for drawing it by hand, which remain the best way to understand its shape: It starts at the open-loop poles (K = 0) and ends at the zeros (K → ∞); if there are more poles than zeros, the remaining branches go to infinity. On the real axis, the segments that have an odd number of poles and zeros to their right belong to the locus. The branches that go to infinity follow asymptotes that leave the centroid at fixed angles: \\[ \\sigma_a = \\frac{\\sum p_i - \\sum z_j}{n - m} \\] \\[ \\theta_a = \\frac{180° (2k + 1)}{n - m} \\] \\( n \\) poles and \\( m \\) zeros. With three poles and no zeros, the asymptotes make 60°, 180° and 300°: two branches cross into the right half-plane, and that is why the loop becomes unstable at high gain. Two branches that meet on the real axis separate at the breakaway point, where \\( dK/ds = 0 \\). The crossing of the imaginary axis is obtained with Routh: it is the critical gain. Lab · the root locus The locus of \\( K\\,G(s) \\) with \\( G(s) = 1/(s(s+a)(s+b)) \\), with the option of adding a zero at \\( -z \\). The light-blue dots are the locus for K between 0.01 and 1000; the large ones, the closed-loop poles for the chosen K; the crosses, the open-loop poles, and the circle, the zero. a = b = No zero Zero at −0.5 Zero at −2 Gain K ="},{"t":"Shaping the locus with poles and zeros","d":"The root locus","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/lugar-raices#compensar","x":"03 Shaping the locus with poles and zeros A zero attracts the branches: added to the left of the dominant poles, it bends the locus to the left, moves the asymptotes away from the imaginary axis and allows more gain with more damping. It is the effect of derivative action and of the lead network, which adds a zero and a farther pole. A pole added near the origin, like integral action or the lag network, pushes the branches to the right: it improves the steady-state error at the cost of stability, and so it is accompanied by a very close zero, so that the locus barely changes."},{"t":"In the lab","d":"The root locus","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/lugar-raices#laboratorio","x":"04 In the lab Activity 1 · Locus with software Plot the root locus of the lab loop with the Python control library and verify the breakaway point and the critical gain calculated by hand. Activity 2 · Design by pole placement Choose the gain that gives a damping ratio of 0.5 in the dominant poles and verify the overshoot with a simulation. Activity 3 · Lead zero Add a zero to the loop built with op amps and measure how the critical gain and the overshoot change."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"The root locus","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/lugar-raices#errores","x":"05 Common mistakes Forgetting that the locus is of the closed loop. The poles that move are those of \\( 1 + KG = 0 \\), not those of G. Counting the real-axis segments wrongly. Poles and zeros are counted to the right of the point, not to the left. Using the locus for a parameter that does not multiply the whole gain. The equation has to be rewritten so that it appears as a factor. Ignoring the imperfect cancellation of a pole with a zero. It leaves a slow response of low amplitude."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"The root locus","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/lugar-raices#autoevaluacion","x":"06 Self-assessment Where is the centroid of the asymptotes of K/(s(s+1)(s+5))? \\( (0 - 1 - 5)/3 = -2 \\). What segments of the real axis belong to that locus? From 0 to −1 and from −5 to −∞. Where do the branches of the segment from 0 to −1 separate? At s ≈ −0.47, with K ≈ 1.13. What angles do the asymptotes have if a zero is added? ±90°: two branches go to infinity."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"The root locus","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/lugar-raices#ampliar","x":"07 Further reading Katsuhiko Ogata. Modern Control Engineering. 5th ed., Prentice Hall, 2010 (Spanish edition: “Ingeniería de control moderna”, Pearson). The root locus rules and the design of lead and lag compensators. Walter R. Evans. Control-System Dynamics. McGraw-Hill, 1954. The book by the creator of the method. Gene F. Franklin, J. David Powell and Abbas Emami-Naeini. Feedback Control of Dynamic Systems. 8th ed., Pearson, 2019. Root locus design explained with real examples."},{"t":"What a locus is","d":"Impedance and admittance loci","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/lugares-geometricos#lugar","x":"01 What a locus is The impedance of a circuit is a complex number: a point on the plane. If one of its parameters changes —the frequency, an adjustable resistor, the load on a motor—, that point moves and draws a curve. That curve is the locus of the impedance, and looking at it all at once teaches more than calculating a hundred isolated values: it shows at a glance the complete range of possible behaviors of the circuit. What is remarkable is that, for the most common circuits, these loci are always of two kinds: straight lines or circles. And going from the impedance \\( Z \\) to the admittance \\( Y = 1/Z \\) turns one into the other."},{"t":"Series impedances: straight lines","d":"Impedance and admittance loci","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/lugares-geometricos#rectas","x":"02 Series impedances: straight lines In a series circuit the impedances add, and if only one of the terms changes, the point moves in a single direction. Series circuit Parameter that varies Locus of \\( Z \\) RL \\( R \\), from 0 to ∞ Horizontal half-line at height \\( \\omega L \\) RL \\( \\omega \\), from 0 to ∞ Vertical half-line going up, on \\( \\text{Re}\\,Z = R \\) RC \\( \\omega \\), from 0 to ∞ Vertical half-line rising from \\( -j\\infty \\) up to \\( R \\) RLC \\( \\omega \\), from 0 to ∞ Complete vertical line: it crosses the real axis at resonance In the same way, in a parallel circuit it is the admittances that add, and they are the ones that draw straight lines."},{"t":"Inversion: from lines to circles","d":"Impedance and admittance loci","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/lugares-geometricos#inversion","x":"03 Inversion: from lines to circles Taking the inverse of a complex number is a transformation with a precious geometric property: a straight line that does not pass through the origin becomes a circle that does pass through the origin, and vice versa. Thus, the vertical half-line \\( Z = R + j\\omega L \\) of a series RL becomes, in admittance, a semicircle. \\[ Z = R + jX \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\left|\\,Y - \\frac{1}{2R}\\,\\right| = \\frac{1}{2R} \\] With \\( R \\) fixed and \\( X \\) variable, \\( Y = 1/Z \\) traces the circle centered at \\( 1/2R \\) on the real axis with radius \\( 1/2R \\). For \\( X=0 \\) the point is at \\( Y = 1/R \\), the rightmost end; for \\( X \\to \\pm\\infty \\), at the origin. With \\( X = R \\) it falls exactly at the lowest point of the circle, with conductance and susceptance both equal to \\( 1/2R \\). Since the current is \\( \\mathbf{I} = \\mathbf{V}\\,Y \\), with a fixed voltage the tip of the current phasor traces that same circle, scaled. This is the circle diagram that electrical engineers used for decades to predict the behavior of an induction motor with a compass and straightedge, without a single complex multiplication. Lab · the line and its circle Series RL circuit with \\( R = 100 \\) Ω. You move the frequency: on the left the point \\( Z \\) climbs its line; on the right, \\( Y = 1/Z \\) descends along its semicircle. L = 1 mH L = 10 mH L = 100 mH Frequency = 1.59 kHz"},{"t":"This is where the Smith chart comes from","d":"Impedance and admittance loci","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/lugares-geometricos#smith","x":"04 This is where the Smith chart comes from The Smith chart is the most famous application of this idea. Instead of inverting \\( Z \\), it transforms the normalized impedance with \\( \\Gamma = (z-1)/(z+1) \\), another transformation of the same type —bilinear—, which also maps lines and circles to lines and circles. That is why the lines of constant resistance and constant reactance, which in the \\( Z \\)-plane are straight lines, appear on the chart as the families of circles that make it recognizable. Everything done with the chart —matching an antenna, calculating a stub— is working with transformed loci."},{"t":"Where it shows up in electronics","d":"Impedance and admittance loci","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/lugares-geometricos#electronica","x":"05 Where it shows up in electronics Case Locus in play Impedance matching in RF Moving along circles of the Smith chart by adding series and parallel reactances Induction motor The circle diagram of the current as the slip varies Vector network analyzer Shows the impedance of a device as a curve as a function of frequency Nyquist criterion (control) The locus of \\( H(j\\omega) \\) and its position relative to the point −1 decides stability"},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Impedance and admittance loci","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/lugares-geometricos#laboratorio","x":"06 In the lab Activity 1 · Checking the lowest point For the series RL of 100 Ω and 10 mH, calculate the frequency at which \\( X_L = R \\) and verify with the lab that there \\( Y \\) falls at the lowest point of the semicircle, with \\( G = B = 5 \\) mS. Activity 2 · The locus of a parallel RC Sketch by hand the locus of \\( Y \\) of a parallel RC as \\( \\omega \\) varies, and from it deduce the locus of \\( Z \\) by inversion. Activity 3 · Measuring a locus With a generator and an oscilloscope, measure the magnitude and phase of the current of a series RL at ten frequencies and plot the points on the complex plane. Check that they fall on a circle."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Impedance and admittance loci","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/lugares-geometricos#errores","x":"07 Common mistakes Expecting a straight line in admittance for a series circuit. In series it is the impedances that are straight lines; their inverses are circles. Forgetting that the circle passes through the origin. A straight line that does not pass through the origin always inverts into a circle that does pass through it. Plotting the frequency as if it were an axis. In a locus the parameter is not seen on the axes: it is marked along the curve. Confusing the circle diagram with the Smith chart. They are relatives, but the chart uses a different transformation and is normalized to a characteristic impedance."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Impedance and admittance loci","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/lugares-geometricos#autoevaluacion","x":"08 Self-assessment What locus does \\( Z \\) of a series RL describe as the frequency varies? A vertical half-line on \\( \\text{Re}\\,Z = R \\), toward positive reactances. What does that line become when you go to admittance? A semicircle centered at \\( 1/2R \\) with radius \\( 1/2R \\), which passes through the origin. What property does complex inversion have? It transforms lines and circles into lines and circles; a straight line that does not pass through the origin becomes a circle that does. What is the circle diagram of a motor? The locus of its current as the load varies, which turns out to be a circle because the current is proportional to the admittance. Why do circles appear on the Smith chart? Because the bilinear transformation that defines it takes the lines of constant resistance and constant reactance to circles."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Impedance and admittance loci","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/lugares-geometricos#ampliar","x":"09 Further reading Mac E. Van Valkenburg. Network Analysis. 3rd ed., Prentice Hall, 1974 (Spanish edition, “Análisis de redes”, Limusa). The classic treatment of loci and of inversion in the complex plane. Phillip H. Smith. Electronic Applications of the Smith Chart. 2nd ed., Noble Publishing, 1995. The book by the chart’s author: how the transformation of loci became a design tool. Stephen J. Chapman. Electric Machinery Fundamentals. 5th ed., McGraw-Hill, 2011 (Spanish edition, “Máquinas eléctricas”). The circle diagram of the induction motor, the most important historical application of this unit."},{"t":"Variables and indicators","d":"Macroeconomics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/macroeconomia#variables","x":"01 Variables and indicators Macroeconomics looks at the economy as a whole. Its main variables, and the indicators used to measure them, are: Variable Indicator Output Gross domestic product (GDP), nominal and real; its growth rate Prices Consumer price index (CPI), GDP deflator, inflation rate Employment Labor force participation, employment and unemployment rates External sector Trade balance and balance of payments, exchange rate, reserves Money and credit Monetary base, monetary aggregates, interest rates Public sector Fiscal balance, public debt In Argentina, INDEC (the national statistics and census institute) publishes the national accounts, the CPI and the Permanent Household Survey, from which the employment rates are derived; the Central Bank (BCRA) publishes the monetary and exchange-rate variables."},{"t":"National accounts","d":"Macroeconomics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/macroeconomia#cuentas","x":"02 National accounts GDP is the value of the final goods and services produced in the country in a period. It can be measured in three ways, which must coincide: by adding up the value added of each sector —sales minus inputs—, by adding up the income generated by production —wages, profits, rents, net taxes—, or by adding up the expenditure on final goods: \\[ GDP = C + I + G + X - M \\] Private consumption, investment, government spending, exports and imports. Counting only final goods, or the value added of each stage, avoids double counting the components: the value of a chip is already included in that of the phone. Nominal GDP is valued at the prices of each year; real GDP, at the prices of a base year, and its change measures how much output grew in quantities. The ratio between the two is the deflator. The CPI, on the other hand, measures the cost of a fixed basket of household consumption, and its change is the inflation rate used to adjust wages and contracts."},{"t":"Inflation and the real interest rate","d":"Macroeconomics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/macroeconomia#inflacion","x":"03 Inflation and the real interest rate Rates compound: a monthly inflation \\( \\pi \\) sustained for \\( n \\) months gives a cumulative inflation greater than \\( n\\,\\pi \\). An investment yields in real terms the amount by which its nominal return exceeds inflation, according to the Fisher relation: \\[ 1 + \\pi_{ac} = (1 + \\pi)^n \\] \\[ 1 + r = \\frac{1 + i}{1 + \\pi_{ac}} \\] A nominal annual rate (TNA, in Argentine usage) with monthly compounding yields \\( TNA/12 \\) per month. Only if that monthly rate exceeds the monthly inflation does the investment gain purchasing power. Lab · cumulative inflation and real rate The price index (red) and the capital of a fixed-term deposit renewed every month at the chosen nominal rate (blue), both with base 100. The green line is the purchasing power of 100 pesos kept without investing. Monthly inflation = Nominal annual rate of the fixed-term deposit = Months ="},{"t":"Interpreting economic reality","d":"Macroeconomics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/macroeconomia#interpretacion","x":"04 Interpreting economic reality The economy does not grow evenly: it goes through cycles of expansion and recession. The government tries to smooth them with fiscal policy —public spending and taxes— and the central bank with monetary policy —interest rates and the quantity of money—. Inflation can originate in the issuance of money to finance a deficit, in rising costs such as the exchange rate or energy, or in the inertia of expectations and indexed contracts. Reading the indicators together, with their definitions and sources, and distinguishing data from opinions, is the basis for understanding economic news and for planning a project in a changing context."},{"t":"In the laboratory","d":"Macroeconomics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/macroeconomia#laboratorio","x":"05 In the laboratory Activity 1 · National accounts Download from INDEC the series of GDP at constant prices for the last ten years and calculate its annual growth rate. Activity 2 · Inflation With the monthly CPI changes of the last year, calculate the cumulative inflation and compare it with the simple sum. Activity 3 · Value added Estimate the local value added of an electronic unit assembled in the country with imported components."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Macroeconomics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/macroeconomia#errores","x":"06 Common mistakes Adding up monthly inflation rates. They compound: twelve months at 3 % give 42.6 %, not 36 %. Comparing nominal GDPs of different years. Real GDP has to be used to measure growth. Counting intermediate goods twice. Only final goods or value added count. Judging an investment by its nominal rate. What matters is the real rate."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Macroeconomics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/macroeconomia#autoevaluacion","x":"07 Self-assessment What annual inflation does 3 % monthly give? \\( 1.03^{12} - 1 \\approx 42.6 \\) %. What real rate does a fixed-term deposit at 40 % nominal annual give in that context? It yields 48.2 % nominal over the year; in real terms, \\( 1.482/1.426 - 1 \\approx 3.9 \\) %. What are the components of GDP by the expenditure approach? Consumption, investment, government spending and exports net of imports. What distinguishes real GDP from nominal GDP? Real GDP values output at the prices of a base year; nominal GDP, at current prices."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Macroeconomics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/macroeconomia#ampliar","x":"08 Further reading Olivier Blanchard. Macroeconomics. Pearson. (Spanish edition: “Macroeconomía”.) The reference textbook on output, money, inflation and economic policy. N. Gregory Mankiw. Macroeconomics. Worth Publishers. (Spanish edition: “Macroeconomía”, Antoni Bosch.) National accounts and the basic macroeconomic models. INDEC. Metodologías del sistema de cuentas nacionales y del índice de precios al consumidor. indec.gob.ar. (In Spanish.) How the variables on this page are measured in Argentina."},{"t":"The magnetic field is defined by what it does","d":"Magnetostatics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/magnetostatica#fuerza","x":"01 The magnetic field is defined by what it does There are no magnetic charges: there is no magnetic equivalent of an electron. That is why the magnetic field is not defined by the force on a charge at rest —that one is zero— but by the force on a charge in motion, and that force is perpendicular to everything. \\[ \\vec{F} = q\\,\\vec{v}\\times\\vec{B} \\qquad\\qquad \\vec{F} = I\\,\\vec{L}\\times\\vec{B} \\] The Lorentz force, and its version for a conductor. Being perpendicular to the velocity, the magnetic force never does work: it changes the direction of motion but not the speed or the energy. From the second expression comes every electric motor, and from the first the cathode-ray tube (CRT), the mass spectrometer and the Hall effect. And also a consequence worth keeping in mind: since there are no magnetic charges, the magnetic flux through any closed surface is always zero. \\[ \\oint \\vec{B}\\cdot d\\vec{A} = 0 \\] The second of Maxwell's equations. All magnetic field lines close on themselves: they are neither born nor die anywhere, unlike electric ones."},{"t":"What produces a magnetic field","d":"Magnetostatics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/magnetostatica#fuentes","x":"02 What produces a magnetic field Currents. The Biot–Savart law gives the field of a current element, and Ampère's law does for magnetism what Gauss's law did for electrostatics: it turns an integration problem into a one-line calculation when there is symmetry. \\[ \\oint \\vec{B}\\cdot d\\vec{l} = \\mu_0 I_{enc} \\ \\mu_0 = 4\\pi\\times10^{-7}\\ \\mathrm{T\\,m/A} \\] Configuration Field Where it appears Infinite straight wire \\( B = \\dfrac{\\mu_0 I}{2\\pi r} \\) Any cable; the field that couples into its neighbors Center of a loop \\( B = \\dfrac{\\mu_0 I}{2R} \\) Deflection coils, loop antennas Long solenoid \\( B = \\mu_0 n I \\) Relays, electromagnets, inductors Toroid \\( B = \\dfrac{\\mu_0 N I}{2\\pi r} \\) Power-supply inductors: the field is confined The third row has no distance: inside a long solenoid the field is uniform and depends only on the turns per meter. And the fourth explains why the inductors of switching power supplies are toroidal: in a toroid the field is enclosed inside the core and hardly radiates outward, which is what keeps the inductor from interfering with the rest of the board. Lab · magnetic field of a current Conductors perpendicular to the plane: the dot is current coming out toward you, the cross is going in. Tap anywhere to query the field there. With two conductors the force between them also appears. one conductor two conductors, opposite currents · the loop seen edge-on two conductors, equal currents solenoid, seen edge-on Current = 10 A"},{"t":"The force between conductors","d":"Magnetostatics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/magnetostatica#fuerzas","x":"03 The force between conductors Two parallel conductors attract each other if the currents flow in the same direction and repel if they flow the opposite way. Each one creates a field where the other is, and the other feels the Lorentz force. \\[ \\frac{F}{L} = \\frac{\\mu_0 I_1 I_2}{2\\pi d} \\] With two wires one meter apart carrying one ampere each, the force is \\( 2\\times10^{-7} \\) newton per meter. That number was the definition of the ampere until 2019, when the International System switched to defining it by fixing the charge of the electron. The force is tiny with ordinary currents, but it grows with the square. In a switchboard busbar during a short circuit, tens of thousands of amperes flow, and the forces can reach the point of tearing off the supports: that is why busbars are calculated mechanically and not only by allowable current. The pair of conductors that cancels out If the two currents are equal and opposite and the conductors are very close together, their fields cancel almost completely at a distance. That is what a twisted pair does, and it is why the outgoing and return paths of a current must go together. The practical rule in board design is the same: the return path has to run right next to the outgoing one, because the area the two enclose is what determines how much field they generate and how much they pick up."},{"t":"The Hall effect","d":"Magnetostatics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/magnetostatica#hall","x":"04 The Hall effect In a strip carrying current inside a perpendicular magnetic field, the Lorentz force pushes the carriers to one side until the electric field that builds up stops them. That transverse voltage is the Hall voltage: \\[ V_H = \\frac{IB}{n q t} \\] With \\( n \\) the carrier density and \\( t \\) the thickness. Since \\( n \\) is enormous in a metal and small in a semiconductor, Hall sensors are made of semiconductor: they give a voltage thousands of times greater. The effect serves three purposes at once. It measures magnetic field, which is what a gaussmeter does; it measures current without contact, which is what a DC clamp meter does; and the sign of \\( V_H \\) reveals whether the carriers are negative or positive, which was the first experimental evidence that in a p-type semiconductor conduction is by holes."},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Magnetostatics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/magnetostatica#laboratorio","x":"05 In the lab Activity 1 · Field of a solenoid Measure with a Hall probe the field on the axis of a solenoid for several currents. Verify the proportionality with \\( I \\) and compare the value with \\( \\mu_0 n I \\). Then measure how it falls off beyond the end. Activity 2 · The current balance Hang a rigid conductor from a balance inside the air gap of a magnet and measure the force against the current. Obtain \\( B \\) from the slope and compare with the value measured with the probe. Activity 3 · Magnetic shielding Measure the field of a magnet through sheets of aluminum, steel and mu-metal. Explain why aluminum shields nothing and steel does, and relate it to the permeability of each material."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Magnetostatics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/magnetostatica#errores","x":"06 Common mistakes Believing the magnetic force does work. It is perpendicular to the velocity: it does not change the kinetic energy. Looking for an isolated magnetic north pole. It does not exist; the closed flux always gives zero. Confusing \\( B \\) with \\( H \\). \\( B \\) is the induction, \\( H \\) the excitation; in vacuum they differ by \\( \\mu_0 \\). Using the solenoid formula near the end. It holds for the interior of a long solenoid; at the mouth the field drops to half. Separating the outgoing path from the return of a current. The enclosed area is what generates and picks up field. Shielding a magnetic field with aluminum. For DC it does not work: you need a high-permeability material."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Magnetostatics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/magnetostatica#autoevaluacion","x":"07 Self-assessment Why does the magnetic force do no work? Because it is always perpendicular to the velocity: it changes the direction, not the speed. What is the magnetic flux through a closed surface? Zero, always. There are no magnetic charges and the lines close on themselves. What does the field inside a long solenoid depend on? Only on the turns per meter and the current: \\( B = \\mu_0 n I \\). Not on the distance from the axis. Why are power-supply inductors toroidal? Because the toroid confines the field inside the core and hardly radiates to the rest of the board. Why are Hall sensors made of semiconductor? Because their carrier density is much lower than that of a metal, and the Hall voltage turns out thousands of times greater. What force do two wires 1 m apart carrying 1 A exert on each other? \\( 2\\times10^{-7} \\) N per meter. It was the definition of the ampere until 2019."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Magnetostatics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/magnetostatica#ampliar","x":"08 Further reading Raymond A. Serway and John W. Jewett. Physics for Scientists and Engineers with Modern Physics. 10th ed., Cengage, 2019 (Spanish edition, “Física para ciencias e ingeniería”). Magnetic field, Lorentz force, Biot–Savart and Ampère's law, with the Hall effect worked out. Edward M. Purcell and David J. Morin. Electricity and Magnetism. 3rd ed., Cambridge University Press, 2016 (Spanish edition, “Electricidad y magnetismo”). Magnetism deduced from electrostatics plus relativity, which is the most illuminating approach there is. Henry W. Ott. Electromagnetic Compatibility Engineering. Wiley, 2009. Why the return path matters, the twisted pair and magnetic shielding in real equipment."},{"t":"Analytic, and what comes with it","d":"Analytic functions and conformal mapping","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/mapeo-conforme#analitica","x":"01 Analytic, and what comes with it To recap: \\( f \\) is analytic on a domain if it is differentiable in the complex sense at all of its points, which by Cauchy-Riemann is equivalent to \\( u \\) and \\( v \\) being tied to each other. That requirement, which at first seems technical, brings very concrete geometric consequences, and from it comes the tool most used at high frequency. The key is to look at the first-order approximation around a point: \\[ f(z) \\approx f(z_0) + f'(z_0)\\,(z - z_0) \\] Near \\( z_0 \\), every analytic function behaves like multiplication by a fixed complex number. And multiplying by a complex number \\( f'(z_0) = \\rho\\,e^{j\\alpha} \\) is one single thing: scaling by \\( \\rho \\) and rotating by \\( \\alpha \\). It does not stretch more in one direction than another, does not shear, does not reflect. That is why a small figure, once transformed, comes out the same as itself apart from size and orientation."},{"t":"Preserving angles","d":"Analytic functions and conformal mapping","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/mapeo-conforme#conforme","x":"02 Preserving angles The central result follows from the previous approximation. If two curves cross at \\( z_0 \\) forming an angle \\( \\varphi \\), their images cross forming the same angle \\( \\varphi \\), and in the same sense of rotation. A mapping with that property is called conformal. \\[ f'(z_0) = \\rho\\,e^{j\\alpha} \\;\\Longrightarrow\\; \\begin{cases} \\text{each tangent rotates by } \\alpha \\\\[2pt] \\text{each length is multiplied by } \\rho \\end{cases} \\] Both tangents rotate by the same amount, so the difference between them —the angle— does not change. And since all lengths are multiplied by the same \\( \\rho \\), small figures keep their shape. Large ones do not: the approximation holds only on a neighborhood, and at the scale of the whole plane an analytic function deforms as much as it likes. Lab · the angle, before and after Two rays leave the chosen point at the angles set below. On the right, what they turn into. Compare the angle between them on one side and on the other, and shrink the size to watch the small square become a square. f(z) = z² f(z) = e^z f(z) = 1/z f(z) = (z−1)/(z+1) · the Smith chart f(z) = z³ − 3z f(z) = z̄ · not analytic Show the small test square First ray = 20 ° Second ray = 80 ° Size = 0.40 The conjugate also preserves the angle, and it does not qualify \\( f(z) = \\bar{z} \\) keeps the absolute value of the angle between curves, but it reverses its direction: it is a reflection. This is called anticonformal, and it is not the same thing. The definitio"},{"t":"Where it stops working: critical points","d":"Analytic functions and conformal mapping","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/mapeo-conforme#criticos","x":"03 Where it stops working: critical points The whole argument rested on \\( f'(z_0) \\ne 0 \\). If the derivative vanishes, the first-order approximation says nothing and you have to go to the first term that survives: \\[ f(z) - f(z_0) \\approx \\frac{f^{(m)}(z_0)}{m!}\\,(z - z_0)^m \\;\\Longrightarrow\\; \\text{angles are multiplied by } m \\] With \\( w = z^2 \\) at the origin, \\( m = 2 \\): two curves that crossed at 45° come out crossing at 90°. You can see this in the lab by moving the point to zero. The points where \\( f' \\) vanishes are called critical, and they are exactly the places where the mapping stops being conformal. The function \\( f(z) = z^3 - 3z \\) in the lab has two: \\( f'(z) = 3z^2 - 3 \\) vanishes at \\( z = \\pm 1 \\). Moving the point there shows the fold."},{"t":"What it is used for","d":"Analytic functions and conformal mapping","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/mapeo-conforme#aplicaciones","x":"04 What it is used for Application What is transformed What is gained Smith chart \\( \\Gamma = \\dfrac{z-1}{z+1} \\) takes the right half-plane to the unit disk The whole impedance plane fits on one sheet, and the circles of \\( r \\) and of \\( x \\) remain circles Nyquist criterion The Nyquist contour, mapped by \\( 1 + L(s) \\) Counting encirclements of −1 tells how many unstable poles the closed loop has Fields and capacitances A complicated geometry, taken to a rectangle or a half-plane Laplace is solved in the simple geometry and the solution is brought back Microstrip Schwarz-Christoffel, which maps the half-plane onto the interior of a polygon Gives the closed-form characteristic impedance formulas used by design software Filter stability \\( z = e^{sT} \\), from the \\( s \\)-plane to the \\( z \\)-plane The left half-plane becomes the interior of the unit circle The last row will be seen in detail when we reach the Z-transform, but it is worth noting now: the stability criterion for a digital filter —poles inside the circle— is the usual criterion —poles on the left— seen through a mapping. The argument principle, in one line If you traverse a closed curve in the \\( s \\)-plane and watch how its image winds around the origin, the number of turns is \\( Z - P \\): the zeros minus the poles that ended up inside. Nyquist is that count applied to \\( 1 + L(s) \\), and the calculus of residues is the same idea carried over to integrals. Everything left in the first half of the course comes "},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Analytic functions and conformal mapping","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/mapeo-conforme#laboratorio","x":"05 In the lab Activity 1 · Measuring the scale factor For \\( f(z) = z^2 \\) at \\( z_0 = 1 + j \\), compute \\( f'(z_0) = 2 + j2 \\), its modulus \\( 2\\sqrt{2} \\approx 2.83 \\) and its argument 45°. Transform a square of side 0.1 centered there and verify with a ruler and protractor that the result measures 0.283 on a side and is rotated 45°. Activity 2 · The fold at a critical point With \\( f(z) = z^3 - 3z \\), move the lab point to \\( z = 1 \\) and check that two rays 60° apart come out 120° apart. Verify analytically that \\( f'(1) = 0 \\) and that the first nonzero derivative is the second. Activity 3 · Nyquist by hand For \\( L(s) = \\dfrac{K}{s(s+1)(s+2)} \\), evaluate \\( L(j\\omega) \\) at about ten frequencies, plot the result in the complex plane and find the value of \\( K \\) that makes the curve pass through −1. Compare it with the one given by the Routh criterion."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Analytic functions and conformal mapping","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/mapeo-conforme#errores","x":"06 Common mistakes Believing that conformal means “does not deform.” It preserves angles and the shape of small figures; at large scale it deforms as much as it needs to. Forgetting the condition \\( f'(z_0) \\ne 0 \\). At critical points the angles are multiplied. Taking the conjugate to be conformal. It preserves the angle but reverses its direction: it is anticonformal. Confusing the scale factor with the circuit gain. \\( |f'(z_0)| \\) is a local stretching of the plane, not an amplitude. Using the first-order approximation far from the point. It holds on a neighborhood, and the lab shows how quickly it breaks down as the size is increased."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Analytic functions and conformal mapping","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/mapeo-conforme#autoevaluacion","x":"07 Self-assessment What two things does \\( f'(z_0) \\) do locally? Its modulus scales and its argument rotates. Near \\( z_0 \\), the function is that multiplication and nothing more. Why are angles preserved? Because all the tangents rotate by the same \\( \\arg f'(z_0) \\); the difference between any two of them does not change. What happens at a point where \\( f' = 0 \\)? Angles are multiplied by \\( m \\), the order of the first nonzero derivative. With \\( z^2 \\) at the origin, they are doubled. Where does \\( f(z) = z^3 - 3z \\) have critical points? At \\( z = \\pm 1 \\), where \\( f'(z) = 3z^2 - 3 \\) vanishes. What transformation is behind the Smith chart? The bilinear transformation \\( \\Gamma = (z-1)/(z+1) \\), which takes the right half-plane to the unit disk while preserving circles and angles. What does the argument principle count? The turns that the image of a contour makes around the origin: they equal \\( Z - P \\), zeros minus enclosed poles. It is the basis of Nyquist and of residues."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Analytic functions and conformal mapping","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/mapeo-conforme#ampliar","x":"08 Further reading Ruel V. Churchill and James W. Brown. Complex Variables and Applications. 7th ed. (Spanish edition, “Variable compleja y aplicaciones”), McGraw-Hill, 2004. The chapters on conformal mapping and its applications to Laplace’s equation, with the classic examples worked out. David M. Pozar. Microwave Engineering. 4th ed., Wiley, 2011. The Smith chart derived as a bilinear transformation, and the use of mapping in planar lines. Katsuhiko Ogata. Modern Control Engineering. 5th ed. (Spanish edition, “Ingeniería de control moderna”), Pearson, 2010. The Nyquist criterion presented as a contour mapping, which is exactly what it is."},{"t":"The real transformer","d":"Transformers and AC machines","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/maquinas-ca#transformador","x":"01 The real transformer The ideal transformer, seen in coupled circuits, relates voltages by the turns ratio and conserves power. The real one has losses and flux that does not link the two windings, and it is modeled with an equivalent circuit: the resistances of the windings and their leakage reactances in series, and a parallel branch that represents the core, with a resistance for the iron losses and a reactance for the magnetizing current. Test How it is done What it gives No-load Rated voltage on one winding, the other open The iron losses and the magnetizing branch Short-circuit Secondary short-circuited, reduced voltage until rated current The copper losses and the series impedance; the short-circuit voltage in % The iron losses are almost constant and the copper losses grow with the square of the current: the efficiency is maximum when the two are equal, and in a power transformer it exceeds 98%. Installation and basic calculation are covered in transformers, in the technician program."},{"t":"The rotating field","d":"Transformers and AC machines","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/maquinas-ca#campo-rotante","x":"02 The rotating field Three windings displaced by 120° in the stator, fed with three-phase currents shifted by 120°, produce a magnetic field of constant amplitude that rotates. Its speed, the synchronous speed, depends only on the frequency and the number of pole pairs \\( p \\): \\[ n_s = \\frac{60 f}{p} \\] In rpm. At 50 Hz: 3000 rpm with one pole pair, 1500 with two, 1000 with three. Swapping two phases reverses the direction of rotation."},{"t":"The asynchronous machine","d":"Transformers and AC machines","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/maquinas-ca#asincronica","x":"03 The asynchronous machine In the induction motor, the rotating field induces currents in the rotor —short-circuited bars, the “squirrel cage”— and those currents produce the torque. If the rotor turned at the synchronous speed there would be no relative motion, no induction and no torque: the rotor always turns a little slower. That relative difference is the slip: \\[ s = \\frac{n_s - n}{n_s} \\] \\[ T = \\frac{3\\, V^2\\, R_2'/s}{\\omega_s\\left[(R_1 + R_2'/s)^2 + (X_1 + X_2')^2\\right]} \\] The torque comes from the per-phase equivalent circuit, which is that of a transformer whose secondary has a load \\( R_2'/s \\) that depends on the speed. At rated operation the slip is 2 to 5%. The torque curve as a function of speed has a starting torque, rises to a maximum torque and falls to zero at the synchronous speed. The useful region is the one to the right of the maximum, almost straight: if the load increases, the motor slows a little and produces more torque. To the left of the maximum the operation is unstable. In direct starting the current reaches 6 or 8 times the rated current, which is why wye-delta starting, soft starters and drives are used. Lab · the operating point of an induction motor A 5.5 kW, four-pole motor, wye-connected at 380 V (220 V per phase), with R₁ = 0.8 Ω, R₂' = 0.75 Ω, X₁ = 1.6 Ω, X₂' = 1.8 Ω and X m = 40 Ω at 50 Hz. A drive changes the frequency while keeping V/f constant. The dot marks where the motor curve crosses the load curve. Constant-torque load (conv"},{"t":"The synchronous machine","d":"Transformers and AC machines","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/maquinas-ca#sincronica","x":"04 The synchronous machine In the synchronous machine the rotor has its own field, from a winding fed with DC or from permanent magnets, and turns locked to the rotating field, exactly at the synchronous speed. Almost all the electrical energy in the world is generated by synchronous alternators in power plants. As a motor, by changing the excitation current the power factor is controlled: overexcited, it delivers reactive power, like a capacitor. Permanent-magnet synchronous motors, controlled by electronics, are those of electric vehicles and of many modern appliances."},{"t":"Variable-frequency drive control","d":"Transformers and AC machines","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/maquinas-ca#variador","x":"05 Variable-frequency drive control Since the synchronous speed is proportional to the frequency, a drive that generates three-phase power of variable frequency controls the speed of the induction motor. To keep the flux in the core, the voltage has to drop in the same proportion: this is constant V/f control. At low frequencies the drop across the stator resistance weighs more and the maximum torque falls; drives compensate with a voltage boost. The drive circuits are covered in variable-speed drives, in the technician program."},{"t":"In the laboratory","d":"Transformers and AC machines","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/maquinas-ca#laboratorio","x":"06 In the laboratory Activity 1 · Transformer tests Perform the no-load and short-circuit tests of a single-phase transformer and calculate its equivalent circuit and its full-load efficiency. Activity 2 · Slip With a tachometer, measure the speed of an induction motor at no load and with increasing load on a brake, and calculate the slip in each case. Activity 3 · Drive Run the same motor with a drive between 10 and 50 Hz and measure the current and the speed with a constant load."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Transformers and AC machines","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/maquinas-ca#errores","x":"07 Common mistakes Expecting an induction motor to turn at the synchronous speed. Without slip there is no torque. Starting a large motor directly on a weak grid. The starting current drags the voltage down. Running a motor at low speed for a long time with its own fan. It is poorly cooled. Confusing shaft power with electrical power. A motor’s nameplate gives the mechanical output power."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Transformers and AC machines","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/maquinas-ca#autoevaluacion","x":"08 Self-assessment What synchronous speed does a 6-pole motor have at 50 Hz? \\( 60 \\cdot 50 / 3 = 1000 \\) rpm. A 4-pole motor turns at 1440 rpm at 50 Hz. What is the slip? \\( (1500 - 1440)/1500 = 4 \\)%. What voltage must a drive give at 25 Hz for a 380 V, 50 Hz motor, with constant V/f? 190 V. When is a transformer’s efficiency at its maximum? When the copper losses equal the iron losses."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Transformers and AC machines","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/maquinas-ca#ampliar","x":"09 Further reading Stephen J. Chapman. Electric Machinery Fundamentals (Spanish edition: “Máquinas eléctricas”). 5th ed., McGraw-Hill, 2012. The course text: transformers and induction, synchronous and DC machines. Jesús Fraile Mora. Máquinas eléctricas (in Spanish). 8th ed., Garceta, 2016. A complete treatise in Spanish, with many solved problems. A. E. Fitzgerald, Charles Kingsley and Stephen D. Umans. Electric Machinery. 7th ed., McGraw-Hill, 2014. The classic, with the field approach and the models for control."},{"t":"The easiest motor to control","d":"DC machines and brushless motors","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/maquinas-cc#principio","x":"01 The easiest motor to control In a DC motor, the rotor conductors carry current inside the stator field and experience a force. A segmented commutator with brushes reverses the current in each coil just as it passes from one pole to the other, so that the torque always pushes in the same direction. As they turn, the same conductors generate a back EMF proportional to the speed. Two equations describe the whole motor: \\[ V = R\\,I + k\\,\\omega \\] \\[ T = k\\,I \\] In steady state. The same constant \\( k \\) links the back EMF to the speed (V·s/rad) and the torque to the current (N·m/A). The voltage sets the speed; the load sets the current. This simplicity made the DC motor the favorite for speed and position control for a century, and it remains in millions of small applications: toys, battery-powered tools, windshield wipers."},{"t":"Forms of excitation","d":"DC machines and brushless motors","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/maquinas-cc#excitacion","x":"02 Forms of excitation Excitation Behavior Use Permanent magnet Constant flux: speed almost proportional to voltage Small motors, servos, tools Separately excited or shunt Like the magnet type, with the flux adjustable through the field Older machine tools, rolling mills Series The same winding carries the field and armature current: very high starting torque, and the speed runs away at no load Traction, starters for combustion engines, universal motors in appliances Compound Combines series and shunt Cranes, older elevators"},{"t":"Curves, control and efficiency","d":"DC machines and brushless motors","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/maquinas-cc#curvas","x":"03 Curves, control and efficiency Solving, the speed falls in a straight line with torque, from the no-load speed \\( V/k \\) to zero at the stall torque \\( kV/R \\). Changing the voltage shifts the line in parallel: that is why the speed is controlled with an H-bridge that applies the supply voltage modulated by pulse width, with an average value proportional to the duty cycle. The efficiency depends on the operating point and has a maximum: \\[ \\eta_{max} = \\left(1 - \\sqrt{\\frac{I_0}{I_{stall}}}\\right)^2 \\] \\( I_0 \\) is the no-load current, which overcomes friction, and \\( I_{stall} = V/R \\) the current with the rotor locked. The maximum occurs at \\( I = \\sqrt{I_0\\, I_{stall}} \\), well before the stall torque: a DC motor is most efficient when only lightly loaded. Lab · the operating point of a DC motor A permanent-magnet motor with R = 1.2 Ω, k = 0.05 N·m/A and a friction of 5 mN·m, fed by an H-bridge. The gray lines are the characteristic at 6, 12, 18 and 24 V; the blue one, at the chosen voltage. The dot is the operating point at the load torque. Average applied voltage = Load torque ="},{"t":"Brushless motors","d":"DC machines and brushless motors","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/maquinas-cc#bldc","x":"04 Brushless motors Brushes wear out, produce sparks and interference, and limit the speed. The brushless motor (BLDC) turns the construction inside out: the magnets go on the rotor and the windings on the stator, and the commutation is done by an electronic circuit that knows the rotor position from Hall-effect sensors or from the back EMF of the phase that is not conducting. With six-step commutation, the behavior is that of a DC motor; with vector control and sinusoidal currents (permanent-magnet synchronous motor), the torque is smoother and the efficiency higher. Brushed Brushless Control A DC voltage or an H-bridge A three-phase inverter with position feedback Service life Limited by the brushes Limited by the bearings Typical efficiency 60 to 80% 85 to 95% Applications Toys, inexpensive tools, auto parts Drones, disk drives, computer fans, electric vehicles"},{"t":"In the laboratory","d":"DC machines and brushless motors","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/maquinas-cc#laboratorio","x":"05 In the laboratory Activity 1 · Measuring k and R Measure the winding resistance with the rotor locked and the constant k as the generated voltage divided by the speed, spinning the motor with another one. Activity 2 · PWM control Control a motor with an H-bridge and a microcontroller, and measure the speed as a function of duty cycle with and without load. Activity 3 · A BLDC Observe with the oscilloscope the three phases and the Hall signals of a computer fan or a drone motor with its controller."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"DC machines and brushless motors","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/maquinas-cc#errores","x":"06 Common mistakes Sizing the H-bridge for the rated current. At startup the stall current flows, V/R. Running a series motor without load. The speed runs away and can destroy it. Forgetting the diode or the freewheeling path. The winding inductance generates overvoltages when switching off. Braking a DC motor abruptly. It acts as a generator and returns energy to the supply."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"DC machines and brushless motors","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/maquinas-cc#autoevaluacion","x":"07 Self-assessment What no-load speed does a motor with k = 0.05 V·s/rad have at 12 V? 240 rad/s, about 2290 rpm. What stall torque does the same motor give with R = 1.2 Ω? \\( kV/R = 0.05 \\cdot 12/1.2 = 0.5 \\) N·m, with 10 A. What maximum efficiency does it have if the no-load current is 0.1 A? \\( (1 - \\sqrt{0.1/10})^2 = 0.81 \\): 81%. How does a BLDC know when to commutate? From the rotor position, measured with Hall sensors or estimated from the back EMF."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"DC machines and brushless motors","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/maquinas-cc#ampliar","x":"08 Further reading Stephen J. Chapman. Electric Machinery Fundamentals (Spanish edition: “Máquinas eléctricas”). 5th ed., McGraw-Hill, 2012. DC machines with their different excitations and their curves. Austin Hughes and Bill Drury. Electric Motors and Drives. 5th ed., Newnes, 2019. Motors seen from the electronic drive, with DC, induction and brushless. Padmaraja Yedamale. Brushless DC (BLDC) Motor Fundamentals (AN885). Microchip, 2003. The classic application note on the commutation of brushless motors."},{"t":"Synchronous design","d":"Sequential logic: state machines and timing","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/maquinas-estado#sincronico","x":"01 Synchronous design Flip-flops, counters and shift registers are covered in the page on sequential logic in the Electronics Technician program. This unit goes up one level: how any sequential system is designed and what limits it. Almost all modern digital logic is synchronous: a single clock marks the instants at which all the flip-flops take their new value, and between one edge and the next the combinational logic has time to settle. That discipline solves the hazard problem of the previous unit in one stroke: glitches still occur, but the flip-flop looks at its input only right at the edge, when it has already settled. \\[ \\text{next state} = f(\\text{current state},\\ \\text{inputs}) \\] A register holds the state; combinational logic computes the next one. Every synchronous circuit, from the simplest counter to the processor, has this form."},{"t":"State machines: Moore and Mealy","d":"Sequential logic: state machines and timing","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/maquinas-estado#fsm","x":"02 State machines: Moore and Mealy A finite state machine describes a sequential circuit as a set of states and of transitions between them. There are two ways to produce the outputs, and the difference matters: Moore Mealy The output depends on The state only The state and the current input In the diagram, the output goes Inside the state On the transition When it responds One cycle later In the same cycle States needed Usually needs a few more Fewer The output can have glitches No: it comes from flip-flops Yes: it passes through the input logic Lab · a 1011 sequence detector The machine receives one bit per clock cycle and sets the output to 1 when the last four bits were 1011 (sequences may overlap). You choose Moore or Mealy and feed it bits one at a time; the active state is highlighted in the diagram and the table keeps the last cycles. Moore machine (5 states) Mealy machine (4 states) input 0 input 1 reset load 1011011"},{"t":"From the diagram to the circuit","d":"Sequential logic: state machines and timing","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/maquinas-estado#diseno","x":"03 From the diagram to the circuit The diagram is translated into a state table, and each state receives a binary code. The choice of that code —the state assignment — changes the resulting circuit: Encoding Flip-flops for N states When it is a good choice Binary \\( \\lceil \\log_2 N \\rceil \\) When flip-flops are expensive, as in discrete logic Gray \\( \\lceil \\log_2 N \\rceil \\) If the transitions follow a sequence: one bit changes at a time One-hot \\( N \\) In FPGAs: flip-flops are plentiful and the next-state logic ends up minimal and fast With the encoding chosen, each bit of the next state and each output are combinational functions of the state bits and the inputs, and they are minimized exactly as in the previous unit."},{"t":"Timing: the maximum frequency","d":"Sequential logic: state machines and timing","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/maquinas-estado#temporizacion","x":"04 Timing: the maximum frequency Between two clock edges, the data has to leave the first flip-flop, cross the logic and reach the second with enough time to spare for it to capture the data. That race sets the maximum frequency of the whole system. \\[ T_{clk} \\geq t_{cq} + t_{\\text{logic}} + t_{su} \\] \\[ f_{max} = \\frac{1}{t_{cq} + t_{\\text{logic},\\,max} + t_{su}} \\] \\( t_{cq} \\) is the clock-to-output delay of the flip-flop; \\( t_{su} \\), the setup time that the data has to be steady before the edge. With 1 ns, 6 ns and 0.5 ns the minimum period is 7.5 ns: 133 MHz. Speeding up the system means shortening the critical path. There is also a condition in the opposite direction: the data cannot change too soon after the edge, during the hold time (the hold window). And if the clock does not reach all the flip-flops at the same time —the difference is called clock skew — both margins shrink."},{"t":"Metastability: when the input does not respect the clock","d":"Sequential logic: state machines and timing","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/maquinas-estado#metaestabilidad","x":"05 Metastability: when the input does not respect the clock A signal that comes from outside —a pushbutton, another circuit with its own clock— can change right inside the setup-and-hold window. The flip-flop is then left in a metastable state: neither 0 nor 1, like a ball balanced on top of a hill, and it takes a random time to fall to one side. If the following logic reads it before then, it may read it differently in two places of the circuit and the system fails in a way that is impossible to reproduce. The probability that it is still undecided decays exponentially with the time it is given to resolve. From this comes the mean time between failures: \\[ MTBF = \\frac{e^{\\,t_r/\\tau}}{T_0\\, f_{clk}\\, f_{data}} \\] \\( t_r \\) is the available resolution time; \\( \\tau \\) and \\( T_0 \\), constants of the flip-flop technology. The exponential is the key: each extra \\( \\tau \\) of time multiplies the MTBF by \\( e \\). Lab · the two-flip-flop synchronizer An asynchronous input that changes on average one million times per second enters a 100 MHz system, with flip-flops of \\( \\tau = 50 \\) ps and \\( T_0 = 1 \\) ns (illustrative values, on the order of fast logic). You choose how many flip-flops in a chain the synchronizer uses and how much resolution time each one leaves. 1 flip-flop (no synchronizer) 2 flip-flops in a chain 3 flip-flops in a chain Resolution time per stage = 1.0 ns That is why no asynchronous signal enters a synchronous design without first passing through a synchronizer"},{"t":"Where it shows up in electronics","d":"Sequential logic: state machines and timing","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/maquinas-estado#electronica","x":"06 Where it shows up in electronics Case What is used from this unit Controller of a serial protocol (UART, I²C) A state machine that steps through the bits of each frame Pushbutton on a device Two-flip-flop synchronizer plus debouncing before it enters the logic FPGA synthesis report The tool computes the critical path and the maximum frequency reached Passing data between two clock domains Synchronizers and FIFO memories with Gray-code pointers"},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Sequential logic: state machines and timing","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/maquinas-estado#laboratorio","x":"07 In the lab Activity 1 · Designing the detector Turn the lab’s Moore machine into a state table, encode it in binary and in one-hot, and compare how many flip-flops and gates each version needs. Activity 2 · Moore versus Mealy on the oscilloscope Implement both versions on a development board and observe with two channels when the output of each one goes high relative to the clock edge on which the last 1 arrives. Activity 3 · Maximum frequency With the timings from the synthesis report of a design of your own, verify the maximum frequency by hand with the critical-path formula."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Sequential logic: state machines and timing","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/maquinas-estado#errores","x":"08 Common mistakes Using a Mealy output as a clock or an enable. It can have glitches: it passes through the input logic. Forgetting the initial state. Every machine needs a reset that takes it to a known state. Reading an asynchronous input directly. Without a synchronizer, metastability is a matter of time. Believing a synchronizer eliminates metastability. It makes it improbable, not impossible. Adding up only the logic delays. The minimum period includes \\( t_{cq} \\) and \\( t_{su} \\)."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Sequential logic: state machines and timing","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/maquinas-estado#autoevaluacion","x":"09 Self-assessment What distinguishes a Moore machine from a Mealy machine? In Moore the output depends only on the state; in Mealy, on the state and the current input, so it responds one cycle earlier. Why is one-hot encoding used in FPGAs? Because they have plenty of flip-flops, and so the next-state logic ends up minimal and fast. What limits the maximum frequency of a synchronous circuit? The critical path: \\( t_{cq} + t_{\\text{logic}} + t_{su} \\) between two flip-flops. What is metastability? The undefined state a flip-flop is left in when its input changes during the setup-and-hold window; it resolves in a random time. Why does a second flip-flop improve the MTBF so much? Because it gives the first one an extra period to resolve, and the MTBF grows exponentially with that time."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Sequential logic: state machines and timing","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/maquinas-estado#ampliar","x":"10 Further reading John F. Wakerly. Digital Design: Principles and Practices. 5th ed., Pearson, 2018. State machines, timing and the best introductory chapter on metastability and synchronizers. William J. Dally and R. Curtis Harting. Digital Design: A Systems Approach. Cambridge University Press, 2012. Synchronous design from the viewpoint of someone who designs processors, with the timing margins in detail. Clifford E. Cummings. Clock Domain Crossing (CDC) Design & Verification Techniques Using SystemVerilog. SNUG Boston, 2008. The industry’s reference paper on how to pass signals between clock domains."},{"t":"The environment in the Constitution","d":"Environmental legal framework","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/marco-legal-ambiental#constitucion","x":"01 The environment in the Constitution The 1994 reform incorporated Article 41: all inhabitants have the right to a healthy, balanced environment fit for human development, and the duty to preserve it; environmental damage generates, as a priority, the obligation to restore. The same article divides the task: the Nation enacts the minimum-standards protection rules, and the provinces complement them, without being able to lower that floor. It also prohibits the entry into the country of hazardous and radioactive waste. Two other articles complete the framework: Article 43 allows collective amparo actions (constitutional protection suits) in defense of the environment, and Article 124 recognizes the provinces’ original ownership of their natural resources. The structure of the regulatory system was covered in the Argentine regulatory system."},{"t":"The minimum-standards laws","d":"Environmental legal framework","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/marco-legal-ambiental#minimos","x":"02 The minimum-standards laws The General Environmental Law, 25,675, of 2002, is the foundation: it sets out the principles of environmental policy, among them prevention, precaution, intergenerational equity and responsibility; the management instruments, such as land-use planning, environmental impact assessment and access to information; environmental insurance for hazardous activities and the regime of collective environmental damage. Other minimum-standards laws deal with specific subjects: Law Year Subject 25,612 2002 Integrated management of industrial and service-activity waste 25,688 2002 Environmental management of waters 25,831 2004 Free access to public environmental information 25,916 2004 Management of household waste 26,331 2007 Protection of native forests 26,639 2010 Preservation of glaciers and the periglacial environment 27,520 2019 Climate change adaptation and mitigation Law 24,051 on hazardous waste, of 1991, which predates the reform, remains central for industry: it creates the registry of generators, transporters and operators, and the manifest that accompanies each waste from the moment it is generated until its treatment. As always with rules, the current text must be consulted in InfoLEG."},{"t":"The provincial and municipal level","d":"Environmental legal framework","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/marco-legal-ambiental#provincial","x":"03 The provincial and municipal level Each province has its own environmental law and its enforcement authority, which defines the impact assessment procedure and grants the permits: for example, Law 11,723 in the province of Buenos Aires, 11,717 in Santa Fe and 10,208 in Córdoba. The municipalities regulate the licensing of establishments, zoning and the management of urban waste. A specific project almost always needs permits from all three levels."},{"t":"The international level","d":"Environmental legal framework","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/marco-legal-ambiental#internacional","x":"04 The international level The 1972 Stockholm Conference inaugurated international environmental law, and the 1992 Rio Summit gave it its principles, among them the precautionary principle, and the conventions on climate change and biodiversity. The agreements that Argentina approved by law rank above ordinary laws: Agreement Year Subject Montreal Protocol 1987 Substances that deplete the ozone layer Basel Convention 1989 Transboundary movements of hazardous waste Stockholm Convention 2001 Persistent organic pollutants Minamata Convention 2013 Mercury Paris Agreement 2015 Climate change Escazú Agreement 2018 Access to information, participation and environmental justice in Latin America Cases · which environmental rules apply? Situations in which an electronic engineer encounters environmental law. When you choose one, the rules that come into play appear, and at which level. This is general guidance: the current text of each rule and the authority of each jurisdiction decide."},{"t":"In the laboratory","d":"Environmental legal framework","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/marco-legal-ambiental#laboratorio","x":"05 In the laboratory Activity 1 · The provincial law Look up the environmental law of the province where you study and its impact assessment procedure. Identify which activities require it and how public participation is carried out. Activity 2 · Information request Draft an environmental information request under Law 25,831 about the waste management of a public agency. Activity 3 · The manifest Review a model hazardous waste manifest and follow the path of a board-etching waste from the workshop to the operator."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Environmental legal framework","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/marco-legal-ambiental#errores","x":"06 Common mistakes Believing that a provincial law can be less demanding than the national one. The minimum standards are a floor. Thinking that the impact assessment is national. Each province sets the procedure. Pouring etching or soldering waste down the sewer. They are hazardous waste with their own circuit. Citing laws without checking their current text. Environmental rules are regulated and amended frequently."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Environmental legal framework","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/marco-legal-ambiental#autoevaluacion","x":"07 Self-assessment Which article of the Constitution establishes the right to a healthy environment? Article 41. What are minimum standards? The national environmental protection rules that set a common floor; the provinces may be more demanding, not less. What document accompanies a hazardous waste from the moment it is generated until its treatment? The manifest, under Law 24,051. Which convention regulates the export of hazardous waste? The Basel Convention."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Environmental legal framework","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/marco-legal-ambiental#ampliar","x":"08 Further reading Law 25,675. Ley General del Ambiente (General Environmental Law) (in Spanish). Updated text in InfoLEG. The principles, the management instruments and the regime of environmental damage. Néstor A. Cafferatta (ed.). Tratado jurisprudencial y doctrinario de derecho ambiental (Treatise of case law and doctrine on environmental law) (in Spanish). La Ley, 2012. The doctrine and the main rulings of Argentine environmental law. United Nations Environment Programme. Texts of the Basel, Stockholm and Minamata conventions. UNEP. The international agreements on hazardous waste and substances."},{"t":"The rules in order","d":"Legal framework of occupational health and safety","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/marco-legal-hys#piramide","x":"01 The rules in order The protection of health at work in Argentina has several levels. At the top is the Constitution: Article 14 bis guarantees dignified and equitable working conditions, and Article 75, paragraph 22, gives constitutional rank to international treaties. Below come the laws of Congress, their regulatory decrees and the resolutions of the enforcement authority. Technical standards, such as those of IRAM (Argentine Institute for Standardization and Certification) or the AEA (Argentine Electrotechnical Association) regulations, are voluntary unless a mandatory rule incorporates them. The general structure of the system was covered in the Argentine regulatory system, and labor law in labor law, in Legislation. Laws and resolutions are amended frequently. What follows is a map to find your bearings; before applying a rule, consult its current text in InfoLEG or in the Boletín Oficial (Official Gazette)."},{"t":"Law 19,587 and its regulations","d":"Legal framework of occupational health and safety","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/marco-legal-hys#ley19587","x":"02 Law 19,587 and its regulations Law 19,587 on Hygiene and Safety at Work, of 1972, sets out the principles: the employer must take the measures to protect the life and integrity of workers, and the worker must comply with the rules and use the protective equipment. Its regulations detail the requirements by activity: Rule Scope Decree 351/79 General regulation: establishments, electrical installations, fire protection, contaminants, noise, lighting, occupational hygiene and safety services and occupational medicine Decree 911/96 Construction industry Decree 617/97 Agricultural activity Decree 249/07 Mining activity The Superintendency of Occupational Risks (SRT) updates the technical values by resolution. Some of those frequently used in electronics: 295/03, with the reference values for physical and chemical agents; 85/12 and 84/12, with the protocols for measuring noise and lighting; 900/15, on grounding measurement, and 299/11, on the supply of personal protective equipment."},{"t":"The Occupational Risks Law","d":"Legal framework of occupational health and safety","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/marco-legal-hys#lrt","x":"03 The Occupational Risks Law Law 24,557, of 1995, organized the system of prevention and compensation. Every employer must take out cover with an occupational risk insurer (ART), which provides medical and cash benefits in the event of an accident or an occupational disease, and which also has prevention obligations: advising the employer and monitoring compliance. The SRT supervises the system. It covers work accidents, those that occur on the way between home and work ( in itinere ) and the occupational diseases on the official list. Law 26,773, of 2012, reorganized compensation, and Law 27,348, of 2017, established the prior step through the medical commissions."},{"t":"The provincial level","d":"Legal framework of occupational health and safety","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/marco-legal-hys#provincial","x":"04 The provincial level The provinces retain the police power over labor: their labor authorities inspect establishments, draw up citations and impose penalties for breaches of the hygiene and safety rules. Coordination between the Nation and the provinces was organized in the Federal Labor Pact, approved by Law 25,212. Some activities also have local rules, such as those on the licensing of establishments or on fire prevention in each municipality."},{"t":"The international level","d":"Legal framework of occupational health and safety","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/marco-legal-hys#internacional","x":"05 The international level The International Labour Organization (ILO) adopted Convention 155 on occupational safety and health, of 1981, and Convention 187 on the promotional framework, of 2006. Argentina ratified them through Law 26,693, of 2011. In 2022, the ILO incorporated a safe and healthy working environment into its fundamental principles and rights. On the voluntary side, the ISO 45001 standard, of 2018, defines an occupational health and safety management system that companies can have certified. It does not replace the law: it presupposes it. Cases · which rules apply? Situations from the professional life of an electronic engineer. When you choose one, the rules that come into play appear, and at which level. This is general guidance: the current text of each rule decides."},{"t":"In the laboratory","d":"Legal framework of occupational health and safety","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/marco-legal-hys#laboratorio","x":"06 In the laboratory Activity 1 · Searching InfoLEG Find the updated text of Decree 351/79 and locate the chapter on electricity. List the requirements that apply to an electronics laboratory. Activity 2 · Protocol Complete the lighting measurement protocol of SRT Resolution 84/12 for a classroom-workshop, with your own measurements. Activity 3 · Reporting Describe the path of the report of a work accident: who files it, with whom and what benefits apply."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Legal framework of occupational health and safety","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/marco-legal-hys#errores","x":"07 Common mistakes Citing a resolution without checking whether it has been amended. SRT resolutions are updated frequently. Believing that the ART replaces the employer’s prevention. The duty to prevent remains with the employer. Taking an IRAM or ISO standard as mandatory in itself. It is so only if a legal rule incorporates it or a contract requires it. Forgetting the in itinere accident. It is also covered."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Legal framework of occupational health and safety","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/marco-legal-hys#autoevaluacion","x":"08 Self-assessment Which decree regulates Law 19,587 for industry in general? Decree 351/79. Who provides the benefits in the event of a work accident? The employer’s occupational risk insurer. Which ILO conventions on safety and health did Argentina ratify in 2011? Nos. 155 and 187, through Law 26,693. Who inspects compliance with the rules in an establishment? The provincial labor authority, and the ART and the SRT within the occupational risks system."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Legal framework of occupational health and safety","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/marco-legal-hys#ampliar","x":"09 Further reading Law 19,587 and Decree 351/79. Higiene y seguridad en el trabajo (Hygiene and safety at work) (in Spanish). Updated texts in InfoLEG. The general regulation with its technical annexes. Law 24,557. Riesgos del trabajo (Occupational risks) (in Spanish). Updated text in InfoLEG. The system of insurers, benefits and prevention. International Labour Organization. Conventions 155 and 187 on occupational safety and health. NORMLEX, ILO. The international principles that Argentina has ratified."}]);
